[ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Selten F, Neylon C, Huang CK, Groth P. A Longitudinal Analysis of University Rankings. Quant Sci Stud (2020) 1(3):1109–35. 10.1162/qss_a_00052 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. de Leeuw E. European Schools of Public Health in State of Flux. The Lancet (1995) 345:1158–60. 10.1016/s0140-6736(95)90983-4 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Wilbers S, Brankovic J. The Emergence of University Rankings: A Historical-Sociological Account. High Educ (2021) 86:733–50. 10.1007/s10734-021-00776-7 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials DataSheet1.pdf (453.5KB, pdf)',12,'2025-10-20 07:12:09','2025-10-20 07:12:09');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7359,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7346348','Community-based rehabilitation service in Chengdu, Southwest China: a cross-sectional general survey.','Chen S, Lei Y, Dai H, Wu J, Yang Z, Liao X.','2020','township thcs thcs thcs thcs thcs dialects ashamed purposely exaggerating 390km dwell priamry bureaus rechecked epidata thcs thcs thcs thcs thcs thcs kf01 kf05 kf06 ry02 ry08 kf09 kf02 kf07 kf08 kf12 kf13 kf14 quantitively covert quantitively kf05 kf03 ry01 ry02 ry08 kf09 kf11 kf02 ks0301 kf12 kf14 thcs thcs thcs thcs inspires thcs therefor shorts thcs 12913_2020_5480_moesm1_esm thcs 2017yfc0907303 cmhc cmhc cmhc cmhc leiyi111 scu xiaoyang liaoxiaoyang sohu lightfoot diability yonghe honglu glimpse hampton guoqing content_5073060 lsw cdpf sjzx cjrgk t20120626_387581 maeseneer commitee zhengce content_5124174 tb00327 mineyama farh dobbins relativity tb00693 shek dtl cdstats detail_1524 cdstats detail_144653 nhc xxgk viewdocument dispatchdate staticurl jws s2910 4e1cf3ddc2fc4c68aac2a9f3ffefca77 wenhao utitle topictype publishedorg indexnum manuscriptid 4e1cf3ddc2fc4c68aac2a9f3ffefca77 grandisson thibeault kns55 navi n2015110062 ldv043 12913_2020_5480_moesm1_esm','Abstract Background World Health Organization initiated community-based rehabilitation (CBR) in 1978, and by now, it has been an essential process of medical services worldwide. China had strengthened primary health care on building more than 35,000 community health centers (CHCs) in cities, and more than 34,000 township health centers (THCs) in the rural area. Nevertheless, it remains unclear that if these primary health centers could provide optional rehabilitation services for disabilities. And this study aims at evaluating the supply capacity of rehabilitation service in primary health centers of Chengdu, a regional center city of southwest China. Method We conducted a general investigation of primary health centers in Chengdu, a city located in southwest China with more than 15 million population. Our investigation covered all of Chengdu’s 390 primary health centers from October to November 2016. We researched these primary health centers on basic rehabilitation services, diseases, and rehabilitation equipment quantity and quality, and traditional Chinese medicine (TCM) physiotherapy. Result Rehabilitation therapy is available in 88.9% (337 of 379) of all primary health centers. Meanwhile, CHCs slightly surpass THCs with an available rate of 92.2% (106 of 115) and 87.5% (231 of 264), respectively. Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) physiotherapy is available in 97.1% (368 of 379) of all primary health centers, 97.3% (112 of 115) of CHCs and 97.0% (256 of 264) of THCs. Quantitative analysis showed that substantial factors which could make an impact on the number of patients per year contain: categories of rehabilitation disease ( P < 0.001, 95% confidence interval (CI) [− 1.571, − 0.702]),number of rehabilitation bed (P < 0.001, 95%CI [− 1.249, − 0.290]). Conclusion CBR and TCM physiotherapy has become accessible for disabilities in most basic health centers of Chengdu City, whereas, available rate of CBR in THCs is lesser than in CHCs, which suggests an imbalance in primary health service development between rural and urban area. Categories of rehabilitation diseases, and the number of rehabilitation beds constitute co-factors that make an impact on the CBR capacity of basic health centers. Keywords: Community-based rehabilitation, General survey, Primary health center Background Community-based rehabilitation (CBR) has been a widely-accepted pattern for individuals to get access to convenient, flexible and economic rehabilitation services since its implementation in the 1970s [ 1 ]. A strategy called community-based rehabilitation was launched by the World Health Organization in 1978 to meet the need of rehabilitation services around the world [ 2 ]. Since being initiated, CBR has been introduced into more than 90 countries as a rational pattern to deliver rehabilitation services. Statistics show that more than 1 billion people are with disabilities globally. And about 110–190 million adults and 93 million children are experiencing significant difficulties in daily function [ 3 ]. The World Health Organization (WHO) set CBR as a critical development direction for disabilities, according to the Global Disability Action Plan [ 3 ] . After being introduced into China in the 1980s, rehabilitation services were mainly provided by volunteers in the early stage. However, due to the lack of systematic training for volunteers, CBR service remains in a low quality for many years. With more investment and support from the government, rehabilitation services based on communities began to develop in a rapid speed to alleviate the lack of rehabilitation services for disabilities [ 4 ]. Nevertheless, inadequate financial support and lack of rehabilitation physicians have hindered development of CBR services [ 5 ]. There are 34,522 CHCs and 36,871 THCs in mainland China by Sep, 2015 [ 6 ]. Even though the Chinese government has devoted significant resources in building primary health centers in recent years, rehabilitation services and personnel are still largely in need. In the next 10 years, it is estimated that China required 60,000 rehabilitation physicians, 150,000 rehabilitation therapists and 60,000 nurses to meet the demand [ 7 ]. By the end of 2010, China had more than 85 million disabled persons, according to the census [ 8 ]. But until 2018, there were only 9036 professional rehabilitation institutions and about 250,000 staff in these institutions, including physicians, nurses and administrative [ 9 ], which indicated that most of the disabled persons in mainland China could not get easy access to rehabilitation service. On the patients’ side, they prefer to go to hospitals rather than primary health care centers for what they believe in having more professional service [ 10 ]. Consequently, the Chinese government initiated the “Healthy China 2030” plan, in which service capacity and scale of primary health care system was set as a focused point to invest [ 11 ]. Nevertheless, there are rare data about how widely the primary health centers could cover disabilities who require convenient and economic rehabilitation services. Because there are many challenges during the process of CBR evaluation would hamper investigators to get reliable data. For instance, community health centers in the vast rural area of China are difficult to be evaluated because of the low literacy and the large variety of dialects. In addition, participants tend to select the midpoint and skip some items because Chinese culture affects their behavior and drives them prefer to be modest and to underestimate their performance [ 12 – 14 ]. They are ashamed to openly discuss and rate their family or organization [ 15 ]. Hence, we designed and performed this cross-sectional survey to evaluate the supply capacity of rehabilitation services in primary health centers of Chengdu and find out how we could promote the development of community-based rehabilitation. We purposely conducted this survey among the leaders of primary health centers rather than patients, which may get objective data and minimize bias caused by Chinese patients’ inclination to rate their performance and refrain from exaggerating their achievements modestly. Methods Subjects To effectively evaluate CBR in CHCs, we selected Chengdu, the capital of Sichuan province, in southwest China with a land area of 12,390km 2 and a population of 16.33 million by 2018 [ 16 , 17 ]. This city is the economic and cultural center of western China, and about 34.49% of the population dwell in rural areas. And we conducted a general cross-sectional survey of all 390 primary health centers in Chengdu. To reduce the uncertainty caused by the large variety of education levels of patients, the survey questionnaire was undertaken by lead physicians in these community health facilities from October 2016 to November 2016. At the same time, data about these community health facilities was obtained from the Health Commission of the Sichuan Province to verify survey results. In this way, we expected to receive as accurate and objective data as we could. In summary, there are two critical directions for China to enhance CBR: the first is to improve the scale of CBR service in primary health centers; the second is to improve service quality to attract patients instead of sending them to superior hospitals. To evaluate priamry health centers from these two aspects, we selected data of the number of patients (person-time) in 2015 as a proxy of CBR service capacity. Since this number could directly reflect the size and volume of CBR service. On the other hand, the larger this number was, the more patients the primary health center attracted, which indicated the higher quality of CBR service. Development of the questionnaire The questionnaire was designed according to the policy of <Service quality evaluation guideline for primary care facilities> [ 18 ], made by the National health and family planning commission of the people’s republic of China in 2016, and modified after consideration of applicability as well as expert judgment. We focused on evaluating the basic capabilities of primary care facilities in providing services Thus we mainly collected the following variables: Total number of rehabilitation patients of each primary health facilities in 2015 Total number of disabilities in the area under the jurisdiction of each primary health facilities in 2015 .Categories of rehabilitation diseases Basic conditions: served population, area of the structure, the total number of rehabilitation physicians, physical therapy equipment, cervical and lumbar traction equipment, infrared therapy apparatus, ultrasound therapy apparatus, number of rehabilitation bed, number of therapeutic rehabilitation room. Rehabilitation training: rehabilitation lecture, rehabilitation counseling. Rehabilitation management measures: rehabilitation training plan, rehabilitation-related system, self-inspection. The questionnaire is available in additional file 1 . Administration of the questionnaire An instruction for data quality control was developed for the questionnaire and then distributed by local health bureaus to the leaders of each primary health center. Three hundred ninety lead physicians completed the questionnaires independently and submitted them to the survey team in 2 weeks after receiving them. Then the survey team staff rechecked the collected questionnaires and filled the missing data by interviewing leaders of primary health centers through telephone. At last, the team randomly sent staff to 5% of the CHC facilities to validate data received. Data analysis Up to now, qualitative methodologies have dominated the field of evaluations in community-based rehabilitation, but quantitative methods have demonstrated strong potential in capture a better assessment [ 19 ]. Therefore, quantitative data analysis was adopted in our cross-sectional survey. Data were logged into Epidata by dual investigators and analyzed with SPSS 22.0 (SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, USA). Multivariable ordinal logistic regression analysis was used to determine which specific characteristics were independently related to the number of patients per year in CHCs and THCs—among the number of diseases, rehabilitation diseases, area of the structure, rehabilitation equipment, rehabilitation management measures, etc. All significant tests were 2-tailed, and those with a P -value < 0.05 were considered statistically significant. In addition, we also conducted descriptive analysis on the basic condition of CBR service in primary health centers. Results Of all the 390 CHCs this survey covered, 379 primary health centers responded to this survey, which came out with a response rate of 97.2%. After data validation, all 379 feedbacks entered into the analysis. The first part is the basic status of community rehabilitation and TCM physiotherapy (Table 1 .). Rehabilitation service is available in 88.9% (337 of 379) of all primary health centers, meanwhile, CHCs slightly surpass THCs with an available rate of 92.2% (106 of 115) and 87.5% (231 of 264), respectively. Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) physiotherapy is available in 97.1% (368 of 379) of all primary health centers, 97.3% (112 of 115) of CHCs and 97.0% (256 of 264) of THCs. And the top 5 diseases of community rehabilitation in descending order are the cervical vertebral disease, lumbar vertebral disease, osteoarthritis, adhesive capsulitis, and stroke sequelae. Table 1. The status of community rehabilitation and TCM physiotherapy urban community( N = 115) rural community ( N = 264) yes no yes no Rehabilitation 106 9 231 33 TCM physiotherapy 112 3 256 8 both 106 9 231 33 Open in a new tab Statistics about the basic condition of CHCs read that the total number of rehabilitation patients in 2015 were more than 2 million person-time. The average number of patients of CHCs and THCs were 7456.9 and 4762.6 person-time in 2015, respectively. (Table 2 .) Table 2. The basic condition of primary health centers primary health centers Total of CHCs ( N = 115) Average of CHCs Total of THCs ( N = 264) Average of THCs Total ( N = 379) Number of patients a (person-time)(KF01) 857544 e 7456.9 1,257,323 4762.6 2,114,867 Number of disabilities b (KF05) 69,037 600.3 156,438 592.6 225,475 Number of disabilities with health record c (KF06) 47,007 408.8 109,166 413.5 156,173 Basic condition Building area d (RY02)(square meter) 376,090.85 3270.4 893,922.37 3386.1 1,270,013.22 Number of rehabilitation physicians (RY08) 215 1.9 323 1.2 538 Rehabilitation equipment Cervical and lumbar traction equipment (KF09) 107 0.9 243 0.9 350 Infra-red ray therapy apparatus 93 0.8 188 0.7 281 Ultrasound therapy equipment 60 0.5 93 0.4 153 Rehabilitation beds (KF02) 1274 11.1 2270 8.6 3544 Number of rehabilitation therapeutic room 290 2.5 495 1.9 785 Rehabilitation training Rehabilitation lecture f (times)(KF07) 847 7.4 1381 5.2 2228 Rehabilitation counseling f (times)(KF08) 21,748 189.1 9914 37.6 31,662 Rehabilitation management measures rehabilitation training plan (number of launch) g (KF12) 95 0.8 189 0.7 284 Rehabilitation related system (number of launch) (KF13) 104 0.9 205 0.8 309 Self-inspection (number of launch) (KF14) 86 0.7 179 0.7 265 Open in a new tab a .Number of patients: number of all patients received CBR service in 2015, counted in person-time b .Number of disabilities: total number of disabilities in the service area c .Number of disabilities with health record: total number of disabilities with health documents in primary health centers and visit for CBR regularly in the service area d .Building area: total building area of primary health centers e .This number indicates the total number of patients in all 115 CHCs f .Rehabilitation lecture and counseling in 2015 g .Number of launch: total number of primary health centers launching this measure To quantitively analyze potential factors that could influence the capacity of CHCs rehabilitation service, we covert the dependent variable, the number of rehabilitation patients to level variable according to quartile. (Table 3 .) Table 3. Conversion of the number of rehabilitation patients Variable Classification criteria Number of patients (person-time/year) 1:< 378; 2:> = 378 AND < 2375; 3:> = 2375 AND < 7255; 4:> = 7255 Open in a new tab Quantitively analysis indicated that substantial factors which could make an impact on the number of patients per year contain: species of rehabilitation disease ( P < 0.001, 95% confidence interval (CI) [− 1.571, − 0.702]).number of rehabilitation bed ( P < 0.001, 95%CI [− 1.249,-0.290]) (Table 4 .) Table 4. Multivariable ordinary logistic regression analysis on the number of patients of primary health centers in Chengdu β Wald P 95% Confidence Interval Lower limit Higher limit Number of disabilities (KF05) < 248 − 0.032 0.011 0.92 − 0.630 0.565 > = 248 AND < 486 0.174 0.300 0.58 −0.447 0.794 > = 486 AND < 840 0.144 0.219 0.64 −0.460 0.748 > = 840 reference Rehabilitation diseases (KF03) < 9 −1.136 26.242 0.00 −1.571 − 0.702 > = 9 reference Served population a (RY01)(10 thousand) < 1.8 −0.561 2.827 0.09 −1.216 0.093 > = 1.8 AND < 3 −0.628 4.646 0.03 −1.198 − 0.057 > = 3 AND < 5.94 −0.194 0.468 0.49 −0.750 0.362 > = 5.94 reference Building structure (RY02)(square meter) < 1600 −0.687 3.816 0.05 −1.376 0.002 > = 1600 AND < 2628.4 0.157 0.276 0.60 −0.428 0.741 > = 2628.4 AND 4296 −0.297 1.091 0.30 −0.855 0.260 > = 4296 reference Number of rehabilitation physicians (RY08) <=1 −0.438 3.761 0.05 −0.882 0.005 > 1 reference Cervical and lumbar traction equipment (KF09) Yes −1.047 1.352 0.25 −2.811 0.718 No reference Infra-red ray therapy apparatus No 0.016 0.004 0.95 −0.507 0.540 Yes reference ultrasound therapy equipment (KF11) No −0.196 0.758 0.38 −0.638 0.245 Yes reference Rehabilitation bed <=7 −0.770 9.908 0.00 −1.249 − 0.290 (KF02) > 7 reference Number of rehabilitation therapeutic room (KS0301) <=2 −0.148 0.389 0.53 −0.614 0.317 > 2 reference rehabilitation training plan (KF12) No −0.289 0.848 0.36 −0.905 0.326 Yes reference Rehabilitation self-examination (KF14) No 0.390 1.943 0.16 −0.158 0.939 Yes reference Open in a new tab a Served population: number of all inhabitants in the service area Discussion To authors’ knowledge, by Sep 2019, this survey is the ever first general survey of rehabilitation capacity of primary health care in China from a literature study, as well as the first analysis of factors that influence rehabilitation service capacity in the primary health system. In the general survey, we found more than 88% of CHCs and THCs involved in this survey have been well equipped to provide rehabilitation services. But, the imbalance between urban and rural community health centers remains prominent, even though the government has made efforts to minimize the gap. For nearly the same average number of disabilities (600.3 of CHCs, 592.6 of THCs), CHCs’ number of patients is 1.56 times as THCs’ (7456.9 vs 4762.6). Compared to THCs, CHCs obviously equipped with higher-quality equipment, more rehabilitation doctors (1.9 vs 1.2) and more rehabilitation counseling (189.1 vs 37.6). Reasons that make this kind of gap between urban and rural may be various, such as economic level, physician attraction, transportation facilities, and citizens’ health awareness. Consequently, this phenomenon of the urban-rural imbalance inspires the government and community to shift more financial support and health education to the rural area to improve the rehabilitation capacity of primary health centers there. As to factors that were analyzed by multivariable ordinary logistic regression, categories of rehabilitation diseases and the number of rehabilitation bed are considered statistically significant. However, we should not ignore the number of rehabilitation physicians as a potential factor even though it is not statistically significant ( P = 0.05, 95%CI [− 0.882,0.005]). For instance, β value of categories of rehabilitation diseases is − 1.136, which means primary health centers house more than 9 categories of rehabilitation diseases would attract a larger number of patients, comparing to those with less than 9. It indicates that increase the types of rehabilitation diseases would improve CBR service capacity, which should be considered during the planning of the primary health care system. Following the same logic, increasing the number of rehabilitation beds and physicians should also be included in the plan when possible. Since 2009, the Chinese government has been putting a large number of resources to improve its primary health care system. For example, in 2014, the expenditure on primary care reached ¥110 billion [ 20 ]. In less than 10 years, the infrastructures and facilities of the CHCs and THCs were significantly enhanced [ 21 ]. Along with the giant leap in the primary health care system under a powerful and effective push from China government on medical services, the rehabilitation capacity of these primary health centers also advanced in both hardware and software. However, in the southwest of China, there are dozens of regions troubling in short of appropriate medical service, and inevitable lack of affordable and convenient rehabilitation services, because of poverty, inadequate transportation and lack of health awareness. Chengdu, as the central city of southwest China, has a powerful and comprehensive influence on regional development in every aspect, including economy, education, culture, technology, and medicine. The outcome of our study, therefor, may be representative to entire southwest China rather than a city. Due to poor education and training systems on general practitioners, rehabilitation services could not be well delivered to disabilities, even though CHCs have been equipped with the newest equipment and technology. Hence, creating an optimal general practitioner training system would be another driver that could further improve rehabilitation service capacity in the primary health system. Limitation In this survey covering all 390 basic primary health centers of Chengdu city, we eventually received 379 intact replies that could be used in analysis. Other 11 primary centers were ruled out from analysis due to incomplete data, which may lead to unknown bias in the results. Another limitation is that we selected the number of patients as a proxy of CBR service, which we think could reflect the scale and service quality of a primary health center. Patients did not know exactly how a primary health center is, they would select which is better according to their experience and impression during accepting CBR service. Hence, patients’ subjective judgement decides whether to accept CBR service in CHCs and then has an influence on our results. Conclusion Rehabilitation service capacity of primary health facilities in Chengdu took a giant leap in the past 20 years and reached a relatively high level in both quantity and quality. However, there are still many flaws and shorts during the rapid development of the primary health system and rehabilitation service. For instance, the imbalance between CHCs and THCs is one of the challenges that need to be addressed. Our study shows that the government could take measures directly correlated with rehabilitation service capacity to improve CBR service capacity, such as house more categories of rehabilitation diseases in the center, train more rehabilitation physicians and augment the number of rehabilitation beds. Additional Files 12913_2020_5480_MOESM1_ESM.docx (23.2KB, docx) Additional File 1. Self-evaluation form of basic medical service capacity of primary medical institutions in Chengdu Acknowledgments We would like to thank all the CHCs and THCs who participated in the survey. We would also like to thank the generous support of the Chengdu health and family planning commission. Abbreviations CBR Community-based rehabilitation CHC Community health center THC Town health center TCM Traditional Chinese medicine CI Confidence interval Authors’ contributions SC analyzed and interpreted survey data and was a major contributor in writing the manuscript. YL and XL design of the work. HD and ZY collected data and contributed to the analysis. JW contributed to writing the manuscript. All authors have read and approved the manuscript. Funding This study was supported by a fund of Chengdu Municipal Commission of Health and Family Planning (project no. 0040205502002) and The National Key Research and Development Program of China (grant numbers 2017YFC0907303). Chengdu Municipal Commission of Health and Family Planning authorized this survey and provided financial support for this research. Availability of data and materials The datasets used and analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Ethics approval and consent to participate This survey was authorized by the Chengdu Municipal Health Commission (CMHC). And CMHC signed a consent document to authorize us to use the information of the survey and information in the database. All primary health centers signed consent documents to be included in this general survey. CMHC initiated this general survey and accessed these information (not including patients’ privacy) under the law of <Regulation on the Administration of Medical Institutions> of People’s Republic of China. CMHC was a certified institution to check ethics issues under the law of <Measures for the Ethical Review of Biomedical Research Involving Humans> of People’s Republic of China. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Contributor Information Yi Lei, Email: leiyi111@scu.edu.cn. Xiaoyang Liao, Email: liaoxiaoyang@sohu.com. Supplementary information Supplementary information accompanies this paper at 10.1186/s12913-020-05480-3. References 1. Lightfoot E. Community-based rehabilitation: a rapidly growing method for supporting people with disabilities. Int Soc Work. 2004;47(4):455–468. doi: 10.1177/0020872804046253. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. World Health Organization . From Alma Ata to the year 2000 : reflections at the midpoint. 1988. [ Google Scholar ] 3. World Health Organization. WHO global diability action plan 2014-2021. p. 2015. 4. Ran C, Wen S, Yonghe W, Honglu M. A glimpse of community-based rehabilitation in China. Disabil Rehabil. 1992;14(2):103–107. doi: 10.3109/09638289209167080. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Hampton N. An evolving rehabilitation service delivery system in the People''s Republic of China. J Rehabil. 2001;67(3):20. [ Google Scholar ] 6. National Health Commission of the People''s Republic of China. Number of medical institutions of mainland China 2016 [Available from: http://www.gov.cn/guoqing/2016-05/13/content_5073060.htm . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 7. Li J, Li LSW. Development of rehabilitation in China. Phys Med Rehabil Clin N Am. 2019;30(4):769–773. doi: 10.1016/j.pmr.2019.07.010. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. China Disabled Persons'' Federation. Number of disabled persons in China by the end of 2010 2012 [Available from: http://www.cdpf.org.cn/sjzx/cjrgk/201206/t20120626_387581.shtml . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 9. China Disabled Persons'' Federation . Bulletin of statistics on development of disabled persons in 2018. 2019. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Li X, Lu J, Hu S, Cheng KK, De Maeseneer J, Meng Q, et al. The primary health-care system in China. Lancet (London, England) 2017;390(10112):2584–2594. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(17)33109-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. CPC Central Commitee, State Council. The plan for "Healthy China 2030" 2016 [Available from: http://www.gov.cn/zhengce/2016-10/25/content_5124174.htm . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 12. Chen C, Lee S, Stevenson HW. Response style and cross-cultural comparisons of rating scales among east Asian and north American students. Psychol Sci. 1995;6(3):170–175. doi: 10.1111/j.1467-9280.1995.tb00327.x. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Lee JW, Jones PS, Mineyama Y, Zhang XE. Cultural differences in responses to a likert scale. Res Nurs Health. 2002;25(4):295–306. doi: 10.1002/nur.10041. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Farh J, Dobbins GH, Cheng B. Cultural relativity in action: a comparison of self-ratings made by Chinese and U.S. workers. Pers Psychol. 1991;44(1):129–147. doi: 10.1111/j.1744-6570.1991.tb00693.x. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Shek DTL. The Chinese version of the self-report family inventory: does culture make a difference? Res Soc Work Pract. 1998;8(3):315–329. doi: 10.1177/104973159800800305. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Chengdu bureau of Statistics. Land area of Chengdu city 2004 [Available from: http://www.cdstats.chengdu.gov.cn/htm/detail_1524.html . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 17. Chengdu bureau of Statistics. Population of Chengdu by 2018 2019 [Available from: http://www.cdstats.chengdu.gov.cn/htm/detail_144653.html . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 18. National Health Commission of the People''s Republic of China. Service quality evaluation guideline for primary health center 2016 [Available from: http://www.nhc.gov.cn/xxgk/pages/viewdocument.jsp?dispatchDate=&staticUrl=/jws/s2910/201607/4e1cf3ddc2fc4c68aac2a9f3ffefca77.shtml&wenhao=%E5%9B%BD%E5%8D%AB%E5%9F%BA%E5%B1%82%E7%A4%BE%E5%8D%AB%E4%BE%BF%E5%87%BD%E3%80%942016%E3%80%9565%E5%8F%B7&utitle=%E5%9B%BD%E5%AE%B6%E5%8D%AB%E7%94%9F%E8%AE%A1%E7%94%9F%E5%A7%94%E5%9F%BA%E5%B1%82%E5%8D%AB%E7%94%9F%E5%8F%B8%E5%85%B3%E4%BA%8E%E5%BC%80%E5%B1%95%E7%A4%BE%E5%8C%BA%E5%8D%AB%E7%94%9F%E6%9C%8D%E5%8A%A1%E4%B8%AD%E5%BF%83%E5%8C%BB%E7%96%97%E5%8D%AB%E7%94%9F%E6%9C%8D%E5%8A%A1%E8%83%BD%E5%8A%9B%E8%B0%83%E6%9F%A5%E7%9A%84%E5%87%BD&topictype=&topic=&publishedOrg=%E5%9F%BA%E5%B1%82%E5%8D%AB%E7%94%9F%E5%8F%B8&indexNum=000013610/2016-00138&manuscriptId=4e1cf3ddc2fc4c68aac2a9f3ffefca77 . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 19. Grandisson M, Hébert M, Thibeault R. A systematic review on how to conduct evaluations in community-based rehabilitation. Disabil Rehabil. 2014;36(4):265–275. doi: 10.3109/09638288.2013.785602. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. National Health Commission of the People''s Republic of China. China Health and Family Planning Statistics Yearbook 2015 Beijing: Peking Union Medical College Press; 2015 [Available from: http://tongji.cnki.net/kns55/Navi/YearBook.aspx?id=N2015110062&floor=1 . Accessed 18 Mar 2020. 21. Wang HH, Wang JJ, Wong SY, Wong MC, Mercer SW, Griffiths SM. The development of urban community health centres for strengthening primary care in China: a systematic literature review. Br Med Bull. 2015;116:139–153. doi: 10.1093/bmb/ldv043. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 12913_2020_5480_MOESM1_ESM.docx (23.2KB, docx) Additional File 1. Self-evaluation form of basic medical service capacity of primary medical institutions in Chengdu Data Availability Statement The datasets used and analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',4,'2025-10-20 07:12:14','2025-10-20 07:12:14');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7360,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6046276','Addressing Children''s Needs in Disasters: A Regional Pediatric Tabletop Exercise.','Chung S, Gardner AH, Schonfeld DJ, Franks JL, So M, Dziuban EJ, Peacock G.','2018','countermeasure d68 convening lmi lmi multistate grantees stockpile wil coxon scripted texting congregate notices translators vetting homeland savoia behar krug faap faap faap diederich breanna monuteaux sosin facp lmi 5u38ot000167 eradicated variola 42cfr selectagents fagbuyi lozon 0b013e3182a9e613 midgley selvarangan d68 cuevas rozo microcephaly mm6549e1 mm6549e1 cybercemetery unt nccd acf ohsepr nccdreport e1407 e1417 biddinger savoia massin 00333549101250s514 griese mm6435a3 mm6435a3 hr4509 114hr4509rfs savoia biddinger dmp 0b013e 3181a539bc behar upperman dorey nager amj','Abstract Objective Preparing and responding to the needs of children during public health emergencies continues to be challenging. The purpose of this study was to assess the usefulness of a tabletop exercise in initiating pediatric preparedness strategies and assessing the impact of the exercise on participants'' understanding of and confidence in their roles during pediatric public health emergencies. Methods A tabletop exercise was developed to simulate a public health emergency scenario involving smallpox in a child, with subsequent spread to multiple states. During the exercise, participants discussed and developed communication, collaboration, and medical countermeasure strategies to enhance pediatric public health preparedness. Exercise evaluation was designed to assess participants'' knowledge gained and level of confidence surrounding pediatric public health emergencies. Results In total, 22 participants identified over 100 communication and collaboration strategies to promote pediatric public health preparedness during the exercise and found that the most beneficial aspect during the exercise was the partnership between pediatricians and public health officials. Participants'' knowledge and level of confidence surrounding a pediatric public health emergency increased after the exercise. Conclusion The tabletop exercise was effective in identifying strategies to improve pediatric public health preparedness as well as enhancing participants'' knowledge and confidence surrounding a potential pediatric public health emergency. Keywords: children, public health, emergency preparedness, pediatric disaster response, disaster exercise US children have been disproportionately impacted by emerging infectious disease outbreaks such as H1N1 pandemic influenza and enterovirus D68, as well as by the long-term effects of congenital Zika virus infection. 1 - 3 The public health community has needed to rapidly communicate with health care providers to develop appropriate pediatric screening and treatment protocols to ensure proper management. Pediatric expertise is essential in developing such protocols and communicating with families. The ongoing lessons learned from each outbreak continue to reinforce the importance and urgency of connecting pediatric and public health leaders and improving pediatric public health emergency preparedness and response. Preparing for the unique needs of children during disasters and public health emergencies continues to pose challenges. Traditional approaches for “all-hazard and all-populations” preparedness may fail to fully address the needs of more vulnerable populations at increased risk for poor outcomes. 4 Although it is widely accepted that children have unique care requirements due to their anatomic, physiologic, and developmental/behavioral characteristics, planning at federal, state, and local levels is insufficient to fully protect children throughout the disaster life cycle. 5 Tabletop exercises have been widely adopted as proven and effective tools in promoting awareness and planning for disasters. 6 However, representation of the pediatric population in national and regional planning and exercises has been limited. 7 This article describes the process of developing and convening a pilot regional tabletop exercise for an infectious disease outbreak scenario disproportionately affecting children and reports collaborative strategies between pediatricians and public health providers that enhance pediatric planning for and response to public health emergencies. Methods Development of Tabletop Exercise The initial proposal for this exercise came during the 2014 Leadership Management Institute (LMI) training program on pediatric preparedness at the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) in which LMI team members designed a hypothetical project to address lack of coordination around the needs of children during disaster response. Subsequently, a planning team from the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) and CDC convened to create a scenario for a pediatric-centered infectious disease outbreak in a multistate region. The planning team included pediatric primary care, infectious disease, emergency medicine, critical care, developmental-behavioral pediatrics, and public health perspectives. The logistics and implementation of the exercise were refined over a 6-month period. The AAP Institutional Review Board (IRB) determined that these efforts were not subject to IRB review. Participants State teams from Federal Region VI (Arkansas, Louisiana, New Mexico, Oklahoma, Texas) were invited to participate. Each state team included 2 pediatricians chosen by the AAP state chapter and 2 state or local public health officials chosen by CDC-funded Public Health Emergency Preparedness grantees. Representatives from the CDC, Office of the Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response, and the Federal Emergency Management Agency observed the exercise. Participants were informed ahead of time that the exercise would involve a case of smallpox and were given background information on the virus. a Participants received an orientation call to review participant expectations. Tabletop Exercise The tabletop exercise was held at CDC offices in Atlanta, GA. The exercise was designed as an 8-hour event with opportunity for participant interactions and short presentations by CDC experts on smallpox and the federal strategic national stockpile. AAP facilitators introduced the scenario and led the exercise. State teams worked through pieces of the progressing scenario: a smallpox outbreak spread through accidental exposure to stored samples by high school students at a laboratory, which leads to possible exposures in multiple states. Facilitators encouraged the sharing of team experiences across states to discuss best practices. During the scenario, a call was made to AAP headquarters leadership, allowing exercise facilitators to summarize the situation and AAP leadership to describe how the organization would respond if confronted with this evolving emergency. At the conclusion, participants were led through a debriefing session to elicit feedback about the exercise and to discuss processes to further enhance pediatric preparedness. The meeting proceedings for the exercise are available ( www.aap.org/disasters/tabletop ). Evaluation of the Tabletop Exercise The primary objectives for the exercise were 3-fold: first, to have state teams identify at least 10 collaborative strategies that AAP chapters, pediatric clinicians, and public health leaders could implement to advance pediatric preparedness at state and local levels; second, for states to determine at least 5 steps that teams could take to improve communications, specific to children''s issues, between public health and pediatric leaders; and third, for participants to discuss and evaluate strategies to optimize plans for the distribution and dispensing of medical countermeasures for children. Secondary objectives included assessment of the impact on state team exercise participants. Participants were given an electronic survey 24 hours after the exercise. Participants were asked at that time to rate their knowledge and confidence before and after the exercise, on a Likert scale from 1 (totally unaware/unprepared) to 10 (completely aware/ prepared), regarding: preparedness for a pediatric infectious outbreak, personal role during a public health emergency, communication strategies, partnerships, and resources. Wil-coxon''s rank score tests were performed to compare pre- and post-exercise results. Statistical significance was determined a priori using α = 0.05. All statistical analysis was performed using STATA statistical software, version 13.1 (StataCorp College Station, TX, USA). Participating state teams were asked to report on improvements in pediatric planning in their respective states during the 6 months post-exercise. Results In total, 22 people participated in the exercise: 12 pediatricians and 10 public health officials from the 5 states. Participants identified 113 collaborative strategies and 25 communication strategies to promote pediatric preparedness. General recommendations for state public health agencies included reviewing emergency preparedness and response plans and initiatives to see if children are addressed, and connecting with or forming a state pediatric preparedness council. Communication strategies included developing pre-scripted communication materials and monitoring social media to address concerns. See Table 1 for examples of collaborative and communication strategies. State teams also identified vaccination strategies (including rationing), if a limited supply was available. Table 1. Examples of Communication and Collaboration Strategies Identified During the Exercise. Communication strategies Reinforce parents as an integral part of the care team, especially when planning for or messaging about parental presence when children are potentially exposed to an infectious disease Use existing telephone banks or texting methods within school systems to disseminate accurate and factual information Leverage AAP, CDC, and other professional associations as sources of high-level and credible information to respond appropriately Offer education on children''s needs and preparedness strategies to personnel who work in places where children congregate (child care programs, school, camps) Forward Health Alert Network (HAN) notices to physicians and hospitals Plan ahead to translate messages and materials into various languages, including American Sign Language (ASL); identify interpreters and translators to address specialized issues related to cultural competency, language barriers, and tribal medicine Implement a daily (brief) update for health care professionals Designate a web page to focus on preparedness issues specific to children Adapt the communication practices schools use to notify parents of key issues in an emergency for broader use (eg, reverse 911 system) Exercise planning Partnership planning Explore how to implement pediatric tabletop exercises for response to known threats in the geographical area Identify specific processes, outcomes and actions that the public health and clinical communities should jointly take to improve pediatric preparedness Expand the reach of the HANs to include medical trainees Engage hospital Public Information Officers in the development and dissemination of messaging to health care providers Form a state pediatric preparedness task force Policy and guidelines Develop clear guidance to improve state plans for distribution and dispensing of medical countermeasures to children Open in a new tab Abbreviation: AAP, American Academy of Pediatrics; CDC, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Survey Results Out of 22 (86%) participants, 19 completed the survey (10 pediatricians and 9 public health officials). All respondents strongly agreed or agreed that the exercise generated productive discussion. All but 1 respondent strongly agreed or agreed that the exercise helped identify individual or agency strengths and weaknesses, as well as gaps in planning in their state and/or community. All but 1 respondent stated the exercise helped build relationships with participants outside of their agency and their area of expertise. Participants also described improvement in disaster readiness knowledge, their understanding of personal roles during a public health emergency, confidence, and awareness around a public health emergency ( Table 2 ). A theme that consistently emerged when participants were asked to name the most beneficial exercise component was the potential collaboration and partnership between pediatricians and public health officials. All participants recommended that this exercise be replicated with other states and regions. Table 2. Participant (n =19) Rating Pre- and Post-Exercise (Scale 1 [Least Confidence] to 10 [Most Confidence]). Pre-Exercise Median (Interquartile Range Q1,Q3) Post-Exercise Median (Interquartile Range Q1,Q3) P value My understanding/knowledge of how to prepare for an infectious outbreak that affects children 7 (5, 9) 9 (7, 9) P < 0.002 My understanding of my role in an emergency event 9 (5, 10) 9 (7, 10) P <0.016 My understanding of the roles of others 6 (3, 9) 8 (7, 9) P <0.001 My understanding of the steps I might take to partner with others in an emergency 7 (5, 10) 9 (8, 10) P <0.002 My confidence in my ability to respond to a public health emergency 8 (3, 10) 9 (7, 10) P <0.002 My confidence in my ability to locate relevant AAP resources/contacts in an emergency 5 (3, 8) 8 (7, 9) P <0.002 My confidence in my ability to locate relevant public health resources/contacts in an emergency 8 (5, 10) 10 (8, 10) P <0.002 My awareness of opportunities to collaborate in pediatric-public health partnerships in my state 4 (3, 6) 8 (7, 9) P <0.0001 My awareness of ways to communicate between public health/pediatric professionals during a public health emergency to address pediatric needs in my state 5 (3, 7) 8 (7, 9) P < 0.0002 My knowledge of distribution and dispensing of pediatric medical countermeasures strategies in my state 5 (2, 10) 9 (6, 10) P < 0.0008 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: AAP, American Academy of Pediatrics. As a result of the exercise, participants were asked to indicate the extent to which they were likely to follow up and take actions in the future. Eighty-four percent of respondents indicated they were very likely to initiate new partnerships. When asked how likely they were to make changes to disaster preparedness and response planning, 100% reported they were likely to do so. 6-Month Follow-Up Participants were contacted through e-mail for 6 months following the exercise to assess for further pediatric preparedness planning. Participants from 4 of the 5 states replied to the 6-month e-mail. Pediatricians and public health officials who replied stated that they continued to work together on pediatric preparedness topics after the exercise. One state received additional funding to implement a state pediatric preparedness and response plan that identified a pediatric contact in each of the regions within the state and brought these individuals to an exercise and workshop similar to the tabletop exercise described. Another state AAP chapter partnered with its state public health colleagues to collaborate in a hospital pediatric emergency readiness project. A third state AAP chapter and public health department developed a task force and plan to protect immunization supplies in clinic sites that may be at risk. Notably, states were only asked to report on changes that have been implemented as a direct result of having participated in the tabletop exercise, suggesting that these pediatric-public health partnerships and preparedness activities would not have occurred otherwise. Discussion This article describes the development and pilot testing of a tabletop exercise designed to improve pediatric public health emergency preparedness and response by building partnerships between public health officials and pediatricians at the state and local level. This tabletop exercise is the first of its kind, as it was based on AAP and CDC collaboration and aimed to bring state public health officials and pediatricians in a region together as state teams for collaboration. A majority of participants agreed that the tabletop exercise was valuable towards building pediatric collaborations, with evidence of sustained partnerships in the 6 months after the exercise. Ongoing collaboration between pediatricians and public health officials is essential for long-term partnerships to be maintained. 5 At the state and local level, pediatricians can assist public health departments with vetting and development of materials addressing the unique characteristics of infectious disease outbreaks that impact children, as well as collaborate on communication strategies to reach families. Those with pediatric expertise can also participate in health care coalitions to promote child- and family-appropriate response and recovery plans. Recent legislation now requires a representative to address children''s needs at the state and local level for all major US Homeland Security emergency grants recipients. 8 Tabletop exercises have been used to discover gaps and advance planning, including as they relate to health care. Savoia et al showed that participants from a variety of disciplines including public health and health care who engaged in tabletop exercises were more confident about the availability and sufficiency of legal authorities for infectious disease emergencies. 9 Behar et al demonstrated that a tabletop exercise increased hospital physicians'' and nurses'' sense of knowledge and comfort with pediatric disaster medical topics. 10 This tabletop exercise differs from those in the literature in that pediatricians and public health officials in each state actively worked together during the tabletop exercise to identify gaps and strategies in operational planning for a pediatric-specific event. There was also a transmission of knowledge, as states that developed certain pediatric strategies shared those with other states. There are limitations to this pilot tabletop exercise. Given the small number of participants, it is possible that the participants do not represent the perspectives of all pediatricians and public health officials, and these results may not be generalizable to other regions or states. Participant pre- and post-ratings of knowledge and confidence were assessed at the same timepoint post-exercise. The 6-month follow-up elicited self-reported impacts of the exercise. Positive impacts may have been inflated by social desirability bias and were not validated by other means. Preparedness plans are presumed to translate into increased effectiveness in meeting children''s needs in the event of a disaster or crisis, but this hypothesis has yet to be tested. Continuity can be challenged by turnover of individuals, particularly in government; however, the institutionalization of collaborative face-to-face relationships between public health and pediatric health care holds promise for improved sustainability. There was a cost to assemble all participants in one area to participate, limiting options for replication. However, AAP and CDC are adapting this tabletop into a virtual exercise, allowing cost-effective widespread participation and applicability to all states. Further evaluation is needed to identify what aspects of the tabletop exercise produce positive strategies for pediatric preparedness and determine whether preparedness plans and the use of such exercises actually improve pediatric response during an incident. Conclusions The AAP/CDC pediatric and public health tabletop exercise was effective in determining strategies to enhance pediatric public health preparedness, including synergy from collaboration between pediatricians and public health officials at the state level. Participants'' knowledge and level of confidence regarding a pediatric public health emergency increased following the exercise. A 6-month follow-up evaluation indicated continuing collaboration between pediatricians and public health officials. Acknowledgments The authors thank Steven E. Krug, MD, FAAP; Scott Needle, MD, FAAP; Margaret Fisher, MD, FAAP; Laura Aird, MS; Sean Diederich; Kate Klein, MA, MPH; and Breanna Smith for their participation and thoughtful review of the manuscript; Michael Monuteaux, ScD, for his assistance with the analysis; and Daniel Sosin MD, MPH, FACP, and the CDC Leadership Management Institute (LMI) planning team for their contributions to the exercise planning process. Funding : This publication was supported by Cooperative Agreement Number 5U38OT000167-04, funded by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Its contents are solely the responsibility of the authors and do not necessarily represent the official views of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention or the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. Footnotes a While eradicated in 1980, the causative agent of smallpox (variola major virus) is a select agent with potential for uses in bioterrorism, and select agent regulations (42CFR part 73) must be followed once the virus has been identified. See https://www.selectagents.gov/regulations.html Conflicts of Interest : None. References 1. Chung S, Fagbuyi D, Lozon MM, et al. Going viral: adapting to pediatric surge during the H1N1 pandemic. Pediatr Emerg Care. 2013;29(11):1159–1165. doi: 10.1097/PEC.0b013e3182a9e613. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Midgley CM, Jackson MA, Selvarangan R, et al. Severe respiratory illness associated with enterovirus D68 – Missouri and Illinois, 2014. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2014;63(36):798–799. http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25211545 . [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cuevas EL, Tong VT, Rozo N, et al. Preliminary report of microcephaly potentially associated with Zika virus infection during pregnancy— Colombia, January–November 2016. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2016;65(49):1409–1413. doi: 10.15585/mmwr.mm6549e1. http://dx.doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm6549e1 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. National Commission of Children and Disasters. 2010 Report to the President and Congress. [Accessed January 6, 2017]; https://cybercemetery.unt.edu/archive/nccd/20110426214356/http://www.acf.hhs.gov/ohsepr/nccdreport/index.html Published 2010. 5. Disaster Preparedness Advisory Council, Committee on Pediatric Emergency Medicine. Ensuring the health of children in disasters. Pediatrics. 2015;136(5):e1407–e1417. doi: 10.1542/peds.2015-3112. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Biddinger PD, Savoia E, Massin-Short SB, et al. Public health emergency preparedness exercises: lessons learned. Public Health Rep. 2010;125:100–106. doi: 10.1177/00333549101250S514. http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21133066 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hinton CF, Griese SE, Anderson MR, et al. CDC grand rounds: addressing preparedness challenges for children in public health emergencies. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2015;64(35):972–974. doi: 10.15585/mmwr.mm6435a3. http://dx.doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm6435a3 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. US Congress. HR 4509. Washington, DC: US Congress; 2014. [Accessed January 6, 2017]. 14th Congress 2D session in the senate of the United States. https://www.congress.gov/114/bills/hr4509/BILLS-114hr4509rfs.pdf . [ Google Scholar ] 9. Savoia E, Biddinger PD, Fox P, et al. Impact of tabletop exercises on participants'' knowledge of and confidence in legal authorities for infectious disease emergencies. Disaster Med Public Health Prep. 2009;3(2):104–110. doi: 10.1097/DMP.0b013e. 3181a539bc. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Behar S, Upperman JS, Ramirez M, Dorey F, Nager A. Training medical staff for pediatric disaster victims: a comparison of different teaching methods. AmJ Disaster Med. 2008;3(4):189–199. http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18822838 . [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-20 07:12:20','2025-10-20 07:12:20');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7361,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3844507','Increasing the dose of acute rehabilitation: is there a benefit?','Parker AM, Lord RK, Needham DM.','2013','saturday peiris peiris saturday saturdays saturday peiris saturdays peiris saturday imparted ots saturday saturday peiris eagerly await peiris rkl dmn rkl dmn aparke36 jhmi jhmi t32hl007534 kayambu boots paratz 0b013e31827ca637 peiris mayhew korupolu mantheiy 0b013e3182711de2 peiris brusco saturday keogh f621 f621 bunston colangelo nargi dorevitch s0004 brusco pri tehranchi cc12884 peiris s1836 peiris saturday','Abstract Rehabilitation interventions, including physiotherapy and occupational therapy, can improve patient outcomes; however, the optimal duration and frequency of inpatient rehabilitation interventions is uncertain. In a recent randomized controlled trial published in BMC Medicine , 996 patients in two publicly-funded Australian metropolitan rehabilitation facilities were assigned to physiotherapy and occupational therapy delivered Monday through Friday (five days/week control group) versus Monday through Saturday (six days/week intervention group). This increased dose of rehabilitation in the intervention group resulted in greater functional independence and quality of life at discharge, with a trend towards significant improvement at six-month follow-up. Moreover, the length of stay for the intervention group was shorter by two days (95% CI 0 to 4, P = 0.10). Hence, in the acute inpatient rehabilitation setting, a larger dose of physiotherapy and occupational therapy, via six versus five days/week treatment, improves patient outcomes and potentially reduces overall length of stay and costs. Please see related research: http://www.biomedcentral.com/1741-7015/11/198 . Keywords: Occupational therapy, Physical therapy modalities, Rehabilitation, Quality of life, Activities of daily living, Mobility limitation, Length of stay Background Across a wide variety of settings, physiotherapy (PT) and occupational therapy (OT) can improve patient outcomes, including physical function and quality of life, and reduce length of stay (LOS) and healthcare costs [ 1 - 3 ]. However, the optimal dose of PT and OT has not been established [ 2 ], making Peiris and colleagues’ [ 4 ] recently reported randomized controlled trial (RCT) comparing six versus five days/week inpatient rehabilitation of great importance. A prior meta-analysis, including 16 RCTs, demonstrated improved patient outcomes and reduced LOS with extra PT (weighted mean of 19 minutes/day) in acute care and rehabilitation hospitals [ 2 ]. While most of the individual RCTs did not show a significant benefit of extra PT, the meta-analysis demonstrated that additional PT resulted in a significant decrease in average LOS of one day in acute care and four days in rehabilitation settings, along with significantly greater functional mobility and quality of life. The RCTs included in the meta-analysis delivered extra PT through a variety of approaches, including both longer duration of sessions and additional weekend or weekday sessions; thus, the relative contribution of these differences in duration and frequency of PT could not be addressed. A better understanding of this issue of rehabilitation dose is important because the delivery of rehabilitation services on weekends varies substantially throughout Australia, Europe and North America, with fewer services frequently provided [ 2 , 5 - 8 ]. Description of the RCT Peiris and colleagues [ 4 ] conducted an assessor-blinded RCT including 996 patients in two Australian metropolitan rehabilitation facilities from July 2010 to June 2011. Patients were assigned to one hour each of PT and OT either Monday through Friday (five days/week usual care group) or Monday through Saturday (six days/week intervention group). The extra rehabilitation therapy provided on Saturdays was administered by the patient’s weekday therapist or by another routine care therapist (rather than a research therapist), and in the latter case, the Saturday therapy was based on written handover instructions from the weekday therapist. Non-English speaking patients and those with reduced cognition were included in the study, thus enhancing the generalizability of the results. In addition to evaluating functional independence (measured via the Functional Independence Measure (FIM)), quality of life (via EQ-5D questionnaire) and length of stay (LOS) at discharge, functional independence and quality of life were also assessed at 6- and 12-month follow-up. While Peiris and colleagues [ 4 ] aimed to provide the intervention group with an additional two hours of rehabilitation services on Saturdays, an average of 53 additional minutes of rehabilitation were actually administered during the RCT, with missed therapy sessions resulting from patients feeling unwell, having day leave, or being admitted too late to permit randomization prior to the start of weekend rehabilitation sessions. LOS was non-significantly reduced in the intervention versus usual care group, with a mean LOS of 21 versus 23 days. At discharge, the intervention versus usual care group had greater functional independence and quality of life, with intervention patients being more likely to achieve clinically significant improvements from admission. There were no serious adverse events in either group and no significant difference between groups with respect to any adverse events. At the 6-month follow-up, improvement in patients’ functional independence and quality of life from their status upon admission to the rehabilitation facility still favored the intervention group, but the magnitude of this improvement was smaller, with no significant differences remaining by the 12-month follow-up. Interpretation and clinical implications This RCT has important implications for the delivery of patient care in facilities where rehabilitation services are currently restricted to weekdays. Given the variability in dose and delivery of existing rehabilitation interventions [ 2 , 5 - 8 ], the study results and their generalizability must be considered in the context of the usual care control group at the study sites, that is, delivering PT and OT for two hours per day, five days per week [ 9 ]. This RCT by Peiris and colleagues, when combined with a prior meta-analysis [ 2 ], demonstrates that increasing the standard dose of rehabilitation from five to six days/week broadly improves outcomes for a wide range of patients. The potential benefits of seven versus six days/week rehabilitation or increased duration of weekday sessions is an important area of future research. This study also raises interesting questions regarding how additional PT and OT improve patient outcomes. One compelling mechanism may be a change in patient motivation induced by weekend therapy. To explore this issue, a separate qualitative analysis of a subgroup of patients from this RCT [ 10 ] investigated how patients’ experience of rehabilitation differed whether they received five versus six days/week rehabilitation. While patients in both groups perceived their therapists as providing motivation to participate in rehabilitation, those in the intervention group were more likely to view Saturday as a day to work toward improved function rather than a day of rest. Hence, patients in the intervention group may have realized more of their rehabilitation potential due to a greater sense of motivation to achieve rehabilitation goals imparted by the increased presence of PTs and OTs on weekends. This increase in motivation among patients in the intervention group may have translated to an increase in habitual activity extending beyond the formal additional Saturday sessions to Sunday as well, when no PT and OT interventions were provided for either group. A second, separate analysis of another sub-group of patients revealed that patients in the intervention versus usual care group took significantly more steps and spent more time upright on Sunday as well as Saturday [ 11 ]. Thus, the additional rehabilitation may have led to improved functional independence and quality of life through motivating an increase in habitual activity and creating an expectation that patients should be working toward their rehabilitation goals every day. It is uncertain whether a different intervention that focused on this motivational component, such as an educational intervention promoting physical activity seven days per week, would similarly motivate patients and lead to the demonstrated benefit of PT and OT delivered six days/week. Conclusion In conclusion, Peiris and colleagues’ RCT demonstrates that delivering a mean of 53 additional minutes of PT and OT per week, via six versus five days/week of therapy, significantly improves functional independence and quality of life at discharge, and may decrease LOS and provide some prolonged benefit to the six-month follow-up. In addition, we eagerly await the formal economic analysis planned by Peiris and colleagues to determine if the observed patient benefits and reduced LOS are associated with an overall economic benefit, which would make weekend rehabilitation an even more compelling intervention. Abbreviations FIM: Functional Independence Measure; LOS: Length of stay; OT: Occupational therapy; PT: Physiotherapy; RCT: Randomized controlled trial. Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Authors’ contributions AMP, RKL and DMN contributed important intellectual content to the manuscript. As first author, AMP was responsible for drafting and final revisions with substantial contributions from RKL and DMN. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Contributor Information Ann M Parker, Email: aparke36@jhu.edu. Robert K Lord, Email: robert.lord@jhmi.edu. Dale M Needham, Email: dale.needham@jhmi.edu. Acknowledgements Dr. Parker receives support from the National Institutes of Health (grant # T32HL007534-31). References Kayambu G, Boots R, Paratz J. Physical therapy for the critically Ill in the ICU: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Crit Care Med. 2013;41:1543–1554. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0b013e31827ca637. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Peiris CL, Taylor NF, Shields N. Extra physical therapy reduces patient length of stay and improves functional outcomes and quality of life in people with acute or subacute conditions: a systematic review. Arch Phys Med Rehabil. 2011;92:1490–1500. doi: 10.1016/j.apmr.2011.04.005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Lord RK, Mayhew CR, Korupolu R, Mantheiy EC, Friedman MA, Palmer JB, Needham DM. ICU early physical rehabilitation programs: financial modeling of cost savings. Crit Care Med. 2013;41:717–724. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0b013e3182711de2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Peiris CL, Shields N, Brusco NK, Watts JJ, Taylor NF. Additional Saturday rehabilitation improves functional independence and quality of life and reduces length of stay: a randomized controlled trial. BMC Med. 2013. In press. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Keogh B. Should the NHS work at weekends as it does in the week? Yes. BMJ. 2013;346:f621. doi: 10.1136/bmj.f621. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Campbell L, Bunston R, Colangelo S, Kim D, Nargi J, Hill K, Brooks D. The provision of weekend physiotherapy services in tertiary-care hospitals in Canada. Physiother Can. 2010;62:347–354. doi: 10.3138/physio.62.4.347. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Smith P, Galea M, Woodward M, Said C, Dorevitch M. Physical activity by elderly patients undergoing inpatient rehabilitation is low: an observational study. Aust J Physiother. 2008;54:209–213. doi: 10.1016/s0004-9514(08)70028-x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Shaw KD, Taylor NF, Brusco NK. Physiotherapy services provided outside of business hours in Australian hospitals: a national survey. Physiother Res Int. 2013;18:115–123. doi: 10.1002/pri.1537. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Parker A, Tehranchi K, Needham D. Critical care rehabilitation trials: the importance of ‘usual care’. Crit Care. 2013;17:183. doi: 10.1186/cc12884. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Peiris CL, Taylor NF, Shields N. Patients value patient-therapist interactions more than the amount or content of therapy during inpatient rehabilitation: a qualitative study. J Physiother. 2012;58:261–268. doi: 10.1016/S1836-9553(12)70128-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Peiris CL, Taylor NF, Shields N. Additional Saturday allied health services increase habitual physical activity among patients receiving inpatient rehabilitation for lower limb orthopedic conditions: a randomized controlled trial. Arch Phys Med Rehabil. 2012;93:1365–1370. doi: 10.1016/j.apmr.2012.03.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',162,'2025-10-20 07:12:26','2025-10-20 07:12:26');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7362,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12403309','Study on regional disparities and influencing factors of public health service supply in China from the perspective of fairness.','Zhu S, Liu J, Wang Z, Chen S.','2025','dagum tobit tobit trickle earnestly deepened rationally equalization deconstruct nouna markazi unevenly readjusted unevenly unevenly scorecard laos envelopment overhaul aroused duality hurts interweaving sovereign equalize subsidies theil endowments dagum tobit innovatively dagum tobit dagum tobit tobit economist tobit logarithmically dagum dagum economist camillo dagum dagum dagum decomposed gnb phcpi macao promulgated interpolation eleventh hainan shanxi jiangxi shaanxi ningxia liaoning jilin endowments eased promulgating overtaken yangtze agglomeration macao jilin liaoning echelon tilting jiangxi shanxi liaoning jilin inland stata17 discontinuity jiangxi locational conforms depressions agglomeration communist equalizing fluctuates wildly equalization revitalization township scientificity tobit tobit tobit vif vif tobit scientificity tobit winsorized stata17 tobit tobit dagum tobit agglomeration agglomeration depressions tobit econometric redistributing trickle yangtze talents talents socialized purchases nurtured diversify exempts shengjiao jinsi zhihua jinsi liujinsi1798 whu shixiang chensx whu geogr yangtze arroyos calvera goodyear universalism marschall flessa abolhallaje mja shinjo aramaki effi township multistate straw ferrandiz gomis murcia delnoij dmj groenewegen fanelli phommachanh nuhu mpambije ngussa salaam valdmanis denicola bernet e1225 hamzah konca sapaz envelopment giscience hukou abimbola baatiema bigdeli governmentality geochem blh dutton e66 saltman benatar wenham kittelsen envelopment tobit azzopardi muscat hainmueller reweighting dagum bf01205777 township mitropoulos mitropoulos sissouras veillard phcpi rajkumar swaroop adriana lleras muney eberth crouch urbanism roehner vollmer leidl melecky podpiera boubakri cosset saffar financ hirschman dualism','Abstract Background The public health service supply level is an essential factor affecting public health, and fairness is vital in distributing public health services. The spatiotemporal differences in public health services have attracted academic attention. However, few studies have systematically analyzed regional disparities and their influencing factors from a fair perspective. Therefore, this paper aims to explore the spatiotemporal distribution of public health service supply in China and its influencing factors from the standpoint of fairness. Methods First, based on the panel data of 31 provinces in China from 2010 to 2023, this paper constructs an objective evaluation index system for the supply level of public health services. Secondly, through the empirical analysis of the Dagum Gini coefficient, the temporal and spatial differences in the supply of public health services are explored. Regional disparities are analyzed from the perspective of fairness. Finally, the Tobit model is used to examine the impact of factors such as the proportion of the elderly population, the level of economic development, and demographic factors on the fairness of public health service supply. Results The study found that China’s public health service supply has improved significantly since the new medical reform. However, there are still large differences between different regions, and the problem of unbalanced development within the region is prominent. The eastern coastal areas show a “polarization” trend, with hot spots and weak areas in public health service supply coexisting. Simultaneously, Tobit regression confirms that the deepening of aging and improving economic development significantly drive the supply of public health services. Conclusions The government should reasonably allocate public health resources according to the actual conditions of each region to achieve a “trickle-down effect.” Especially in underdeveloped areas, the government should achieve fairness in service supply through precise policies. Concurrently, a cross-regional counterpart support mechanism should be established to promote soft power support, such as talent and technology. Medical and health services should be provided to the elderly and vulnerable groups through medical care and health industry models. Finally, relying on “Internet + medical health” to break down resource barriers and build a digital service platform covering the entire urban and rural areas to achieve fair distribution of public health resources and a “diffusion effect”. Keywords: Health equity, Public health services, Supply level, Regional distribution, Influencing factors Introduction The provision of public health services is a primary responsibility of the government. At the same time, it is also a long-term and fundamental institutional arrangement of the government. To meet the public’s demand for public health services, the government has formulated and implemented numerous medical and health policies to improve social health levels. Introducing and implementing public health service policies have improved regional medical and health services, especially the ability to respond quickly to sudden outbreaks. Therefore, public health services safeguard the people’s fundamental health rights and interests and promote social equity. The government should earnestly perform its public health service functions, implement the value orientation of fairness in allocating medical resources, and demonstrate the government’s governance level and ability. This is a prerequisite for achieving equity in public health services. For example, the Outline of the Healthy China 2030 Plan clearly states that urban and rural residents should enjoy equal essential public health services, emphasizing the equitable public health supply. Thus, it is imperative to understand the reasons for differences in the provision of public health services. China has a relatively large population base, especially with the intensification of aging, which has brought considerable challenges to the government’s provision of public health services. According to data from the National Bureau of Statistics, the proportion of China’s population aged 65 and above was 8.9% in 2010, and the index reached 15.4% in 2023. The index change shows that the aging population is large in scale and growing rapidly, which has caused an all-around impact on the public health system. Since China’s new medical reform, the government has attached great importance to developing public health services. On the one hand, it has increased funding for public health services. On the other hand, it has continuously optimized resource allocation and improved the service system. From this point of view, the public health service supply level determines the government’s ability to respond to public health emergencies. In 2009, the State Council of China issued the “Opinions on Deepening the Reform of the Medical and Health System,” marking the beginning of the “Healthy China” strategy. The medical system reform has deepened further, so the reform in 2009 is called the “New Medical Reform.” In 2016, China prioritized health development as one of the country’s overall strategic deployment principles. In 2017, the 19th National Congress report identified “Healthy China” as a national strategy. In 2020, relevant public health policy documents were released one after another. Among them, the National Health Commission issued the “Guiding Opinions on Strengthening the Performance Assessment of Primary Medical and Health Institutions,” which the primary medical care assessment form has been stipulated in the form of a policy. China’s medical system reform is still in progress, and the level of public health services has been rapidly improved. A relatively complete public health service system has been established, and remarkable results have been achieved in medical services [ 1 ]. However, significant differences in economic development levels, geographical locations, and cultural factors between regions make the medical and health services levels different [ 2 ]. High-quality public health resources are concentrated in large cities, while the quality of public health services in economically underdeveloped areas needs further improvement [ 3 ]. In response to the growing demand for medical services and continuing to improve the level of essential public health services, it is also necessary to rationally allocate medical resources in space and appropriately allocate them towards underdeveloped areas. Only by controlling the medical resources between regions can the social goal of “health for all” be achieved. In recent years, the Chinese government has established “equalization of basic public services”, “balanced regional development”, “Healthy China 2030”, and “sharing the fruits of development” as core policy orientations. These strategies promote the fair distribution of health resources through institutions. In 2023, the “Opinions on Further Improving the Medical and Health Service System” was issued to resolve the imbalance in regional allocation of medical and health resources by designing a systematic correction mechanism. In the study of the public health service supply level, there is a lack of empirical research that considers fairness. For this reason, to solve the fairness dilemma in regional public health service supply, this study focuses on the regional disparities and influencing factors of China’s public health service supply. This aims to clarify the actual picture of the level of public health service supply and reveal the different characteristics of the public health service supply level index in other regions. And identify the multiple factors affecting the level of public health service supply. Therefore, this paper focuses on the following three issues. (1) Construct a comprehensive evaluation index system for the public health service supply level to systematically measure the spatiotemporal differentiation characteristics of regional service supply capacity. Focus on analyzing the dynamic changes in the supply level before and after the epidemic. (2) Deconstruct the structural differences in the public health service supply level and explore the correlation between spaces. (3) Identify the factors affecting the public health service supply level, aiming to provide suggestions and references for the government to introduce reasonable public health service policies. The research framework of this article is as follows (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Research framework Literature review Public health services are essential health services that the public sector provides to protect and promote public health. The service targets are the entire society or specific groups of people, including disease prevention and control, maternal and child health and care, public health emergencies, and rescue. (1) Regarding the value of public health service supply, public health services cover health improvement, maintenance, and health services [ 4 ]. Then, scholars began to study the value of public health services. Generally speaking, public health services are divided into two orientations: value rationality and instrumental rationality. Public and fairness are the primary considerations. In addition, the adjustment and allocation of health resources also need to consider their economic value, considering both efficiency and fairness [ 5 ]. In health equity-related research, unfair and inefficient allocation of health resources is a global problem. Although New Zealand’s health system has universal and innovative services, it still faces persistent equity issues. This requires strengthening the system culture and capacity to improve the equity of health outcomes for different population groups [ 6 ]. Primary healthcare facilities in Nouna, a rural area of Burkina Faso in Africa, are inefficient, with underutilization of facilities and distance being the main influencing factors [ 7 ]. There is inequality in the distribution of medical facilities in Markazi Province, Iran [ 8 ]. Scholars have proposed a new global health model that balances human health and ecological sustainability [ 9 ]. Japan’s medical resources are unevenly distributed and must be readjusted to achieve a more balanced geographical distribution and an effective medical system [ 10 ]. China’s health resources are unevenly distributed across regions [ 11 ]. For example, regarding population distribution, China’s maternal and child health care human resources are relatively fair, but unevenly distributed across geographical regions [ 12 ]. Some scholars have discussed the Chinese public’s satisfaction with health care and their perception of fairness in health care [ 13 ]. There are significant differences in the perception of equality or welfare between different demographic characteristics, and some people believe that the accessibility of health care resources lacks fairness [ 14 ]. (2) In the research on public health service performance evaluation, the balanced scorecard is first used to develop an evaluation index system, and the weight of the index system is determined by the Delphi method [ 15 ]. Questionnaires and interviews are used to evaluate the performance of primary health institutions [ 16 ]. Secondly, the national public health service performance standards are used to assess local public health service institutions [ 17 ]. The “Government Performance and Results Act” in the United States is conducive to improving the quality of its public health services, thereby increasing the public’s confidence in the US government [ 18 ]. Thirdly, many studies directly evaluate the performance of public hospitals [ 19 ]. For example, indicators such as the number of beds and operating rooms are used as input variables, and indicators such as discharge, emergency, and surgical intervention are used as outcome variables to measure the efficiency level of public hospitals [ 20 ]. (3) Many scholars have analyzed the study of public health service supply from different perspectives. First, the evaluation goal is to improve the performance of public health services. In addition to improving the efficiency of public health services [ 21 ], it is also necessary to give full play to the government’s macro-control capabilities and achieve fair distribution to meet the needs of more people [ 22 ]. Second, many studies focus on the level of public health service supply. In the study of systematic literature review, scholars traced the dynamics and boundaries of public-private partnerships in the healthcare sector in low- and middle-income countries [ 23 ]. There are problems with the quality of public maternal and child health care services in Laos. Scholars have proposed countermeasures from both the services’ supply and demand sides [ 24 ]. In addition, some studies use case studies and interview methods to strengthen public-private partnerships and effectively promote the supply of essential public health services [ 25 ]. Data envelopment analysis is used to evaluate the health service capacity of county-level public health departments [ 26 ]. Additionally, some studies have explored the quality of district health services after China’s new medical reform and found that the quality of community health services has significantly improved after the medical overhaul [ 27 ]. Of course, some scholars have also constructed an essential public service evaluation index system from the output perspective [ 28 ]. Different evaluation index systems cover social supervision and economic level [ 29 ]. For example, a study on the relationship between fairness and efficiency of public health services in African countries showed that the number of nurses and the income gap strongly affected the efficiency of public health services [ 30 ]. Finally, the imbalance in the allocation of public health service resources has aroused discussion among many scholars. The most obvious is the huge gap between urban and rural areas, which has led more people to seek better medical services in county-level cities [ 31 ]. The spending levels of public health institutions in the United States vary significantly between different communities, and this difference has increased over time [ 32 ]. The accessibility of health care in Australia varies in time and space, and the urban-rural imbalance differs for different types of hospitals [ 33 ]. In China’s public health research, there are apparent differences in health status between rural and urban areas and the central and western regions [ 34 ]. Moreover, health inequalities related to regional wealth differences and urban-rural gaps are widening [ 35 ]. China’s medical resources tend to concentrate in economically developed regions, and people in the underdeveloped areas seek medical treatment in provinces with more distant medical resource centers [ 36 ]. For economically developed regions, there are also significant differences in the quality of medical and health services in different cities [ 37 ]. (4) In the study of factors affecting the allocation of public health resources, the impact of China’s urban-rural gap and the new medical reform policy on the allocation of public health resources has attracted much attention. First, the institutional roots of the urban-rural duality have affected public health resources. There are widespread problems in the urban and rural medical fields, such as integrating urban and rural medical insurance and the uneven distribution of resources [ 38 ]. Citizens with urban household registration have better health opportunities, while rural household registration hurts health [ 39 ]. There are also differences between disease prevention in urban and rural areas [ 40 ]. China’s public health system is decentralized and fragmented in response to public health emergencies. This structure is unsuitable for a rapid and coordinated response to public health emergencies [ 41 ]. Second, in the new medical reform, China places more emphasis on central supervision. The bureaucratic management of the UK’s NHS is an interweaving of government and sovereign power, while China aims to equalize essential public services, medical care, and environmental protection [ 42 ]. It is necessary to strengthen central environmental supervision and establish a local environmental supervision office [ 43 ]. At the same time, on the demand side in China, overuse is positively correlated with low health literacy, long duration of illness, large family size, and high economic status. On the supply side, overuse is relatively high at the county level and above hospitals and cities with a relatively low density of medical human resources [ 44 ]. The third is the impact of digital governance strategies. The role of digital technology in medical care and its rise is helpful to patients in remote areas [ 45 ]. Scholars also discussed the opportunities, challenges, and impacts of the increasing digitalization of European public health [ 46 ]. Fourth, the role of China’s unique village medical system, although hospice care has not been genuinely implemented in China’s vast rural areas. However, local hospice care based on the village medical system plays an important role [ 47 ]. (5) In the study of factors affecting public health service supply efficiency, most of them are analyzed from three dimensions. First, government supervision can effectively improve the efficiency of public service supply. Generally speaking, if the government can take the initiative to implement public health service supply evaluation, it can effectively reduce costs and encourage less money to achieve service goals [ 48 ]. Second, capital investment is the basis of public health service supply. Studies have shown that people’s health levels can be significantly improved with increased public health service expenditure [ 49 ]. In other words, the economic development level of a region directly affects the quality of public health services in the area [ 50 ]. In addition, the introduction of market competition will positively impact the quality of public services and find a balance between the public and private sectors [ 51 ]. Third, allocating medical resources is also an essential factor affecting the efficiency of public health service supply [ 52 ]. Currently, the level of public health service supply at the grassroots level needs to be more distributed in space, and the biggest reason is the uneven distribution of medical resources. This shows that the fiscal system directly affects the supply of medical resources [ 53 ]. For example, in the case of an outbreak, most of the funds are used to prevent and control the spread of the epidemic, resulting in a significant reduction in the essential public health service expenditure of local governments [ 54 ]. Subsidies for funding, public health personnel, and material expenditures can significantly improve the effectiveness of crucial public health services [ 55 ]. In the short term, digital technologies may hurt health equity for some groups, especially older, less educated, and lower economic status [ 56 ]. In summary, the above studies mainly focus on public health service performance and supply efficiency and discuss the balance between efficiency and fairness. Some things could still be improved in the current research. First, although some literature mentions the issue of fairness in the supply of public health services, most of them are focused on theoretical discussions. From a methodological perspective, few scientific inequality measurement methods (such as the Gini coefficient and the Theil index) are used to analyze the sources of differences systematically. Second, the evaluation indicators of the public health service supply level need further improvement. Third, the spatial distribution of public health service supply levels could be more balanced due to other regions’ different economic levels and resource endowments. However, the current discussion on this issue needs to be more comprehensive. In particular, there is a lack of dynamic examination of the changing patterns before and after major public health events (such as the COVID-19 epidemic). Therefore, based on China’s panel data from 2010 to 2023, this paper uses the entropy method, Dagum Gini coefficient, and Tobit model to explore the spatiotemporal dynamics and influencing factors of public health service supply level in 31 provinces in China. This paper innovatively integrates the Dagum Gini coefficient and the Tobit model method system compared with existing studies. Through spatial decomposition and group difference analysis, the Dagum Gini coefficient achieves a structural analysis of the inequality in the public health service supply level. And then accurately identifies its influencing factors. Together, the two promote the shift of health equity research from static description to dynamic mechanism analysis. The possible contributions of this paper are as follows: (1) This study constructs a fairness index system from the dual dimensions of resource input and service output, breaking through the traditional single-dimensional evaluation framework. This can be better used to evaluate the public health service supply level. (2) In addition to examining the differences in public health service supply levels in the four major regions, it also explores the differences in the supply levels of specific groups within the region and between different provinces. (3) It focuses on the factors that influence regional public health service supply levels and provides policy recommendations for local government public health service supply. Study design Methods Entropy method The basic principle of the entropy method is to use an objective weighting method to determine the weight of indicators based on the principle of information entropy [ 57 ]. In other words, the weight is determined by the degree of variation of the evaluation indicator value. In the performance evaluation of public non-profit hospitals, the entropy weight method is used to determine the weight of indicators [ 58 ]. The social disability risk measurement indicator system also uses the entropy weight method to calculate the weight of indicators at all levels [ 59 ]. Tobit model The Tobit Model, also known as the Censored Regression Model, is a regression model that handles “censored data” or “truncated data.” It was proposed by economist James Tobin in 1958 and is mainly used to deal with situations where the dependent variable is truncated or restricted within a specific range [ 60 ]. This paper uses the panel Tobit model to estimate the impact mechanism of the level of public health service supply [ 61 ]. The main formula of the model is as follows: 1 The explanatory variables were logarithmically processed to avoid the significant differences between the variable data affecting the analysis results. Dagum Gini index The Dagum Gini coefficient is an improved version of the Gini coefficient proposed by Italian economist Camillo Dagum in 1990. It measures the degree of income or wealth distribution inequality [ 62 ]. Scholars chose the Dagum Gini coefficient to explore the regional disparities in the level of public health service supply in China [ 63 ]. According to Dagum’s decomposition theory, the overall Gini coefficient (G) can be decomposed into intra-regional difference contribution (Gw), inter-regional difference contribution (Gnb), and transfer variation contribution (Gt), that is, . Its basic formula is: 2 Among them, is the public health service level of province in region . is each region’s average public health service level. is the number of provinces. is the number of divided areas. , are the number of provinces in regions and . is the overall Gini coefficient. Research indicators Based on existing research and data availability [ 64 ], this paper constructs an indicator system mainly from three dimensions: resource input and service output (Table 1 ). The indicator’s weight is calculated using the entropy method, which evaluates the input and output of the supply level. Based on data availability and literature support, selecting indicators for public health service supply prioritizes the support of the National Bureau of Statistics, the Health Statistics Yearbook, and existing research literature. This avoids subjective bias. The density of the health workforce (such as the number of health technicians per thousand people) is a basic indicator that reflects the accessibility and fairness of health services [ 65 ]. Bed configuration, as a core resource element of the medical and health service system, is the leading standard indicator for measuring countries’ health resources and service capabilities internationally. According to the framework of the Primary Health Care Performance Initiative (PHCPI), public health expenditure is mainly used to measure the fairness and efficiency of resource allocation [ 66 ]. The reported mortality rate and bed occupancy rate of Class A and B infectious diseases are widely used in evaluating the effectiveness of government public health services [ 67 ]. The 2015 “Guiding Opinions on Promoting the Construction of a Tiered Diagnosis and Treatment System” strengthens service accessibility through quantitative indicators such as the proportion of grassroots services. The high volume of diagnosis and treatment in grassroots institutions means that more people (especially residents in rural and remote areas) can obtain basic services, which aligns with the fairness principle of “everyone has the right to health”. The maternal mortality rate reflects the quality of maternal and child health care and emergency treatment capacity. In addition, health education and training for different groups (such as low-income groups and rural residents) can quantitatively reflect the fairness of service supply. Table 1. Evaluation indicator system for public health service provision level Indicator Type Indicator Name Indicator Content Weight Attribute Resource Input Human Resource Input X1 = Number of health technicians per 10,000 people (persons) 9.77% + Financial Input X2 = Per capita public health financial investment 19.90% + Material Input X3 = Number of hospital beds per 10,000 people 9.73% + Service Output Inpatient Services X4 = Number of outpatient visits at primary healthcare institutions (in ten thousand visits) 28.25% + X5 = Bed occupancy rate in healthcare institutions 3.42% + Disease Control Services X6 = Mortality rate for Class A and B infectious diseases (national) 2.41% - Maternal and Child Health Care X7 = Maternal mortality rate 0.84% - Health Education X8 = Number of health education training sessions per 10,000 people 25.68% + Open in a new tab Data sources This paper takes 31 provinces in China (excluding Taiwan Province, Hong Kong, and Macao Special Administrative Regions) as the research object. In 2009, China promulgated the “Opinions of the CPC Central Committee and the State Council on Deepening the Reform of the Medical and Health System,” marking the beginning of the new medical reform. Because there are significant differences between the health systems before and after the new medical reform, the period of this study is 2010–2023, which can ensure that the research is carried out in a relatively stable policy environment and avoid the interference of policy changes on the research results. Data sources: “China Statistical Yearbook,” “China Health Statistical Yearbook,” “Administrative Division Statistics of the People’s Republic of China,” and the EPS data platform. A few missing data points were filled by linear interpolation. The National “Eleventh Five-Year Plan” outline proposed implementing a regional development overall strategy based on four significant blocks (Table 2 ). Table 2. Provincial division of china’s four major regions Region Provinces Eastern Beijing, Hebei, Shandong, Shanghai, Jiangsu, Zhejiang, Guangdong, Hainan, Tianjin, Fujian Central Henan, Hubei, Hunan, Shanxi, Anhui, Jiangxi Western Sichuan, Shaanxi, Inner Mongolia, Guangxi, Chongqing, Guizhou, Yunnan, Tibet, Gansu, Qinghai, Ningxia, Xinjiang Northeastern Liaoning, Jilin, Heilongjiang Open in a new tab Source: Development Research Center of the State Council of China Results and analysis Analysis of the temporal characteristics of the public health service supply level Macroscopic overall analysis The overall trend in the provision of public health services across the four major regions. This study focuses on the development trend of the public health service supply level in China’s four major regions (Fig. 2 ). The public health service supply level in the four major regions has shown a significant upward trend from the overall trend. The eastern, central, and western regions are particularly prominent, showing a relatively strong growth momentum. In contrast, although the northeastern region has improved, the growth trend is relatively slow. This reflects the specific challenges faced by its public health service development. Regarding horizontal comparison between regions, the public health service supply level shows noticeable regional disparities. Specifically, the eastern and central regions lead, followed by the northeastern region, while the western region lags. This pattern’s formation is mainly due to various regions’ different resource endowments, economic foundations, and policy environments. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Development trends of public health service supply levels in the four major regions The public health service supply level in the eastern and central regions continues to exceed the national average, and the gap between the two tends to widen gradually. This is closely related to the advantages of the eastern and central regions in resource allocation, policy implementation, and innovation capabilities. The level of public health service supply in the western region has always been lower than the national average, but the gap has been narrowing, showing a positive catch-up trend. The northeast region’s public health service supply level was comparable to that of the western region in the early stages, but it fell sharply in the later stages. This relates to multiple factors, such as economic transformation, population structure changes, and regional policy adjustments. It is worth noting that after 2019, the public health service supply level in the four major regions has fluctuated to varying degrees. The public health service supply level briefly declined in 2020, dropping the average value to 0.324. This abnormal phenomenon is very likely to be impacted by global public health events. The epidemic has not only tested the resilience of the public health systems of various countries but also prompted various regions to re-examine and strengthen the construction and management of public health services. Since the beginning of 2021, the national economy has recovered with the control of the epidemic, and the medical supply has been eased. The country’s attention to public health services has reached a new high. The state has increased its support for public health services by promulgating policies. The allocation of grassroots medical and health resources has been further optimized. The accessibility and balance of public health services in various regions continue to increase. (2) Temporal trends in regional public health service provision levels before and after COVID-19. This study takes December 2019 as the key time node and systematically examines the dynamic evolution characteristics of China’s public health service supply level (Fig. 2 ). Before the COVID-19 epidemic, China’s regional public health supply level showed a continuous fluctuation growth state, but there were significant regional disparities. Specifically, the growth rate of each region’s public health service supply level index was relatively slow, without drastic fluctuations. In the early stages, the Northeast region had an obvious advantage in the public health service supply level, and its index was higher than that of the Western region. In 2012, the index of the Northeast region led the western region by about 3.66%, which shows that the gap between the two regions was not large at that time. However, in 2013, the Northeast region was overtaken by the western region. After the COVID-19 epidemic, each region’s public health service supply level index fluctuated slightly in the early stage, which may be due to the outbreak of the COVID-19 pandemic on the public health system. The public health supply level in all regions began to recover rapidly and continued to improve. The growth rate of the regional public health supply level generally accelerated, but the differentiation intensified, and the Northeast region continued to lag. (3) Trends in public health service supply levels within the four major regions. There are also apparent differences in the supply level within the region (Fig. 3 ). First, the eastern region can be divided into the northern coastal comprehensive economic zone, the eastern coastal comprehensive economic zone, and the southern coastal comprehensive economic zone. Overall, the public health service system in the eastern region has been continuously improved, which is highly consistent with the promotion of the national “Healthy China” strategy. However, there is a clear regional gradient differentiation. The eastern coastal economic zone has always maintained an absolute leading position, reflecting the advantages of the Yangtze River Delta urban agglomeration in fiscal investment and medical resource density. The northern coastal and southern coastal areas remain at a similar level. The growth rate of the southern coastal area has accelerated after 2018, which is related to the medical coordination policy of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Level of public health service provision within regions Second, the central region can be divided into the northern central provinces and the southern central provinces. The central region has steadily improved across the board, and the northern and southern central regions have achieved rapid growth. This confirms the continuous increase in public health investment under the central rise strategy. Third, the western region can be divided into the southwestern and northwestern regions. The supply level index in the southwestern region is generally better than that in the northwestern region. Fourth, the northeastern region can be divided into Heilongjiang, Jilin, and Liaoning. There is a significant hierarchical differentiation within the region, and the supply levels of the three provinces show a different echelon structure, which reflects the uneven development within the region. Microscopic analysis of provincial regions From a provincial micro perspective (Fig. 4 ), this study uses each province’s average public health service supply level over the past 14 years. Compared with the national average 0.289, 12 provinces exceeded the national average. The remaining 19 provinces were below the national average. This shows that improving local government’s public health service supply capacity is necessary nationally, significantly tilting policies toward provinces below the average. The balanced development and fair access to public health services can be promoted only in this way. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Provincial-level average public health service supply level The level of public health services among provinces is uneven. In the eastern region, the data for Beijing, Shanghai, Jiangsu, Zhejiang, Shandong, Guangdong, and other regions are significantly higher than the average. These regions have developed economies, sufficient fiscal revenues, relatively concentrated medical resources, and developed transportation, which promotes the accessibility of medical care services. In the central region, the data for Henan, Hubei, Hunan, Jiangxi, and other regions are higher than the average. These regions rely on the strategy of the rise of the central region and have relatively concentrated medical resources. The public health service supply level in Anhui and Shanxi is slightly lower than the average level, which may be because these regions rely on traditional agriculture, have slow economic development, and have insufficient fiscal investment. Except for Sichuan Province, the public health service supply level is higher than the average in the western region. In contrast, the rest of the regions are lower than average. In the Northeast region, the public health service supply level index of Heilongjiang, Liaoning, and Jilin provinces is all below the average level. The traditional industrial transformation in the region is complex, and the population loss is severe, bringing a series of negative impacts to the region [ 68 ]. Overall, there are substantial differences in the level of public health service supply in China at the provincial level, and the overall trend is “stronger in the coastal areas than in the inland areas.” This paper uses Stata17 software for data processing. Then, it analyzes the dynamic trend of the supply level of public health services in 31 provinces (Fig. 5 ). From 2010 to 2020, the overall trend is upward. Especially in the early stages, the rise is more prominent and is probably closely related to the “new medical reform” policy launched in 2009. The new medical reform has injected vitality and impetus into the public health service system. Subsequently, from 2016 to 2023, the supply level of public health services continued to grow steadily. The reason behind this is the release and implementation of the “Healthy China 2030” Outline, which points out the direction for the future development of public health services and provides strong policy support. However, it is worth noting that from 2019 to 2020, the supply level of public health services fluctuated. The reason is the impact of the global COVID-19 pandemic. Many funds have been invested in preventing and controlling the epidemic, reducing the country’s supply of public health services to a certain extent. After 2021, with the control of the epidemic and the support of national policies, the public health service supply level in various provinces continued to increase. Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Trend of public health service supply levels across Chinese provinces From the perspective of regional distribution, the public health service supply level in the eastern developed regions (Beijing, Guangdong, Shanghai, Jiangsu, Zhejiang, etc.) is significantly ahead of the national average. This directly reflects the positive impact of regional economic development on public health service capabilities. The provinces in the western region are relatively weak in the supply of public health services. In summary, the gradual improvement in the level of public health service supply in China directly reflects the government’s high attention to public health services and continued increase in policy support. Spatial characteristics analysis of the public health service supply level Spatial disparities in the provision of public health services To intuitively show the spatial differences in the level of public health service supply in various regions, this paper uses ArcGIS to perform geographic visualization (Fig. 6 ). According to the general practice, the natural discontinuity method is adopted to divide the public health service supply level into three categories. The highest supply level is represented by blue, ranging from 0.32 to 0.51. The second highest is represented by dark green, ranging from 0.24 to 0.32. The lowest is defined by light green, ranging from 0.17 to 0.24. Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Geographic Visualization of Public Health Service Supply Levels in China. (The map is based on the Standard Map with approval number GS (2024) 0650, with no modifications to the boundary of the base map.) In general, the supply level in most areas of the east and central regions is higher than that in the west and north, and the public health service level in economically developed regions is higher than that in underdeveloped regions. In addition, provinces with similar supply levels are adjacent, which shows that public health services in different provinces are prone to form regional clusters. More specifically, the supply level in Anhui, Jiangxi, and Fujian provinces is lower than in other surrounding provinces. This shows that these provinces have not fully utilized their locational advantages, but they need further improvement at the economic and institutional levels. The public health service level of relevant provinces in the northern region is in the middle position due to their vast land and sparse population. Overall, it conforms to the “center-periphery” characteristics. Regions with higher levels of public health service supply are the “center,” while regions with lower supply levels are the “periphery,” which construct a complete binary spatial structure. Spatial correlation in the provision of public health services This study used ArcGIS to draw the spatial autocorrelation LISA map and analyzed the generated LISA cluster map (Fig. 7 ). The LISA cluster map uses four colors to distinguish different spatial autocorrelation patterns. They mainly include high-high clustering (HH), low-high clustering (LH), low-low clustering (LL), and high-low clustering (HL), which represent different spatial correlation characteristics. Fig. 7. Open in a new tab Spatial correlation of public health service provision levels in China. (The map is based on the Standard Map with approval number GS (2024) 0650, with no modifications to the boundary of the base map.) The red area shows a high-high cluster, indicating one or more “hot spots” in the region. Specifically, the regional unit has a high public health service supply. The public health service supply level of the adjacent regional units is also at a high level. There is a strong positive spatial autocorrelation in the high-high clustered areas. The high-level provinces are spatially adjacent and clustered, indicating synergies, scale advantages, or policy spillover effects in the region’s development. The light blue area represents the low-high outlier mode, characterized by the regional unit’s low public health service supply level. The supply level of the adjacent regional units is high. This spatial heterogeneity pattern indicates an imbalance in development within the region, and there are local development depressions in the high-level agglomeration area. The white area means that the spatial autocorrelation relationship is not statistically significant, and its spatial distribution characteristics are manifested in the lack of substantial spatial correlation between the regional unit and the adjacent units. Analysis of spatial differences in the public health service supply level This paper uses the Gini coefficient to analyze the overall, intra-regional, and inter-regional disparities to explore the degree of difference in the level of public health service supply (Fig. 8 ). Fig. 8. Open in a new tab Decomposition of the Gini Coefficient for public health service supply levels From the overall Gini coefficient perspective, it dropped from 0.207 in 2010 to 0.148 in 2023. The decline is about 28.5%, which shows that the regional disparities in the level of public health service supply are gradually narrowing. The “new medical reform” has played a specific policy role. Concurrently, the 2013 Decision of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China on Several Major Issues Concerning Comprehensively Deepening Reform proposed the strategic objective of ‘deepening the reform of the medical and healthcare system. It calls for a coordinated advancement of reforms in medical insurance, healthcare services, public health, pharmaceutical supply, and regulatory systems.’ This strategic plan aims to systematically address issues in the healthcare sector and promote more equitable and accessible public health services. Since 2010, China’s public health service supply has been steadily improving. National and local health investment and policy guidance (such as deepening the “new medical reform”, equalizing basic public health services, and alleviating health poverty) have produced positive results. In the decomposition of the overall difference in supply level, the difference between groups is always higher than the difference within the group, indicating that the difference between groups is the primary source of the overall gap. The average annual contribution rate between groups reached 42.76%, and the overall difference in the public health service supply level was mainly manifested in the four major regions. In addition, the average annual contribution rates of intra-group differences and super-variable density were 25.65% and 31.59%, respectively, which led to the difference in supply level to a certain extent. From the perspective of regional disparities in the public health service supply level, the Gini coefficient within the group is similar to the overall change trend. Overall, the region exhibits a relatively flat profile, indicating that the supply level differences within the area are gradually narrowing. Specifically, the Gini coefficient of the supply level in the eastern region fluctuates wildly, indicating that the differences between regional provinces are still noticeable, and resource allocation needs to be optimized. The Gini coefficient of the supply level in the central region shows overall fluctuations. The Gini coefficient of the service supply level within the western region varies greatly, with the value dropping from 0.194 in 2010 to 0.082 in 2023, a drop of nearly 58%, which indicates that the balance of supply levels within the region has improved. This is related to the strategy of Western development. The Gini coefficient of the supply level in the northeast region dropped from 0.075 in 2010 to 0.024 in 2023, reflecting the equalization of the service supply level within the northeast region. Analysis shows that the reason lies in the policy support of the “14th Five-Year Plan for the Comprehensive Revitalization of Northeast China.” From the perspective of differences between groups in the public health service supply level, the evolution trend of the Gini coefficients between the eastern, central, western, and northeastern regions is mainly fluctuating and declining. Specifically, the region with the most significant Gini coefficient is the East-West region, with an annual average value of 0.187, which will drop to 0.141 in 2023. The annual average value of the Central-West region reaches 0.158, which will drop to 0.120 in 2023, and the gap is narrowing significantly. The annual average value of the West-Northeast region reaches 0.129, which will drop to 0.089 in 2023, and the gap is also narrowing. Analysis of the influencing factors of the public health service supply level The selection of indicator factors affecting the level of public health service provision In discussing the factors affecting public health service supply, factors such as government fiscal expenditure [ 69 ], income, education [ 70 ], and urbanization [ 71 ] have attracted much attention. Based on the literature review in the previous article, this paper selects available indicator data and uses the level of public health service supply as the explanatory variable. Simultaneously, the proportion of the aging population, population density, and economic development level are included in the category of explanatory variables. The government size and urbanization level are control variables (Table 3 ). Specifically, the proportion of the elderly population is associated with medical resources [ 72 ]. The impact of population density on the epidemic has been widely studied [ 73 ]. The level of economic development affects public health [ 74 ]. In the variable selection process, this paper follows three principles: (1) the principle of theoretical completeness. Ensure that the selected variables cover the demand and supply sides of the public health service supply levels. (2) The principle of data availability. Ensure that all indicator data is available. (3) The principle of policy sensitivity. In particular, the township variable is controlled to effectively remove the estimation bias caused by the urban-rural dual structure, thereby improving the accuracy and scientificity of the research. Table 3. Description of indicator variables Variable Type Variable Name Variable Code Variable Definition Dependent Variable Level of Public Health Service Provision PHS Entropy Method Calculation Independent Variable Proportion of Aging Population Ap Statistical Yearbook Population Density PD Statistical Yearbook Level of Economic Development EC GDP per Capita Control Variables Government Size Gov Ratio of General Budget Expenditure to Regional Gross Domestic Product (Gov) Urbanization Level UL Proportion of Urban Population (%) Open in a new tab Results analysis Descriptive statistics of variables and multicollinearity diagnostics. The level of public health service supply is between 0 and 1. This paper uses the Tobit model to explore the factors influencing the public health service supply level. The descriptive statistics of each variable are as follows (Table 4 ). Table 4. Descriptive statistics of variables Variable Obs Mean Std.Dev. Min Max Level of Public Health Service Supply 434 0.289 0.108 0.720 0.620 In Proportion of Aging Population 434 2.365 0.309 0.000 3.073 In Population Density 434 7.887 0.420 6.244 8.669 In Level of Economic Development 434 10.884 0.488 9.482 12.207 In Government Size 434 -1.427 0.476 -2.246 0.321 In Urbanization Level 434 4.044 0.236 3.121 4.495 Open in a new tab To ensure the stability and accuracy of the Tobit model and avoid multicollinearity between independent variables, this paper uses the variance inflation factor method for testing. The test results show that each variable’s variance inflation factor value is less than 10, and all independent variables pass the test. Therefore, the panel Tobit model constructed in this paper does not have multicollinearity problems (Table 5 ). Table 5. Multicollinearity test for variables Variable VIF In Proportion of Aging Population 4.60 In Population Density 3.89 In Level of Economic Development 1.55 In Government Size 1.51 In Urbanization Level 1.07 Mean VIF 2.53 Open in a new tab (2) Regression results analysis. This study selected the panel Tobit regression model to study the impact of different influencing factors on the public health service supply level. To ensure the accuracy and scientificity of the results, this paper selected the fixed effect model to explore the influencing factors of the level of public health service supply (Table 6 ). Table 6. Regression results of factors influencing the supply level of public health services VARIABLES National Region Tobit Model Fixed Effects Model In Proportion of Aging Population 0.0255*** (2.897) 0.0223** (2.512) In Population Density -0.00225 (-0.298) -0.00447 (-0.584) In Level of Economic Development 0.137*** (12.89) 0.138*** (12.63) Control Variables Yes Yes Constant -1.376*** (-17.07) -1.371*** (-17.14) Observations 434 N 31 Open in a new tab Note: *** p < 0.01, ** p < 0.05, * p < 0.1 The proportion of the aging population has a significant positive impact on the level of public health service supply, which is significant at the 1% level. For every 1% increase in the proportion of the aging population, the level of public health service supply increases by 0.0255%. The regional economic development level has a significant positive impact on the level of public health service supply, which is significant at the 1% level. For every 1% increase in the level of economic development, the level of public health service supply increases by 0.137%. Population density did not pass the significance test. Population aging has become a global challenge with far-reaching impacts on the public health system. As the proportion of the elderly population continues to rise, the demand for public health services, the allocation of medical resources, and the socioeconomic burden are facing unprecedented pressure. The impact of the aging population in the United States on the demand for hospital inpatient services in the next decade points out that aging will drive the use of inpatient services [ 75 ]. The impact of the aging population in Germany on the medical system is not only reflected in the cost and financial consequences, but also in the care itself [ 76 ]. For health resources, an increase in public health spending is associated with decreased preventable mortality [ 77 ]. Regions with high economic development levels have higher access to medical technology. With the growth of per capita GDP, the demand for high-quality medical resources among high-income groups has gradually increased, forcing the government to improve the quality of basic health services. Endogeneity and robustness tests Omitted variables and reverse causality can lead to correlations between explanatory variables and random disturbances. The model may have endogeneity problems, leading to biased parameter estimation results [ 78 ]. To solve this problem, this paper selects variables lagged by one period as instrumental variables and uses the instrumental variable method to conduct endogeneity analysis (Table 7 ). The F value of the first-stage estimate is 7013.05 and passes the 1% significance test, eliminating the problem of weak instrumental variables. Table 7. Endogeneity test VARIABLES First Stage Second Stage In Proportion of Aging Population 0.0217** (2.324) 0.0880*** (5.418) In Population Density -0.00214 (-0.224) 0.0502*** (4.816) In Level of Economic Development 0.159*** (8.121) 0.174*** (9.757) Control Variables Yes Yes Constant -1.434*** (-10.03) -0.835*** (-5.709) F Value 7013.05*** / Observations 403 403 N 31 31 Open in a new tab Note: *** p<0.01, ** p<0.05, * p<0.1 In the robustness test, the explanatory variables are Winsorized by 1% of the data above and below to test the robustness of the model [ 79 ]. At the same time, this study removes outliers and excludes extreme years to perform robustness tests. The results were obtained again using STATA17 software (Table 8 ). This is consistent with the results of the benchmark regression, indicating that the benchmark regression is robust. Table 8. Robustness test VARIABLES Truncation Exclusion of Outliers Tobit Model Fixed Effects Model Tobit Model Fixed Effects Model In Proportion of Aging Population 0.0435*** (3.386) 0.0365*** (2.809) 0.0267*** (2.970) 0.0235*** (2.597) In Population Density -0.00690 (-0.833) -0.00888 (-1.054) -0.00238 (-0.305) -0.00475 (-0.599) In Level of Economic Development 0.134*** (11.83) 0.136*** (11.66) 0.136*** (12.21) 0.137*** (11.93) Control Variables Yes Yes Yes Yes Constant -1.309*** (-13.99) -1.318*** (-14.07) -1.389*** (-16.61) -1.383*** (-16.61) Observations 434 403 N 31 31 Open in a new tab Note: *** p < 0.01, ** p < 0.05, * p < 0.1 Conclusions and recommendations Conclusion and outlook Based on the panel data of 31 provinces in China from 2010 to 2023, this paper constructs an objective evaluation index system for the supply level of public health services. Secondly, the Dagum Gini coefficient is used for empirical analysis to explore the spatiotemporal differences in the level of service supply from the provincial and regional dimensions. Finally, this study uses the Tobit model to analyze the factors that influence the level of public health service supply, from the dimensions of the proportion of the elderly population, the level of economic development, and population density. The research results show that since the new medical reform in 2009, the overall public health service supply level has significantly improved. This indirectly indicates that the latest medical reform policy has played a vital role. On the other hand, there are still significant differences in the level of public health service supply in different provinces or regions. Moreover, substantial differences exist in the eastern and western regions’ public health service supply levels. Overall, the differences in supply levels between and within regions are gradually narrowing. The supply of public health services in China’s eastern coastal areas shows a significantly high agglomeration. This indicates the existence of hotspots of coordinated development. Local low-high agglomeration reveals the existence of development depressions in high-level regions, highlighting spatial imbalance. Most regions’ spatial correlation is insignificant, and the overall distribution shows non-coordinated characteristics. The Tobit regression results show that the level of public health service supply is significantly influenced by the aging population and economic development level. This indicates that the aging population and economic development level have a significant positive impact on the supply of public health services. This study can provide valuable reference for the development of basic healthcare services in China. This article still has some research deficiencies. (1) The study did not involve county-level or community-level data. (2) Although the data span is long, it does not cover the benchmark before the new medical reform (such as before 2009), which limits the comparison of long-term trends. Given the above research deficiencies, future research can explore the following topics: (1) Expandable data sources, such as incorporating county-level or community-level panel data. And combine multi-source information, such as multi-source data, to capture more detailed spatiotemporal changes to supplement and verify the conclusions of this study. (2) Spatial econometric analysis can be used to quantify clustering effects and their driving factors. (3) In future research, more complete indicators should be included as much as possible to construct a comprehensive evaluation indicator system of subjective and objective dimensions. For example, innovative indicators such as residents’ health literacy and accessibility to digital services can be included to evaluate fairness more comprehensively. Recommendations First, the formulation of public health service policies needs to adhere to the value orientation of fairness and reduce regional disparities by redistributing medical resources. Through policy adjustments, the “trickle-down effect” [ 80 ] should be exerted to avoid focusing public health resource allocation on “strong” regions, and more attention should be paid to “weak” regions. At the same time, due to differences in economic development, resource endowment, geographical location, etc., among areas, the formulation and implementation of public health policies should fully consider local realities. For example, the eastern region pays more attention to service quality and competent medical care (such as the Yangtze River Delta Medical Alliance) to create a benchmark for qualified medical care. This can promote its radiation-driven effect on surrounding areas. For example, areas with high supply levels in western and northern Guangdong have a positive spillover effect [ 81 ]. The central region focuses on linking the central region’s rise strategy to promote regional cooperation. In response to the aging of the northeastern population and the outflow of young labor, the northeast region optimizes the dynamic allocation of medical resources. The western region prioritizes protecting grassroots facilities and training medical talents in a targeted manner. The region of the west implements the “Strengthening the Grassroots” project, prioritizing the construction of grassroots facilities. Targeted training of local medical talents and improvement of infrastructure and personnel allocation in the western region. Secondly, cross-regional coordination and resource allocation should be strengthened, focusing on overcoming regional gaps. Implement more regionally differentiated public health resource allocation standards and assessment guidelines to avoid “one size fits all”. For lagging regions, policies should allow for greater flexibility and key support. Optimize regional cooperation mechanisms and deepen the mechanism for developed eastern provinces to provide counterpart support to central, western, and northeastern provinces. Provide long-term and stable assistance in soft power such as talent, technology, management, and data. Improve the linkage mechanism between aging response and economic development. Actively explore and promote the “medical care and nursing care” pilot, use the fiscal advantages of economically developed regions to explore socialized service purchases, and alleviate the pressure of single government investment. In underdeveloped areas, the “health industry” driving model should be introduced to promote the development of medical resources. Focus on the elderly, children, pregnant women, and low-income groups. Finally, a regional coordination and linkage mechanism should be established to play a role in the “diffusion effect” between regions [ 82 ]. Through policy guidance and resource tilt, high-quality medical resources will be balanced. A regional cooperation platform should be built to strengthen regional collaboration and expand the capacity of high-quality medical resources. Deepen regional resource integration and capacity building to consolidate the foundation of fairness. Provincial governments should strengthen overall coordination and break down resource barriers between cities and counties within the province. Guide resources to tilt towards underdeveloped areas within the province. In the meantime, the size of the government should be optimized, and a clear hierarchy should be established. The decision-making and execution processes should be relatively efficient. Human, material, and financial resources should be effectively deployed to balance public health services and sustainable development. Moreover, market and social forces should be nurtured and utilized. This will diversify the supply of essential public health services. In addition, the “Internet + Medical Health” model should be used in today’s digital information age. This can improve the accuracy and efficiency of resource allocation. Thus, a digital public health service platform should cover urban and rural areas and regions. Promote the universal application of technologies such as remote diagnosis, treatment, and intelligent monitoring, narrowing the digital health gap between regions and urban and rural areas. Author contributions S.Z., J.L., Z.W. and S.C. wrote the main manuscript text and J.L. prepared Figs. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6. All authors reviewed the manuscript. Funding This work was supported partly by the Hubei University Provincial Teaching Research Project ‘Innovation Research on the Compound Talent Training Model of China''s Administrative Management Disciplines under the Threshold of New Liberal Arts Construction’ (Project No. 2022014) and ‘Research on the Optimization of the Management Standardization System of the Hubei Provincial Government’ commissioned by the Standardization Department of Hubei Provincial Market Supervision Bureau (Project No. 1203/250000471). Data availability Data is provided within the manuscript or supplementary information files. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate This study uses publicly available macro-statistical data from the National Bureau of Statistics of China, which has been anonymized and aggregated by region and industry. As the data does not contain any personally identifiable information or sensitive business data, and is in accordance with national regulations, ethics approval and participant consent are not required. The use of such publicly available, anonymized data exempts the study from further ethical review and the need for informed consent, in compliance with national regulations. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Shengjiao Zhu, Jinsi Liu and Zhihua Wang have contributed equally to this work. Contributor Information Jinsi Liu, Email: liujinsi1798@whu.edu.cn. Shixiang Chen, Email: chensx@whu.edu.cn. References 1. Wang C, et al. Health care in China improvement, challenges, and reform. Chest. 2013;143(2):524–31. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Yin C, et al. Inequality of public health and its role in Spatial accessibility to medical facilities in China. Appl Geogr. 2018;92:50–62. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Shen Y, Sun Z. Estimating the spatial correlation and convergence of China’s healthcare resources allocation: evidence from the Yangtze River Delta Region. Arch Public Health. 2022;80(1). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 4. Thorpe A, et al. The three domains of public health: an internationally relevant basis for public health education? Public Health. 2008;122(2):201–10. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Arroyos-Calvera D, et al. The efficiency-equity trade-off, self-interest, and moral principles in health and safety valuation. Soc Sci Med. 2019;238. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 6. Goodyear-Smith F, Ashton T. New Zealand health system: universalism struggles with persisting inequities. Lancet. 2019;394(10196):432–42. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ]