lar ] 73. United Kingdom Health Security Agency (UKHSA) . Ukhsa strategic plan 2023 to 2026 UK: UKHSA; (2023) (updated 2023 July). Available online at: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/1173959/UKHSA_3_year_strategy.pdf [ Google Scholar ] 74. Ministry for Health and Prevention (France) . National Health Strategy 2023–2033: launch of an online public consultation. [internet]. France: Ministry for Health and Prevention; (2023) (updated 2023 September 08). Available online at: https://sante.gouv.fr/actualites/actualites-du-ministere/article/strategie-nationale-de-sante-2023-2033-ouverture-d-une-consultation-publique-en [ Google Scholar ] 75. Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) . Review of National Public Health Strategies in selected countries [internet]. Dublin: Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) (2023). Available online at: https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2023-10/Review-of-national-public-health-trategies-in-selected-countries-Report.pdf [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Data_Sheet_1.pdf (841KB, pdf)',38,'2025-10-20 07:16:52','2025-10-20 07:16:52');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7404,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12238882','Disparities in outpatient and home health service utilization following stroke: results of a 9-year cohort study in Northern California.','Chan L, Wang H, Terdiman J, Hoffman J, Ciol MA, Lattimore BF, Sidney S, Quesenberry C, Lu Q, Sandel ME.','2009','sharpened noninstitutional hoenig disenrolled irq irq irq irq stansbury farther stoke determinates unstudied aapmr pmrjournal terdiman oakland jeanne marcia ciol bernadette lattimore sidney oakland quesenberry sandel carnethon e181 bandinelli guralnik postrehabilitation beiser kase rodin saliba brummel zorowitz 36e raaschou vive stoier raaschou vive stoier dobkin uswatte s77 s91 gulanick hoenig dauphinee mhurchu rubenach winsor rubenach mhurchu winsor ahern gladman gladman kollen twisk lankhorst koetsier cd002925 stansbury lisabeth christi sandel terdiman morozova kuhlemeier matchar samsa','Abstract Objective : To examine whether there are disparities in utilization of outpatient and home care services after stroke. Design : Retrospective cohort study. Setting : The Kaiser Permanente of Northern California health care system, which provides health care for approximately 3.3 million members. Participants : A total of 11,119 patients hospitalized for a stroke between 1996 and 2003 and followed for 1 year. Main Outcome Measures : Receipt of outpatient rehabilitation (physical therapy, occupational therapy, speech pathology, or physical medicine and rehabilitation/physiatry visits), and/or home health care. Results : There were significant differences in outpatient rehabilitation visits and home health enrollment during the year after acute care discharge for all the parameters under study. Older age and female gender were associated with less outpatient rehabilitation treatment, but these subpopulations were more likely to be enrolled in home health care. Non-whites, patients from urban areas, those with ischemic strokes, and those with longer acute care hospital stays had relatively more outpatient rehabilitation and were also more likely to be enrolled in the home health program. In addition, patients living in geographic areas with a median household income of $80,000 or more had significantly more outpatient rehabilitation visits than did patients living in lower income areas. Conclusions : Variations in outpatient rehabilitation visits and in home health care exist in this large integrated health system in terms of age, gender, race/ethnicity, residence area, type of stroke, and length of stay in an acute care hospital. The Kaiser Permanente integrated health care system seems to have outpatient stroke rehabilitation and home health programs that are providing care without disparities in relation to non-white populations, but other disparities appear to exist that may be related to socioeconomic factors, referral patterns, family support systems, or other cultural factors that have not been identified. INTRODUCTION On average, a stroke occurs every 40 seconds in this country. Stroke is the third leading cause of death in the United States and a leading cause of disability among adults. Two-thirds of the 4.8 million stroke survivors in the United States require health care services, including rehabilitation [ 1 ]. About a third of this group has severe disabilities and 40% have moderate disabilities [ 2 , 3 ]. Over the last 10 years, several stroke rehabilitation guidelines have been developed by various organizations, including the health system studied in this research [ 4 – 7 ]. These guidelines emphasize the need for interventions by a specialized stroke team, in acute hospitals, rehabilitation facilities, skilled nursing facilities, outpatient programs, or by home health care and hospice providers. Several factors have sharpened the focus on outpatient and home health care for stroke. Stroke patients are being moved though the inpatient system (acute hospital, inpatient rehabilitation facility, and skilled nursing facility) at a much faster rate with a much shorter overall length of stay. This has led to an increased need for noninstitutional services for this patient population. In addition, longitudinal studies indicate that stroke recovery typically continues months after the acute event, long after the majority of patients are discharged from inpatient settings [ 8 , 9 ]. Evidence suggests that rehabilitation, even at these later stages, may improve functional outcomes through a variety of mechanisms, including neural reorganization at the cellular level, and by improved function developed through adaptive, compensatory strategies [ 10 , 11 ]. Rehabilitation in the outpatient stroke population may also have a prophylactic benefit. Several stroke guidelines recommend aerobic training, strength, flexibility, coordination, and balance exercise for treatment of stroke as well as prevention of cardiovascular and cerebrovascular disease, including a second stroke [ 7 , 12 ]. Finally, there is evidence that some of the inpatient rehabilitation now occurring might be accomplished in outpatient settings. After an interdisciplinary team model is in place, it seems logical to conclude that rehabilitation could occur in a variety of settings. In fact, research by Hoenig et al suggests that process and structural elements may be more important than the setting of care in producing optimal outcomes in rehabilitation [ 13 ]. In this study, a lack of organized care delivery and low staff expertise led to the poorest outcomes; high staff expertise resulted in the best process and best outcomes for patients with stroke, regardless of institutional setting. Other research suggests that early discharge from the hospital is possible if stroke rehabilitation care can be administered at home and the patient does not require inpatient services [ 14 – 16 ]. There have been several randomized controlled trials demonstrating the value of outpatient stroke rehabilitation. In one study, patients in an outpatient stroke rehabilitation program showed improvements in measures of self-esteem and socialization [ 17 ]. Other studies have documented the benefits of occupational therapy [ 18 , 19 ] and physical therapy in outpatient programs [ 20 , 21 ]. A Cochrane review of outpatient rehabilitation research concluded that “for every 100 stroke patients residing in the community, … 7 patients would be spared a poor outcome if they received therapy-based rehabilitation services” [ 22 ]. Despite the fact that research strongly suggests that outpatient rehabilitation is useful, the authors know little about whether there are racial and ethnic disparities in the receipt of these services. There is a wealth of information suggesting that health care disparities exist in nearly all other aspects of stroke care [ 23 ]. Across various regions of the United States, the rates of stroke for blacks have been more than twice the rates for whites, and these differences are more pronounced in the younger age groups [ 1 ]. Hispanics also share an excess burden of disease and mortality from stroke [ 24 ]. Evidence has also been accumulating that acute care and rehabilitation care are not equally distributed among ethnic groups [ 23 ]. Likewise, there have been suggestions of ethnic differences in regard to the extent of residual disability and rehabilitation outcomes in a number of studies, although the evidence is inconsistent. In the integrated Kaiser Permanente system, after acute stroke hospitalization, patients are referred to a variety of settings that are part of an integrated system that includes outpatient centers, one rehabilitation hospital, skilled nursing facilities, a home health system, and a home-based hospice system. Physicians in The Permanente Medical Group are key decision-makers about the appropriate next level of care after the acute hospital and in post-acute care; discharge planners and, in some cases, social workers and case managers, also help to facilitate the acute care discharge. In outpatient and home-based care, the primary care physician and, in some cases a Permanente Medical Group physiatrist, are involved in making decisions about services or next level of care. The Permanente Medical Group Stroke Guidelines have used since the late 1990s and revisions are made to the guidelines every 2 years. The criteria for the various levels of post-acute care were not substantially revised during the period the authors chose to study. The primary objective of this project was to increase understanding of the nature of disparities in utilization of outpatient and home care services after stroke. The authors hypothesized that minorities would receive less outpatient care and home health services. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study Design and Study Sample A retrospective cohort study was conducted. This article is a follow-up to a study that examined disparities in post-stroke inpatient care (in inpatient rehabilitation hospital or a skilled nursing facility), and thus the study cohort has been described previously [ 25 ]. In brief, the authors identified all stroke patients admitted to Kaiser Permanente Northern California hospitals between 1996 and 2003, using Kaiser Permanente’s computerized encounter databases. Patients were considered for inclusion in the cohort if their primary discharge diagnosis included one of the following 9th International Classification of Disease codes: 430.x, 431.x, 433.01433.99, 434.01–434.99, 436.x, or 997.02. Patients with only one stroke during the study period were identified for further investigation (n = 22,522). Patients were excluded from the final cohort if they disenrolled from the Kaiser Permanente System or died within 365 days of discharge from acute care. Patients were also excluded if they were diagnosed with a brain tumor, abscess, or traumatic brain injury at admission or within 1 year of discharge. In addition, 3% of the cases who were categorized as “other” or who had missing data records in terms of race/ethnicity (“unknown”) were excluded from further data analyses. A total of 11,119 patients remained as the final study cohort. Data Collection Patient demographic data were collected, including age, gender, self-reported race/ethnicity, and it was also determined if they lived in a rural or urban area. Race and ethnicity were divided into several mutually exclusive categories: white, black, Asian, or Hispanic. Median household income was estimated using 2000 census track data, California poverty line information, and the distribution of the data. It resulted in the following grouping: ≤$40,000, $40,001-$80,000, and >$80,000. Type of stroke was identified as hemorrhagic or ischemic. Length of stay (LOS) data for acute hospitalization was also extracted. Patients in the cohort were followed for 365 days after discharge. Outpatient rehabilitation and home health utilization information was obtained from the Kaiser Permanente encounter databases. Using a combination of department and procedure codes, the authors identified each time a stroke patient was seen as an outpatient by a physiatrist, speech pathologist, an occupational or physical therapist in the outpatient setting. The outpatient encounter coding system was not refined enough to reliably determine the type of provider, nor the duration of that encounter for the entire cohort. In a similar manner, the home health program data were determined regarding enrollment in the program, but detailed records on the types and intensity of services were not consistently available. Data Analysis Descriptive statistical analyses were conducted for all explanatory and outcome variables under study. Mean, standard deviation (SD), median, and interquartile range were calculated for continuous variables, including age, length of stay, and numbers of outpatient rehabilitation visits. (Because length of stay and outpatient rehabilitation visits are extremely skewed to high numbers, median and IRQ are more appropriate in summarizing the data.) Frequency tables and percentages were used for the description of categorical variables. Explanatory variables for each person included age at acute care discharge, gender, race/ethnicity, rural/urban residence, median household income, type of stroke, and length of stay for the stroke hospitalization. The 2 outcome measures under study included the number of outpatient rehabilitation visits during follow-up and a binary measure, “ever had home health care within 365 days.” Bivariate analyses were conducted to explore the unadjusted associations between explanatory variables and outcome measures, with corresponding statistics. Wilcoxon signed-rank test was used to compare median of LOS or rehab visits among sub groups of a categorical variable. The association of explanatory variables and the number of outpatient rehabilitation visits was further analyzed using Poisson regression model which is appropriate for counting variables. The association of explanatory variables and “ever had home health within 365 days” was analyzed by means of a logistic regression. Significance level for all variables was set to .05. SAS 9.13 was used for data analysis. RESULTS The mean age of the study cohort ( Table 1 ) was 69.7 years (SD 13.0 years). Of the 11,119 patients, 51.1% were female and 48.9% were male. In all, 70.2% were white; 10.8% black; 8.8% Asian; and 7.4% Hispanic. In terms of residence, 4.0% lived in rural areas and 96.0% in urban areas. Of the study patients, 21.8% lived in areas where median household income was $40,000 or less, 59.7% in areas where it was between $40,001 and $80,000, and 18.5% in areas where it was more than $80,000. In the cohort, 85.5% had had an ischemic stroke and the remainder a hemorrhagic stroke. The average acute care LOS was 3.9 days (SD 3.5 days), with a median of 3.0 days (interquartile range 2.0–5.0). Statistically significant differences were found for most of the variables by race/ethnicity ( P < .001). Whites were older (71.6 ± 12.3 years) than any other racial/ethnic group, and whites (51.6%) and blacks (53.9%) had higher percentages of females than did Asians (47.2%) or Hispanics (46.1%). Higher proportions of whites and Hispanics lived in rural areas, whereas higher proportions of blacks and Asians lived in urban areas. Asians and whites were more likely than other groups to live in areas with a median household income greater than $80,000, while black and Hispanic populations were more likely than the others to live in areas with a median household income equal to or less than $40,000. Asians had a higher percentage of hemorrhagic stroke than the other groups. In addition, Asians had a longer average acute care hospital stay than the other groups ( P = .0014). Table 1. Kaiser Permanente Northern California Stroke Cohort, 1996–2003: Characteristics of study patients Cohort Characteristics Racial/Ethnic Group Total White Black Asian Hispanic n (% of total) 8038 (70.2) 1232 (10.8) 1006 (8.8) 843 (7.4) 11,119 Age (y) Mean ± SD 71.6 ± 12.3 65.4 ± 13.2 63.7 ± 12.9 64.9 ± 13.9 69.7 ± 13.0 Females (%) 51.6 53.9 47.2 46.1 51.1 Living area (%) Rural 4.9 0.4 0.9 3.6 4.0 Urban 95.1 99.6 99.1 96.4 96.0 Living area with median household income (%) <$40,001 19.0 40.7 17.0 26.7 21.8 $40,001-$80,000 61.0 49.9 61.1 60.6 59.7 >$80,000 20.0 9.4 21.9 12.7 18.5 Type of stroke (%) Hemorrhagic 12.8 14.1 26.4 17.6 14.5 Ischemic 87.2 86.0 73.7 82.4 85.5 Acute care LOS in days Mean ± SD 3.8 ± 3.4 4.0 ± 3.9 4.3 ± 3.8 3.9 ± 3.6 3.9 ± 3.5 Median (IRQ) 3.0 (2.0–5.0) 3.0 (2.0–5.0) 3.0 (2.0–5.0) 3.0 (2.0–5.0) 3.0 (2.0–5.0) Open in a new tab IQR = interquartile range (25th and 75th percentile of the data); LOS = length of stay; SD = standard deviation. The median number of outpatient rehabilitation visits for the entire cohort ( Table 2 ) was 6.0 (interquartile range 1.0–21.0). Age at acute care discharge was negatively related to the number of outpatient visits, with a correlation coefficient of −0.1527. Female patients had fewer visits (median 5.0) than men (median 6.0), and median visits differed significantly by race/ethnicity. The median number was higher for Asians (7.0), blacks (7.0), and Hispanics (6.0) than for whites (5.0). Patients living in rural or lower or middle-income areas and those who had a hemorrhagic stroke also had fewer visits. Finally, acute care LOS had a positive association with the number of visits (correlation coefficient of 0.1262). Table 2. Outpatient rehabilitation visits during the year after acute care hospital discharge Cohort Characteristics Outpatient Rehabilitation Visits P R Mean ± SD Median (IRQ) Total 24.4 ± 46.1 6.0 (1.0–21.0) — Age (y) −0.1527 <.0001 Gender <.0001 Female 21.7 ± 42.2 5.0 (1.0–18.0) Male 27.4 ± 49.9 6.0 (2.0–25.0) Race/ethnicity <.0001 White 22.7 ± 44.5 5.0 (1.0–18.0) Black 28.7 ± 48.4 7.0 (2.0–30.0) Asian 30.3 ± 52.6 7.0 (1.0–32.0) Hispanic 26.5 ± 48.4 6.0 (2.0–23.0) Living area .0086 Rural 21.3 ± 41.5 4.0 (1.0–16.0) Urban 24.7 ± 46.4 6.0 (1.0–21.0) Living area with median household income .0027 <$40,000 22.1 ± 42.1 5.0 (1.0–19.0) $40,000-$80,000 24.5 ± 46.2 5.0 (1.0–21.0) >$80,000 27.8 ± 50.7 7.0 (2.0–25.0) Type of stroke .0003 Hemorrhagic 25.7 ± 49.3 4.0 (1.0–20.0) Ischemic 24.4 ± 45.7 6.0 (2.0–21.0) Acute care LOS in days 0.1262 <.0001 Open in a new tab IQR = interquartile range (25th and 75th percentile of the data); LOS = length of stay; R = Spearman correlation coefficient; SD = standard deviation. Overall, just under half of the study cohort (49.1%) was enrolled in a home health program ( Table 3 ). Older and female patients were more likely to be enrolled in the program. Blacks had the highest rate of enrollment (51.3%), followed by whites (49.4%), Asians (47.9%), and Hispanics (45.1%). Rural residence was linked to a lower rate of enrollment (37.3%) than was the urban classification (49.6%). There was no significant difference by household income. Patients with ischemic stroke were more likely to be enrolled in home health care (50.8%) than were patients with hemorrhagic stroke (39.0%). Patients enrolled in the program also had a significantly longer acute care LOS. Table 3. Home health enrollment during the year after acute care hospital discharge Cohort Characteristics Home Health Enrollment P Yes No Total (%) 49.1 50.9 — Age (y) <.0001 Mean ± SD 72.3 ± 11.7 66.9 ± 13.8 Gender (%) <.0001 Female 52.3 47.7 Male 45.7 54.3 Race/ethnicity (%) .0343 White 49.4 50.6 Black 51.3 48.7 Asian 47.9 52.1 Hispanic 45.1 54.9 Living area (%) <.0001 Rural 37.3 62.3 Urban 49.6 50.4 Living area with median household income (%) .6628 <$40,001 48.4 51.6 $40,001-$80,000 49.3 50.7 >$80,000 48.4 51.6 Type of stroke (%) <.0001 Hemorrhagic 39.0 61.0 Ischemic 50.8 49.2 Acute Care LOS in days <.0001 Mean ± SD 4.1 (3.6) 3.6 ± 3.5 Median (IRQ) 3.0 (2.0–5.0) 3.0 (2.0–4.0) Open in a new tab IQR = interquartile range (25th and 75th percentile of the data); LOS = length of stay; SD = standard deviation. The Poisson regression model ( Table 4 ) confirmed the relationships between numerous explanatory variables and the number of outpatient rehabilitation visits ( Table 2 ). Factors associated with a higher number of visits included younger age at acute care discharge, male gender, race/ethnicity other than white, urban residence, higher income living area, an ischemic stroke, and a longer acute care LOS. Table 4. Poisson regression model for number of outpatient visits during the year after acute care discharge * Variable P Value Estimated Rate Ratio 95% Confidence Interval Age (y) <.0001 0.9805 0.9802–0.9808 Gender (reference: male) <.0001 Female 0.83 0.82–0.84 Racial/ethnicity (reference: white) <.0001 Asian 1.06 1.05–1.08 Black 1.05 1.03–1.06 Hispanic 1.01 0.99–1.02 Living area (reference: urban) .0023 Rural 0.97 0.95–0.99 Living area with median household income (reference: >$80,000) <.0001 0-$40,000 0.81 0.80–0.82 $40,000–80,000 0.87 0.86–0.88 Type of stroke (reference: ischemic) <.0001 Hemorrhagic 0.736 0.727–0.744 Acute care length of stay (days) <.0001 1.066 1.065–1.067 Open in a new tab * Year of discharge and Kaiser Permanente Northern California facility areas are also adjusted. Results from the logistical regression model ( Table 5 ) were generally in agreement with the relationship between explanatory variables and the rate of home health care enrollment presented in Table 3 , with a change in the association between race/ethnicity and enrollment. Older age at acute care discharge, female gender, race/ethnicity other than white, urban residence, ischemic stroke, and longer acute care LOS were all associated with the probability of enrollment. Whites had the second-highest enrollment rate in the bivariate analysis ( Table 3 ), but after controlling for other covariates, they had the lowest enrollment rate among the race/ethnicity groups. Table 5. Multiple logistic regression model for home health enrollment during the year after post-acute care discharge * Variable P Value Estimated Odds Ratio 95% Confidence Interval Age (y) <.0001 1.04 1.03–1.04 Gender (reference: male) <.0001 Female 1.23 1.14–1.33 Racial/ethnicity (reference: white) <.0001 Asian 1.30 1.13–1.50 Black 1.36 1.19–1.55 Hispanic 1.17 1.00–1.36 Living area (reference: urban) <.0001 Rural 0.59 0.48–0.73 Type of stroke (reference: ischemic) <.0001 Hemorrhagic 0.61 0.54–0.69 Acute care length of stay (days) <.0001 1.08 1.07–1.09 Open in a new tab * Year of discharge and Kaiser Permanente Northern California facility areas are also adjusted. The analysis showed that race/ethnicity, rural/urban living status, type of stroke, and acute care LOS had similar effects in the 2 areas of interest: receiving outpatient rehabilitation and enrollment in home health. In contrast, the associations of age and gender with the 2 outcome measures were in opposition: older patients and those of female gender had fewer outpatient visits but were more likely to be enrolled in a home health program. In addition, median household income was a significant predictor for number of outpatient visits but was unrelated to enrollment in home health. DISCUSSION This study revealed that disparities or variations exist in outpatient rehabilitation service utilization after stroke in the Kaiser Permanente Northern California health system. Age, gender, race/ethnicity, rural/urban residence, median household income, and type of stroke are all significantly associated with the outpatient rehabilitation and home health care. LOS at acute care hospitalization is also an independent predictor of outpatient rehabilitation service utilization and may in fact be a proxy for stroke severity, a factor not identified in the authors’ study methodology. Results suggest that, within the Northern California Kaiser Permanente Health System, there are some disparities in the provision of post-stroke rehabilitation. However, the findings were not in the direction that the authors hypothesized (ie, disparities based on race-ethnicity). In fact, after controlling for a number of patient and system factors, minorities appear to receive more outpatient rehabilitation, and likely more home health care than their non-minority counterparts. These results differ from a number of studies which have highlighted racial-ethnic disparities in stroke rehabilitation and are consistent with others. For example, Stansbury and colleagues highlighted considerable evidence that blacks have strokes that are more severe, have worse functional outcomes and a higher mortality rate [ 23 ]. However, results in studies of disparities in service delivery are more varied and, to the authors’ knowledge, none have addressed outpatient or home health care. For instance, in a 2006 study of 12,208 Maryland Stroke patients, blacks were 42% more likely to be transferred to an inpatient rehabilitation facility [ 26 ]. Goldstein et al, in a cohort study of 1073 stroke patients in the Veterans Administration system, noted no differences in the rates of rehabilitation evaluation, or physical, occupational or recreational therapy, comparing whites to non-whites, during acute stroke care [ 27 ]. There are likely several reasons for the lack of expected racial-ethnic disparities found in the authors’ 2 studies (this one and a previous study [ 25 ]). First, Kaiser Permanente has made significant efforts to raise the cultural sensitivity and awareness of its providers through its culturally competent care programs. The health system has developed a series of stroke guidelines including a section on inpatient and outpatient rehabilitation. These guidelines may have helped standardize stroke care in the system and limit unintentional biases. In addition, there may have been unmeasured differences between the groups including stroke severity and comorbidities. It is well known that minorities have more severe strokes [ 1 ], and therefore they may need more rehabilitation. Last, urban versus rural residence can be a confounding factor that impacted the results. Rural areas, which have a greater number of white people, tend to have rehabilitation facilities that are fewer in number or farther away in distance. There are several limitations to this study. First of all, the encounter data the authors analyzed was not detailed. Kaiser Permanente has only recently instituted data collection efforts that will permit a more refined analysis of the types of therapy delivered to its members. Therefore, the results could be missing disparities in individual types of therapy. Second, the home health data the authors used also represented hospice use. Although the authors believe they excluded the vast majority of these cases by excluding those who died within 1 year, the home health utilization data probably does represent some hospice services. In addition, because the authors excluded those who died within 365 days of discharge, they may be missing disparities that existed for those individuals. The authors lacked outcome measures that might tell whether the rates of outpatient rehabilitation and home health were appropriate. The authors had limited data on severity of stroke or disability. As the research group speculated in a previous study [ 25 ], cultural factors may also affect the delivery of care. In the case of outpatient care, for example, availability of caregivers to provide transportation to and from outpatient centers may be a factor that affects those who receives care. In home health care delivery, cultural factors may also play a role not yet defined. CONCLUSIONS Despite some limitations, this study, which analyzed one of the largest stoke cohorts ever assembled, suggests that Kaiser Permanente delivers care without the expected racial-ethnic disparities. However, there were other disparities identified, and the data suggest that, in particular, age, gender, and socioeconomic factors may be important factors associated with disparities in care in outpatient and home health. In addition, type of stroke or severity of stroke may be other determinates of the type and extent of care delivered. The availability of caregivers or transportation (or its cost), or the access to outpatient centers (greater in urban areas of the health system), may be other important factors in the determination of who received these services outside the home. The degree of disability associated with home-bound status may be an additional unstudied factor that affected the results. Acknowledgments This study supported by funding from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Additional resources were provided by the Intramural Research Program of (Clinical Research Center) the National Institutes of Health, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, and Kaiser Permanente. Footnotes L.C. , H.W. , J.T. , J.H. , M.A.C. , B.F.L. , S.S. , C.Q. , Q.L. Disclosure: nothing to disclose M.E.S. Disclosure: 3A; 8B, NIH; 9, AAPMR president, employed by Kaiser Permanente Disclosure Key can be found on the Table of Contents and at www.pmrjournal.org The findings and conclusions in this report are those of the authors and do not necessarily represent the official position of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention or the National Institutes of Health. Contributor Information Leighton Chan, Rehabilitation Medicine Department, NIH Clinical Center, Building 10, CRC, Room 1-1469, 10 Center Drive, MSC 1604, Bethesda, MD 20892-1604.. Hua Wang, Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, Kaiser Foundation Rehabilitation Center, Vallejo, CA. Joe Terdiman, Kaiser Permanente Division of Research, Oakland, CA. Jeanne Hoffman, University of Washington, Seattle, WA. Marcia A. Ciol, University of Washington, Seattle, WA. Bernadette Ford Lattimore, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Atlanta, GA. Steven Sidney, Kaiser Permanente Division of Research, Oakland, CA. Charles Quesenberry, NIH Clinical Center, Bethesda, MD. Qi Lu, Kaiser Permanente Division of Research, Kaiser Foundation Rehabilitation Center, Vallejo, CA. M. Elizabeth Sandel, Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, Kaiser Foundation Rehabilitation Center, Vallejo, CA. REFERENCES 1. Lloyd-Jones D, Adams R, Carnethon M, et al. Heart disease and stroke statistics—2009 update: A report from the American Heart Association Statistics Committee and Stroke Statistics Subcommittee. Circulation 2009;119:e21–e181. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Ferrucci L, Bandinelli S, Guralnik JM, et al. Recovery of functional status after stroke. A postrehabilitation follow-up study. Stroke 1993; 24:200–205. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kelly-Hayes M, Beiser A, Kase CS, et al. The influence of gender and age on disability following ischemic stroke: The Framingham study. J Stroke Cerebrovasc Dis 2003;12:119–126. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Rodin M, Saliba D, Brummel-Smith K; American Geriatrics Society Clinical Practice 