he power of TV: cable television and women’s status in India. Quart J Econ. 2009;3:1057–1094. doi: 10.1162/qjec.2009.124.3.1057. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Rosenbaum PR, Rubin DB. The central role of the propensity score in observational studies for causal effects. Biometrika. 1983;70(1):41–55. doi: 10.1093/biomet/70.1.41. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 38. Rupa C. Endogenous fertility and growth in a model with old age support. Econ Theor. 1999;13(2):393–416. doi: 10.1007/s001990050261. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Ryan W. The new worlds of Thomas Robert Malthus: rereading the principle of population. Eur J History Econ Thought. 2019;26(1):225–229. doi: 10.1080/09672567.2019.1601847. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Tang D. An analysis of the characteristics of ethnic floating population in big cities of northwestern China: an example of Lanzhou city. Ethno-National Stud. 2006 doi: 10.4236/ajibm.2020.103044. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Diprete TA, et al. Do cross-national differences in the costs of children generate cross-national differences in fertility rates. Popul Res Policy Rev. 2003;22(5/6):439–477. doi: 10.1023/B:POPU.0000020961.89068.91. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Wang T, Peng X. The impact of social security on fertility desire: evidence from the new rural cooperative medical scheme. Econ Res J. 2015;50(2):103–117. [ Google Scholar ] 43. Xie Y. Influences of access to basic public services on urban integration willing of migrant workers: taking Jiangsu Province for example. Urban Problems. 2017 doi: 10.13239/j.bjsshkxy.cswt.170409. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Yakita A. Uncertain lifetime, fertility and social security. J Popul Econ. 2001;14(4):635–640. doi: 10.1007/s001480000049. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. You HX, Poston DL. Are floating migrants in China “Child-Bearing Guerillas”? An analysis of floating migration and fertility. Asian Pac Migr J. 2004;13(4):405–422. doi: 10.1177/011719680401300401. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Zhong B. On