carrion kaski kivela homophily eaax7310 varda retrum collaboratives vann','Abstract Objective: To learn feasible ways to increase multisector community partnership with tribal organizations, meaning tribal health authorities or American Indian and Alaska Native (AI/AN)–serving organizations, by examining characteristics of local public health systems with exceptional tribal organization participation. Design, Setting, and Participants: In total, 728 local public health departments were surveyed in 2018 to generate a nationally representative sample of local public health systems in the United States. A positive deviance approach using logistic regression helped identify local public health systems that had tribal organization participation despite characteristics that make such participation statistically unlikely. Local public health systems with exceptional tribal organization participation were compared with systems with conventional participation, examining measures known to impact the formation of public health partnerships. Main Outcome Measure: This study used an exploratory logistic regression approach to identify unique characteristics of local public health systems with exceptional tribal organization participation. Results: Of 728 health systems surveyed, 21 were identified as having exceptional tribal organization participation. Across varying thresholds to identify exceptional participation, having a higher network density and prioritizing equity in public health activities were found to consistently distinguish exceptional tribal organization participation in both nonrural and rural areas. Conclusions: Public health partnerships with tribal organizations are possible even in circumstances that make them unlikely. Efforts to build denser networks of collaborating organizations and prioritize equity may help public health systems achieve success with tribal organization partnerships. Keywords: AI/AN health, health equity, partnership, positive deviance, social network theory The 2020 US Census found that 87% of self-identified American Indian and Alaska Native (AI/AN) populations reside in US local public health jurisdictions, rather than tribal government jurisdictions. 1 Partnerships between tribal health authorities and federal agencies, such as the Indian Health Service (IHS) and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, have worked to reduce key disparities for AI/AN populations that health care services can address through better access or treatment for members of federally recognized tribes. 2 , 3 This can overlook the ability to address social determinants of health for AI/AN that occur at a local level in driving population health disparities, including the 7.1 million AI/AN who do not have recognized membership for federal support. 1 , 4 Multisector community partnerships are considered vital to improving health equity through social determinants of health and have been found in local public health efforts to improve population health outcomes when conditions are appropriate. 5 – 8 However, our research group found that in regions with the presence of an AI/AN-serving health facility, just 28% of local public health jurisdictions surveyed in 2018 reported participation of tribal organizations in public health activities. 9 To learn feasible ways to increase multisector community partnership with tribal organizations, the present study seeks to better understand characteristics of local public health systems with exceptional tribal organization participation. Case studies have documented a growing number of state and local tribal health collaborations emerging in recent years. In 2019, the Alaska Native Tribal Health Consortium collaborated closely with the Alaska Department of Health and Human Services to create “Healthy Alaskans 2020,” an initiative to improve health statewide with a specific focus on Alaska Native health. 10 Other public health partnerships commonly involve formal partnerships between tribal health authorities, community members, and local universities, such as a statewide collaboration in California to address the opioid epidemic, Partnership for Native American Cancer Prevention in Arizona, or SACRED connections to address substance use in Native American youth in the rural Midwest. 11 – 13 Tribal nations that installed their own independent local health departments were found to prioritize a culturally relevant approach to service delivery that acknowledges the specific needs of the community served and is implemented with input and collaboration from tribal community members. 14 This emphasis on cultural relevance and community participation is common across the case study collaborations highlighted here 10 – 13 and is cited as a major strength in tribal partnerships to improve AI/AN satisfaction with quality of health services. 2 While learning how to partner with tribal health authorities or AI/AN communities is important, understanding which communities across the United States currently involve tribal partnerships in local public health service delivery may explain the conditions necessary for partnership to be possible. Prior research by our group identified significant predictors of engagement with tribal organizations among local health jurisdictions. 9 These predictors include having an AI/AN population size over 1%, being proximate to reservation land, and having a regional presence of an IHS-funded tribal health facility. 9 However, not all jurisdictions that engaged tribal organizations fit this specific profile. Positive deviance is an exploratory research approach that has been used to identify why some communities have an exceptional outcome or success despite what is predicted on the basis of known risk factors such as socioeconomic status. 15 , 16 The approach helps uncover potential solutions already in practice to achieve population health goals. 15 Its use is especially relevant in circumstances of inequality, as entities being evaluated for exceptional outcomes are facing similar disadvantages in economic restrictions and structural barriers. 17 To our knowledge, this is the first time that positive deviance analysis has been used to understand tribal organization participation in local public health systems. Here, we examined characteristics of local public health systems that exhibit exceptional tribal organization participation. Specifically, we sought to understand which characteristics known to impact multisector community partnerships differentiated exceptional tribal organization participation, including health care, community, demographic, public health, and network characteristics. Methods Study design A retrospective, cross-sectional study was replicated to identify exceptional tribal organization participation in local public health systems in 2018. 9 The original study used a logistic regression model of community characteristics to understand outcomes in tribal organization participation in public health activities. 9 Building from that model, we examined additional measures known to impact public health partnership formation to further understand support for relationships with tribal organizations. All measures were calculated at the level of the local public health jurisdiction. Survey This study used data from the National Longitudinal Survey of Public Health Systems (NALSYS), which follows a nationally representative sample of local public health agency jurisdictions within the 50 US states and the District of Columbia. Because local public health agencies operate as units of local government in the United States, the jurisdictions covered in NALSYS include counties, cities, towns, and multicounty special districts. Importantly, the jurisdictions governed by federally recognized tribes were not included in NALSYS because these jurisdictions represent sovereign nations rather than local governments. A 5-year average total of 3.6 million self-identified AI/AN residents lived in local public health jurisdictions surveyed by NALSYS in 2018, 18 making up roughly 43% of the total self-identified AI/AN population surveyed in the 2020 US Census that live in US jurisdictions. 1 A stratified random sample of local public health jurisdictions was selected for the survey. It included oversampling of jurisdictions that contained at least 100 000 residents to improve generalizability of results of jurisdictions that only make up a minority of local health departments but serve more than half of US residents. 19 Jurisdictions from 4 states were also oversampled (n = 154) as requested by state health departments participating in public health restructuring programs (Kentucky, Ohio, Oregon, and Washington). In the 2018 survey wave, a total of 728 jurisdictions responded, giving a 58% response rate that included all states except for Rhode Island and Hawaii. Five responses were dropped from the analysis because of large numbers of unanswered questions. In each sampled jurisdiction, directors of local public health agencies were requested to complete a survey that asks them to report information about 19 public health activities, including whether or not each activity was implemented in the jurisdiction, and the types of community organizations that participated in implementing each activity. For example, the survey asked about conducting a community needs assessment, investigating adverse health events, and reporting to the media about priority health issues to the community (see Supplemental Digital Content Table 1, available at http://links.lww.com/JPHMP/B246 ). The survey included prompts for 33 types of community organizations, referred to as sectors, that may or may not participate in each activity. “Tribal organizations” was included as one of these sectors (see Supplemental Digital Content Table 1, available at http://links.lww.com/JPHMP/B246 ). Data Positive deviance analysis included measures from the logistic regression model calculated in our original study that addressed population demographics, health care access, proximity to nearest tribal reservation, and presence of an AI/AN-serving health facility within local public health jurisdictions (Table 1 ). For this study to fully examine positive deviance, all survey observations were included in this study rather than being restricted to only public health jurisdictions with an AI/AN-serving health facility that was done in our original study. 9 TABLE 1. Predictors of Tribal Organization Participation in Local Public Health Systems, NALSYS 2018 a . Extensive Margin SE Data Source AI/AN percent—below 1% 0.093 0.083 2018 American Community Survey 5-Year Estimate AI/AN percent—above 1% 0.018*** 0.006 AI/AN-serving health facility IHS Facility Directory IHS facility 0.042* 0.026 Tribal facility with IHS funding 0.088*** 0.028 Tribal facility without IHS funding −0.028 0.043 Urban Indian Health Program 0.002 0.030 Tribal Epidemiology Center −0.050 0.050 Reservation land distance <80 miles −0.019*** 0.006 US Census Bureau''s American Indian Reservation Statistical Area Reservation land distance >80 miles 0.003 0.003 Multicounty jurisdiction 0.033 0.034 2018 NALSYS Rural −0.064*** 0.024 Rural-Urban Commuting Area Code Primary care providers per 10 000 0.009*** 0.002 2019-2020 Area Health Resource Files Population over 65 y, % 0.002 0.003 Income per capita ($10 000) 0.003 0.011 Poverty, % 0.004 0.003 Uninsured, % 0.004 0.003 4-y college degree, % −0.007*** 0.003 Unemployment, % −0.031 0.024 R 2 0.392 Observations 728 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: AI/AN, American Indian and Alaskan Native; IHS, Indian Health Service; NALSYS, National Longitudinal Survey of Public Health Systems; SE, standard error. a Statistical significance is reported at * P < .10, ** P < .05, *** P < .01. The table presents marginal effects of logistic regression model with robust standard errors in parentheses. Logistic regression model estimates the probability of any tribal inclusion (ie, extensive margin). AI/AN above and below 1% are continuous splines variables created using a knot at 1% AI/AN population as a percentage of the total population. Reservation land distance within or above 80 miles from the center of the public health jurisdiction constitutes continuous splines variables created using a knot at 80 miles. AI/AN-serving health facility indicates whether a facility is present in the public health jurisdiction or adjacent counties to the public health jurisdiction. Public health measures Positive deviance analysis additionally included public health measures of the presence of a local board of health, local health department funding, and local health department employees. Local boards of health can positively influence various local health department goals, such as achieving national standards through accreditation and community health improvement planning. 20 Partner engagement was identified as one of 6 main functions of local boards of health to be more representative of communities and collaborate with key community stakeholders. 21 The amount of funding to local health departments is known to impact the development of partnership in local public health systems. 22 Public heath partnership requires time and dedicated employee support beyond direct services a local health department may provide. 23 Data for local health department revenue and employees came from the 2019 National Profile of Local Health Departments survey, and 2016 survey data were used if 2019 values were missing. 24 Network measures Network theories describe the relationships among individuals or organizations to analyze their impact on an outcome. 25 When there may be a large number of relationships involved in a network, such as among a whole community or a public health system, the existence of relationships can be captured by mutual involvement in an activity, without having to measure the relationships directly. This is known as a two-mode network. 26 Positive deviance analysis included two-mode network measures of residents within local public health jurisdictions and the two-mode network of sectors that make up their local public health systems. These measures were social capital index of residents, local public health system network density and network betweenness, and the effectiveness and equity of public health services delivered by local public health systems. We used a 2014 social capital index measure that was developed to compare differences in civic engagement of residents across counties in the United States. 27 It describes overall participation of residents in important civic activities such as volunteering, voting, and being members of formalized social groups. While social capital can be a challenging concept to pin down and measure, it can capture the impactful phenomenon of how individual investment in relationships contributes to communal social good, such as trust in public institutions or collective action. 28 Higher levels of social capital have been found to be predictive of higher levels of specific types of public health partnerships. 29 All other network measures were calculated from data in 2018 NALSYS to describe local public health systems. RStudio version 2022.07.2+56 with the “sna” package version 2.6 was used for calculations. 30 Network density and network betweenness were selected because they impact the ability to join networks. Network density was measured as the total number of connections among sectors within the local public health network divided by the total possible number of connections. 30 Network density affects triadic closure, or the likelihood of forming relationships with others based on how many relationships already exist in a network. 31 Network betweenness was measured as the total number of times a sector helped mediate the shortest connection between 2 other sectors that were not directly connected to each other. 30 A high value for network betweenness can capture gatekeeping, or when a small number of entities are responsible for bridging the majority of relationships that can create structural bottlenecks in forming new relationships. 31 An indicator for public health effectiveness was constructed on the basis of 50% or more of 19 public health activities listed in 2018 NALSYS being rated as meeting “about half” to “fully meets” community need. Local health departments that were surveyed self-rated the effectiveness of the 19 public health activities performed by participating sectors in their local public health network. An indicator for public health equity was constructed similarly based on rating 50% or more of 4 key public health activities in which “about half” to “most” of the effort contributed to reducing health disparities. Performance of public health activities and focusing on health disparities within social determinants of health have been primary goals of public health systems to improve population health outcomes. 32 Multisectoral partnership has been believed to be essential in effectively addressing social determinants of health and may be prioritized by local health departments that also prioritize effectiveness or equity. 5 , 32 Analysis Identifying exceptional participation Stata version 16.0 (StataCorp LP, College Station, Texas) was used to replicate the logistic regression model in our original study that examined the relationship between community characteristics and tribal organization participation in local public health systems. 9 An additional spline of a covariate was selected for inclusion in the model based on likelihood ratio tests demonstrating improved model fit. The final logistic regression model was used to predict tribal organization participation for each observation along a spectrum from 0 to 1 for observations that in actuality had tribal organization participation and 0 to −1 for observations that in actuality did not have participation and to calculate the standardized residual between predicted and actual participation (Table 1 ). These 728 observations were then ranked by their standardized residual to observe natural breaks in outlier observations (see Supplemental Digital Content Table 2, available at http://links.lww.com/JPHMP/B246 ). Observations with a standardized residual greater than 3 were classified as having positively exceptional participation, and those with a standardized residual less than −3 were classified as having negatively exceptional participation. Observations with conventional participation were restricted to observations with a 1 to −1 standardized residual. Deviance comparison Measures between observations with exceptional and conventional tribal organization participation were compared for statistical significance using t tests for continuous variables and chi-squared tests for indicator variables. Because of the small sample size of observations with exceptional participation, large differences in means and distribution of values were taken into consideration to determine meaningful differences. A sensitivity analysis compared results using alternative cutoff points of ±1.5 and ±2 of the standardized residual to classify observations with exceptional participation. Results were also stratified by rural and nonrural observations to examine consistency in the direction and significance of comparisons. Rural was defined using guidance from the US Health and Human Services Office of Rural Health Policy to identify jurisdictions with a rural-urban commuting area code (RUCA) of 4-9. 33 The logistic regression model from our original study included several socioeconomic variables that were found to be sensitive to using different thresholds for classifying observations with an exceptional outcome. The sensitivity was observed by changes in the direction and significance of comparisons between observations with an exceptional outcome and a conventional outcome. In contrast, variables that were not included in the logistic regression model showed consistent results in differences between observations. Regardless, we stratified results across all variables by rural and nonrural observations and found consistency when comparing results between observation groups regardless of which threshold was used to classify observations with an exceptional outcome (Tables 2 and 3 ). TABLE 2. Health Care, Community, and Demographic Characteristics of Local Public Health Systems With Exceptional and Conventional Tribal Organization Participation, NALSYS, 2018 a . Exceptional (>3 STD RES) Exceptional (>1.5 STD RES) Conventional (>−1 and <1 STD RES) Mean SD Mean SD Mean SD Health care characteristics AI/AN-serving health facility Nonrural 0.200 0.414 0.441 0.504 0.358 0.480 Rural 0.000 0.000 0.333 0.500 0.252 0.435 Tribal epidemiology center Nonrural 0.000 0.000 0.000** 0.000 0.026 0.159 Rural 0.000 0.000 0.111 0.333 0.031 0.172 Primary care providers per 10 000 residents Nonrural 13.129 5.655 12.924 4.447 11.862 6.200 Rural 7.645 3.166 10.897 10.782 7.193 5.073 Community characteristics Reservation land distance, miles Nonrural 110.620 77.401 78.287 68.383 90.789 67.575 Rural 135.980 44.404 114.626 50.970 122.155 69.937 Multicounty jurisdiction Nonrural 0.000 0.000 0.088 0.288 0.121 0.327 Rural 0.333*** 0.516 0.333*** 0.500 0.061 0.240 Demographic characteristics AI/AN population, % Nonrural 0.987** 0.392 1.241 0.981 1.265 1.089 Rural 1.244*** 0.189 1.553** 0.525 2.594 6.378 Population over 65 y, % Nonrural 16.051 3.976 17.559 5.739 16.597 3.897 Rural 18.443** 1.505 18.479* 3.002 20.334 3.866 Income per capita, $ Nonrural 49 727* 5 679 51 993 10 573 52 757 15 784 Rural 37 141* 6 601 40 568 8 392 42 502 16 324 Poverty, % Nonrural 12.310 2.817 12.286 3.633 11.920 4.128 Rural 17.483 5.481 16.158 5.031 14.809 5.424 Uninsured, % Nonrural 7.200 2.343 8.521 4.600 7.440 3.625 Rural 6.603* 0.960 7.292 1.503 7.474 3.011 College degree, % Nonrural 20.463 6.143 20.680 5.130 20.446 7.409 Rural 10.803 2.773 12.079 4.004 12.915 5.108 Unemployment, % Nonrural 1.934 0.397 1.907 0.345 1.907 0.490 Rural 1.827 0.186 1.918 0.238 1.917 0.435 Observations Nonrural 15 34 388 Rural 6 9 262 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: AI/AN, American Indian and Alaskan Native; NALSYS, National Longitudinal Survey of Public Health Systems; SD, standard deviation; STD RES, standardized residual. a Statistical significance is reported at * P < .10, ** P < .05, *** P < .01 that compares exceptional observations with conventional observations. Significance determined by t tests for continuous variables or chi-square tests for indicator variables. Statistical significance is not calculated for negatively exceptional observations due to small sample size (n = 3). TABLE 3. Public Health and Network Characteristics of Local Community Health Systems With Exceptional and Conventional Tribal Organization Participation, NALSYS, 2018 a . Exceptional (>3 STD RES) Exceptional (>1.5 STD RES) Conventional (> −1 and <1 STD RES) Mean SD Mean SD Mean SD Public health characteristics Board of health indicator, % Nonrural 0.714 (n = 14) 0.469 0.656 (n = 32) 0.483 0.674 (n = 365) 0.469 Rural 0.750 (n = 4) 0.500 0.714 (n = 7) 0.488 0.784 (n = 241) 0.412 LHD revenue (per capita) Nonrural 35.851* (n = 12) 11.030 37.823 (n = 27) 22.674 44.132 (n = 285) 45.212 Rural 79.465 (n = 4) 62.136 70.623 (n = 7) 51.776 72.046 (n = 163) 87.181 LHD employees (per 10 000 residents) Nonrural 3.716** (n = 14) 1.475 4.083 (n = 31) 2.434 4.818 (n = 358) 4.322 Rural 8.176 (n = 4) 4.851 8.791 (n = 7) 4.319 8.763 (n = 236) 8.510 Network characteristics Social capital index Nonrural −0.514 0.619 −0.585 0.656 −0.490 0.631 Rural −0.605* 0.919 −0.249 1.037 0.156 1.209 Local community health network density, % Nonrural 54.704*** 21.638 54.169*** 26.551 31.936 22.463 Rural 60.181*** 22.494 54.749*** 20.036 20.011 18.102 Local community health network betweenness Nonrural 23.438 50.141 17.174 34.847 19.916 27.890 Rural 3.899*** 5.606 14.394 32.201 16.752 24.318 Essential public health activities'' effectiveness indicator, % Nonrural 0.867** 0.352 0.794** 0.410 0.595 0.491 Rural 0.667 0.516 0.667 0.500 0.431 0.496 Essential public health activities'' equity indicator, % Nonrural 0.733** 0.458 0.765*** 0.431 0.477 0.500 Rural 0.667* 0.516 0.667** 0.500 0.313 0.465 Observations Nonrural 15 34 388 Rural 6 9 262 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: LHD, local health department; NALSYS, National Longitudinal Survey of Public Health Systems; SD, standard deviation; STD RES, standardized residual. a Statistical significance is reported at * P < .10, ** P < .05, *** P < .01 that compares exceptional observations with conventional observations. Significance determined by t tests for continuous variables or chi-square tests for indicator variables. Statistical significance is not calculated for negatively exceptional observations due to small sample size (n = 3). Results Of 728 local public health systems included in the 2018 NALSYS survey, 21 were identified as local public health systems with exceptional tribal organization participation and 650 were identified as having conventional tribal organization participation (Tables 2 and 3 ). Significant predictors of tribal organization participation were similar to results from our original study: having an AI/AN population size over 1%, having a tribal health facility with IHS funding in the region, and being near a reservation, increased the likelihood of participation (Table 1 ). Being in a rural jurisdiction decreased the likelihood of participation. Ranking observations by standardized residuals did not reveal any natural breaks or segmentation to identify exceptional participation (see Supplemental Digital Content Table 2, available at http://links.lww.com/JPHMP/B246 ); therefore, varying thresholds were used as strict cutoffs. The least strict threshold of 1.5 standardized residuals identified at most 43 observations with exceptional participation in our sample (Tables 2 and 3 ). Regardless of threshold, only 3 observations were classified as having negatively exceptional participation because they were predicted to have tribal organization participation but did not. They were removed from results to protect anonymity. Local public health system network density and self-ratings of effectiveness and equity goals of public health activities were found to have large and significant differences between exceptional and conventional participation (Table 3 ). In nonrural public health jurisdictions, public health system network density was on average 22% greater in observations with exceptional participation. The difference was even larger in rural observations with exceptional participation, which had network densities over 30% greater, although there were fewer rural observations overall with exceptional participation (Table 3 ). Effectiveness and equity indicators were found to be highly correlated with each other as well as network density (see Supplemental Digital Content Table 3, available at http://links.lww.com/JPHMP/B246 ). In nonrural public health jurisdictions, roughly 30% more observations with exceptional participation self-rated as meeting about half or more of overall community need and half or more of the effort contributing to health disparities (Table 3 ). In rural public health jurisdictions, observations with exceptional participation were found to similarly have a much higher self-rating of equity than observations with conventional participation. The difference in effectiveness was minimal, though as rural observations with conventional participation also had high self-ratings in effectiveness. While statistically significant differences were found in several other variables between observations with exceptional and conventional participation, when taking the size of difference and comparison of variance into consideration, no other variable differences appeared meaningful (Tables 2 and 3 ). Focusing on public health system network density more closely, it was found on average in both nonrural and rural observations with exceptional participation that tribal organizations were connected to a majority or all sectors by participating in at least one public health activity, reflecting a high diversity in partnership and high network density (Figure). Tribal organizations in both nonrural and rural local public health systems with exceptional participation were most connected with local health departments, local government agencies, hospitals, community health centers, other nonprofits, physician groups, K-12 schools, and state health departments. FIGURE. Open in a new tab Average Local Public Health Networks of Participating Sectors, NALSYS, 2018 a a An “exceptional” local public health network is identified by being greater than 1.5 standardized residuals away from the predicted value of tribal organization participation. A “conventional” local public health network is identified as being within 1 to −1 standardized residual from the predicted value of tribal organization participation. Edge size is calculated by the average across all observations of the proportion of 19 essential public health activities in which one node (sector) participated along with a connecting node (sector). Government sector: LHD (local health department), SHD (state health department), LGA (other local government agency), OSA (other state government agency), federal (federal agency); medical sector: hospital, CHC (community health center), physicians (independent primary care); private sector: employer, insurance (private); nonprofit sector: university, K-12 (primary to high school), faith-based, ONP (other nonprofit); tribal sector: tribal government or tribal-serving organization. This figure is available in color online ( www.JPHMP.com ). In contrast, local public health systems with conventional participation in either nonrural or rural public health jurisdictions did not have any participation of tribal organizations on average (Figure). They both also lacked participation from sectors in economic development services, child and family support services, parks/recreation/physical activity programs, corrections/criminal justice/juvenile justice services, and environmental protection programs. Rural public health systems with conventional participation additionally lacked participation from federal government agencies and health insurers on average. Discussion Despite significant predictors of having a smaller AI/AN population size at or below 1%, having fewer AI/AN-serving health facilities, and being located more than 80 miles away from the nearest reservation, 21 local public health systems were found to have exceptional tribal organization participation in rural and nonrural areas. Having a high network density and prioritizing equity distinguished these local public health systems. Our results are in line with limited research that used positive deviance analysis of local public health systems to identify that they place a high value on partnerships and cross-sector collaboration in areas with exceptional outcomes. 16 , 34 , 35 A 2016 study found that public health jurisdictions with exceptionally good maternal-child health outcomes had local health departments that placed a primary focus on partnerships to outsource services for at-risk populations. 16 It is notable that these local health departments chose to outsource services out of financial necessity when their budgets were cut, which may explain why public health partnerships are still possible regardless of differences in local health department revenue or employee support. Another study that focused on improving equity in community health outcomes identified working partnerships with community stakeholders as a key step. 34 Even if partnership is valued in local public health systems, limitations have been recognized in the potential for partnership in rural areas. Our study found that characteristics related to exceptional tribal organization participation were fundamentally different for rural and nonrural local public health jurisdictions. A 2020 study found that rural counties with exceptional prevention of premature deaths credited their success to collaboration among health organizations. 35 However, these organizations did not exist in other rural counties with nonexceptional outcomes. Underlying resource differences between rural and urban public health systems have been growing since the Great Recession. 36 After that time period, the number of nonrural systems implementing more essential public health activities and having higher network density is also growing compared with rural systems. 37 The survey we used to understand the network of public health systems did not verify whether all potential sectors for public health participation existed in every jurisdiction. As this may be a distinct challenge for AI/AN populations, in which 40% were found to reside in rural areas in the 2020 US Census, proposed solutions to build consistent rural public health support at the national and state levels may benefit AI/AN communities as well. 1 , 36 Our study found that state health departments and nonprofits, for example, were commonly part of rural local public health systems that did not have tribal organization participation. Sectors such as these may help draw state or regional tribal partnership into rural areas where AI/AN communities reside. While local health departments are often the focus for developing multisector public health partnerships to address population health disparities, our study indicates that network density, or the formation of relationships across all participating organizations, significantly impacts participation of tribal organizations. In local public health systems with exceptional tribal organization participation, tribal organizations were well integrated in mutual participation with most sectors. “Triadic closure,” or the cascade effect of connecting to one well-connected organization that inherently leads to connecting with the organizations'' other relationships, is well documented in network theory. 38 While studies examining growth of public health partnerships have found that increasing the total number of partnerships has an inverse relationship with network density due to unmanageable workload, 23 , 39 our study found a positive relationship between diversity of partnerships and network density. This was also related to valuing equity in public health activities. In considering both equity and multisector partnership, it may be more manageable for local public health systems to focus on diversity of partnership rather than quantity, without requiring local health departments to be the only initiator of public health partnerships. Limitations The use of secondary data sources and their associated data collection methods are a source of several limitations within the present study. NALSYS, a survey used to identify public health participation in essential public health activities, only surveys local health departments and is limited by their perception of the local public health system. A 58% response rate is high but may still introduce nonresponse bias, such as local public health jurisdictions with larger AI/AN population sizes that may not have responded, as the survey captured a total of 43% of the 2020 US AI/AN population living outside of tribal statistical areas. Local public health system network measures, equity, and effectiveness indicators were self-reported by the local health department, which may result in correlation due to biased reporting. However, network betweenness was also based on self-reported measures by local health departments and was not correlated with any of the other self-reported indicators or network density (see Supplemental Digital Content Table 3, available at http://links.lww.com/JPHMP/B246 ). Surveying tribal health authorities would improve the results of this study. NALSYS is a validated instrument, 40 but the addition of tribal organizations as a sector was not included in the validation process. “Tribal organization” could be interpreted as representing tribal governments or AI/AN-serving organizations, and it is unclear how local public health departments treated this new sector. This study is cross-sectional and descriptive in nature. Its results should therefore not be extrapolated to assume causality between public health and network measures and exceptional participation. Implications for Policy & Practice If trying to invite tribal organizations into local public health networks, it may be easier, and just as effective, to look for and connect with already existing local partnerships with tribal organizations, even with nontraditional health sectors, such as K-12 schools or any local government agency. For rural local public health jurisdictions with a limited quantity of potential partners, state or regional partners, such as state health departments that are common participants in rural local public health systems, or tribal epidemiology centers that are established regional partners with state health departments, could be necessary to develop tribal partnership where local AI/AN communities reside. State, local, and organizational policies that prioritize equity in public health may help prioritize development of tribal organization partnerships. Conclusion Our study found that local public health systems with exceptional participation of tribal organizations had a high network density among diverse sectors, in both rural and nonrural jurisdictions. They were also found to place a greater emphasis on equity in public health activities. Results from this study would be improved upon through qualitative research to follow up with local public health systems with exceptional participation, especially in rural areas, to better understand how partnerships with tribal organizations developed and what kinds of partnerships are occurring. Supplementary Material jpump-30-274-s001.docx (54KB, docx) Open in a new tab Footnotes This research was supported by a grant from the Robert Wood Johnson Foundation for the Systems for Action Research Program (grant #78892). The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Supplemental digital content is available for this article. Direct URL citations appear in the printed text and are provided in the HTML and PDF versions of this article on the journal''s Web site ( http://www.JPHMP.com ). Contributor Information Kyla L. Bauer, Email: kyla.2.bauer@cuanschutz.edu. Amelia L. Mitchell, Email: amelia.mitchell@cuanschutz.edu. Glen P. Mays, Email: glen.mays@cuanschutz.edu. References 1. Office of Minority Health. Profile: American Indian/Alaska Native. https://minorityhealth.hhs.gov/omh/browse.aspx?lvl=3&lvlid=62 . Published 2023. Accessed August 11, 2023. 2. Kruse G, Lopez-Carmen VA, Jensen A, Hardie L, Sequist TD. The Indian Health Service and American Indian/Alaska Native health outcomes. Annu Rev Public Health. 2022;43:559–576. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Groom A, Espey D, Allison A, Thomas C. CDC partnerships with tribal epidemiology centers to improve the health of American Indian and Alaska Native communities. J Public Health Manag Pract. 2019;25(suppl 5, Tribal Epidemiology Centers: Advancing Public Health in Indian Country for Over 20 Years):S5–S6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. National Indian Health Board. Public Health in Indian Country Capacity Scan Report, 2019. Washington, DC: National Indian Health Board; 2020. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Glasgow L, Clayton M, Honeycutt A, et al. Key insights on multisector community partnerships from real-world efforts to address social determinants of health. Eval Program Plann. 2023;99:102298. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Mays GP, Mamaril CB, Timsina LR. Preventable death rates fell where communities expanded populati