INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7444,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10184820','Comparison of Different Rehabilitation Techniques of Traditional Chinese and Western Medicine in the Treatment of Motor Dysfunction After Stroke Based on Frequency Method: A Network Meta-analysis.','Li RY, Chen KY, Wang XR, Yu Q, Xu L.','2022','wanfang taichi mdas troubled mdas mdas countless sequela conforms mdas mdas baduanjin taichi nmes mdas arat mdas mdas crd42022320020 conforms b872 wanfang baduanjin yijinjing b873 taichi nmes arat corr corr lakeway arat inconformity ifplot inconformity wanfang taichi nmes b874 b875 arat abscission arat arat arat nmes taichi taichi taichi nmes nmes nmes nmes arat arat nmes nmes nmes nmes arat taichi nmes nmes nmes arat nmes mdas symmetrically brunnstrom taichi mdas hemiplegic arat arat arat grasping kneading arat entertaining brunnstrom mdas mdas mdas mdas mdas mdas arat mdas ajpmr 111kb ryl ryl 2016z001 2021yfs0132 2020yfs0415 ajpmr ruo alis7718 cky19940916 yqswc11 xuli_0125 boumrah garbaya radgui tellez bastani jaberzadeh anodal chaimani mavridis e76654 zhuo mongol taijiquan tongdu tiaosheng hemiplegic shaanxi qiqihar instrum mohd nordin allman amadi ipsilesional anodal 330re1 arya pandian galvao borba borba ssy stimul chieffo prezzo houdayer jiu colomer noe llorens e27504 moslemi haghighi kordi yoosefinejad razeghi ikbali afsar mirzayev umit yemisci muthalib contralesional thia kuzgun ozgen armagan beyin damar hastaliklar dergisi jiu e01729 apoplectic mekbib debeli michielsen selles geest ogun kurul yasar neuropsiquiatr pagilla xbox polonio corregidor ictus sallustio xbox alisar ozen sozay bihemispheric jiu jiu jiu jiu marinacci horande electromyogram hever skilbeck champan durango mudanjiang matijevic secic masic croat cyberpsychol ajpmr 111kb','Objective The aim of the study is to evaluate the effect of different traditional Chinese and western medicine rehabilitation techniques on motor dysfunction after stroke using a network meta-analysis. Methods CNKI, Wanfang, PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane databases were searched from inception to September 2022. We independently searched and screened randomized controlled trials of rehabilitation techniques for poststroke motor dysfunction treatment, evaluated the quality, and analyzed the data using Stata 14.0. Results Seventy-four randomized controlled trials involving nine rehabilitation techniques and 5128 patients were included. The results of network meta-analysis showed the following orders regarding improvement of the total scores of Fugl-Meyer Assessment, Action Research Arm Test, and Berg Balance Scale: biofeedback therapy > mirror therapy > repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation > acupuncture therapy > transcranial direct current stimulation > Taichi > common therapy, virtual reality > transcranial direct current stimulation > repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation > mirror therapy > common therapy, and acupuncture therapy > virtual reality > neuromuscular electrical stimulation > mirror therapy > common therapy > transcranial direct current stimulation, respectively. Conclusions Biofeedback therapy had the best comprehensive effect, while virtual reality was the best intervention for improving the index of action research arm test and Fugl-Meyer Assessment–lower extremity. Acupuncture therapy improved lower limb balance function. Key Words: Rehabilitation Techniques, Stroke, Network Meta-analysis What Is Known As one of the most common sequelae after stroke, motor dysfunction after stroke (MDAS) has troubled many stroke patients. Therefore, rehabilitation technology, as a complementary treatment of conventional rehabilitation therapy, is gradually favored by the clinical field. However, the advantages and disadvantages of different technologies are rarely mentioned. What Is New The findings of the network meta-analysis provide evidence support that rehabilitation technology is beneficial to patients with MDAS. The results suggest that BT had the best comprehensive effect, while VR was the best intervention for improving Action Research Arm Test and Fugl Meyer Assessment–lower extremity, and acupuncture therapy has the greatest advantage in improving lower limb balance function. The research results may provide some guidance for rehabilitation doctors in the treatment of MDAS patients. Stroke is one of the most common diseases in the world, 1 causing sequelae and affecting the family life of countless patients. Dysfunction after stroke includes motor, cognitive, and swallowing dysfunction. Motor dysfunction is the most common sequela of stroke. Undoubtedly, motor dysfunction after stroke seriously affects the quality of life of patients. Reducing patients’ dysfunction, improving their quality of life, and making them return to their families and even to society are issues that should be addressed at the early stage of rehabilitation medicine, which also conforms to the function-oriented research direction of contemporary rehabilitation medicine. With the deepening of clinical research, the excellent efficacy of traditional Chinese medicine and western medicine in the treatment of motor dysfunction after stroke (MDAS) has been widely recognized. To date, some degree of consensus on rehabilitation therapy for MDAS at home and abroad, which is mainly divided into physical exercise and occupational therapies, has been established. In addition, in recent years, many emerging rehabilitation therapies have been developed in modern medicine, such as acupuncture therapy (AT), exercise therapy (eg, Baduanjin and Taichi), virtual reality (VR), mirror therapy (MT), biofeedback therapy (BT), neuromuscular electrical stimulation (NMES), repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS), and transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS). Several clinical studies and meta-analyses 2 – 6 have confirmed that the application of these rehabilitation therapies can significantly promote the recovery of MDAS, but most relevant trials involve only the clinical treatment research of a single rehabilitation therapy, and the advantages and disadvantages of different therapies are rarely mentioned. Therefore, we conducted this meta-analysis and adopted the Fugl-Meyer Assessment (FMA) score, Berg Balance Scale (BBS) score, and Action Research Arm Test (ARAT) score as evaluation indicators and ranked their related efficacy based on the surface under the cumulative ranking (SUCRA) to comprehensively study the effectiveness of various physical adjuvant therapies in treating MDAS. This provides strong evidence for the clinical efficacy of physical adjuvant therapy in treating MDAS. MATERIALS AND METHODS Registration This meta-analysis is registered on international prospective register of systematic reviews (registration number CRD42022320020). This study conforms to all Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses guidelines and reports the required information accordingly (see Supplementary Checklist, Supplemental Digital Content 1, http://links.lww.com/PHM/B872 ). Literature Search The retrieval strategy adopted a combination of subject and free words. Retrieval databases used were PubMed, Embase, Cochrane Library, CNKI, and WANFANG. The retrieval time ranged from the establishment of the database to September 2022. Search terms include the following: clinical, motor function, stroke, acupuncture, mirror therapy, tDCS, Baduanjin, Yijinjing, Tai Chi, etc. See the Supplementary Document (Appendix, Supplemental Digital Content 2, http://links.lww.com/PHM/B873 ) for detailed retrieval strategy. Study Selection The inclusion criterion was randomized controlled trials. The study participants were patients who were clinically diagnosed with stroke, met the stroke diagnostic criteria of relevant societies, or were identified as having stroke by imaging evidence. Regarding treatment plan, the control group was treated with common therapy (CT) (including routine physical therapy and routine occupational therapy) or single rehabilitation therapy, and the treatment group was treated with rehabilitation therapies based on routine rehabilitation training, such as AT, Taichi, VR, MT, BT, NMES, rTMS, and tDCS. Outcome measures included FMA and its upper extremity motor function scale (FMA-UE), lower extremity motor function (FMA-LE), BBS, and ARAT scores. Study Exclusion Studies that met the following criteria were excluded: literature on nonclinical patients, studies with no FMA-related outcome indicators, conference papers and master’s and doctoral theses, and repeated published literature. Data Extraction Two staff members conducted an independent evaluation and cross-comparison of the included studies. The different opinions were assessed by a third investigator. The basic information of the extracted literature included the study’s name, name and year of publication of the first author, various sample sizes, patient ages, intervention measures of each group, random grouping method, blind method, and outcome data. All outcome data were included in the difference between before and after treatment (ie, the difference between indicators after and before treatment). If the original text is not calculated, we will calculate it. The formula is as follows, where corr is usually 0.5: SD E change = SD 2 E baseline + SD 2 E final − 2 × Corr × SD E baseline × SD E final Mean E change = Mean E final − Mean E baseline Statistical Analysis STATA 14.0 (Stata Corporation, Lakeway, TX) was used for network meta-analysis. In this study, the outcome indicators (FMA, FMA-UE, FMA-LE, BBS, and ARAT scores) were all measured with a unified standard and were continuous variables; hence, mean difference (MD) and 95% confidence interval (CI) were used as the effect quantity indicators. Stata was used to draw a network diagram of different outcome indicators. The dots in the evidence network diagram represented an intervention, the size of the dots represented the number of samples finally included in the intervention, lines between the two points represented a direct comparison between the two interventions, and the thickness of the lines represented the number of randomized controlled trials using two interventions simultaneously. The node analysis was used for inconsistency test. If P > 0.05, it indicated that there was no overall inconsistency, 7 and the consistency model was used for analysis. To evaluate the local inconsistency, this study calculated the inconformity factors and 95% CI of each closed loop in the network. This calculation method used the ifplot command in Stata to detect loop inconformity. If the lower limit between 95% confidence regions contained or was close to 0, it indicated that the local comparison, that is, the direct comparison evidence, was very consistent with the indirect comparison evidence. Network meta-analysis was performed using Stata, with both direct and indirect estimates. It provided a simultaneous comparison of multiple treatments even if a head-to-head comparison between the two treatment arms was unavailable. The ranking index was reflected by the SUCRA, where 0 ≤ SUCRA ≤100%; 100% represented the most effective treatment, and 0 represented the worst and ineffective treatment. Similarly, the larger the area under the curve of the SUCRA chart, the better the effect of the intervention. Methodologic Quality The quality of the included studies was evaluated according to the quality evaluation scale recommended in the Cochrane Handbook 5.1.0, including six parts: reporting, detection, performance, selection, loss to follow-up, and other biases. The evaluation content of each part can be judged as high, medium, or low risk according to the standard. RESULTS Characteristics of the Included Trials A total of 4153 relevant studies were obtained in the preliminary search, including 385 from CNKI, 770 from Wanfang, 493 from PubMed, 1081 from EMBASE, and 1424 from the Cochrane Library. After exclusion of duplicate studies and strictly adherence to the inclusion and exclusion criteria, 74 randomized controlled trials 8 – 81 were included. Except for a three-arm experiment, 52 all double-arm experiments with a total of 5128 patients. Nine rehabilitation treatment schemes were involved, namely, CT, AT, rTMS, Taichi, tDCS, BT, NMES, MT, and VR. The document screening process and results are shown in Figure 1 A, and the basic information of the included documents is shown in Supplemental Digital Content 3 and 4 ( http://links.lww.com/PHM/B874 , http://links.lww.com/PHM/B875 , basic characteristics of trials included). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab A, Flow chart of literature screening. B, Risk of bias graph (Ba: risk of bias summary; Bb: risk of bias graph). C, Network diagram (Ca: FMA; Cb: FMA-UE; Cc: FMA-LE; Cd: BBS; Ce: ARAT). Risk of Bias The included studies were evaluated using the Cochrane Manual 5.1.0 Bias Risk Assessment Tool. All the included studies of bias risk assessment mentioned random allocation, of which 43 studies described the allocation method, 31 studies mentioned the allocation concealment, 33 studies mentioned the single- or double-blind method between participants and subjects, one study 35 mentioned that the blind method was not used between participants and subjects, 27 studies described the blind method when evaluating data, and 27 studies defined the quantity of abscission. There was a difference in the number of cases before and after. 74 The remainder of the literature has not been clearly described. The risk assessment of bias included in the literature is shown in Figure 1 B. Evidence Network A total of 20 studies reported FMA, involving seven rehabilitation therapies. A total of 48 rehabilitation protocols involving FMA-UE were reported. A total of 24 studies reported FMA-LE, involving seven rehabilitation therapies. Eight studies reported BBS, involving six rehabilitation therapies. A total of seven studies reported ARAT, involving five therapies. The line between the two points represented the evidence of a direct comparison between the two therapies. If there was no connection, this indicated that there was no direct comparison, and the results could be obtained through indirect comparison. The thickness of the line indicated the number of studies using the two rehabilitation therapies in all included studies, and the size of the dots indicated the sample size of the included cases (Fig. 1 C). Inconsistency Test Seven rehabilitation therapies using FMA formed a closed loop. The overall consistency result was P = 0.7639, suggesting that there was no overall inconsistency. The results showed that the inconsistency factor was 6.13. After treatment, the eight rehabilitation treatment schemes with FMA-UE formed two closed loops. The overall consistency results showed P = 0.3320, suggesting that there was no overall inconsistency. The results showed that the inconsistency factors were 12.07 and 3.18, respectively. After treatment, the seven rehabilitation therapies with FMA-LE formed a closed loop. The overall consistency result was P = 0.7516, suggesting no overall inconsistency. The results showed an inconsistency factor of 1.68. The 95% CI lower limit of all indicators was 0, indicating that the consistency of each closed loop was good, that is, the direct and indirect comparisons have little difference in the impact of the whole mesh meta-analysis results, and the statistical results of mesh meta-analysis have high reliability. The other two indicators (BBS and ARAT) did not form a closed loop; therefore, there was no need to conduct a closed-loop inconsistency test (Fig. 2 A). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab A, Inconsistency (Aa: FMA; Ab: FMA-UE; Ac: FMA-LE). B, Cumulative probability ranking plot (Ba: FMA; Bb: FMA-UE; Bc: FMA-LE; Bd: BBS; Be: ARAT). C, Funnel diagram of FMA-UE (A: CT; B: AT; C: VR; D: MT; E: tDCS; F: BT; G: rTMS; H: NMES). Network Meta-analysis Fugl-Meyer Assessment Seven rehabilitation therapies were compared directly and indirectly, and five were found to be statistically different. Compared with the impact on FMA with CT, AT (MD = 8.67, 95% CI = 5.65 to 11.70), rTMS (MD = 10.07, 95% CI = 3.45 to 16.69), BT (MD = 18.45, 95% CI = 8.92 to 27.97), and MT (MD = 12.00, 95% CI = 1.00 to 23.00) could significantly increase FMA. Compared with BT, Taichi (MD = −16.58, 95% CI = −30.41 to −2.75) improved the total score of FMA slightly less than BT, and there was no statistical difference in other comparisons (Table 1 ). TABLE 1. Network meta-analysis of FMA (MD [95% CI]) Treatment MT tDCS Taichi BT rTMS AT CT MT — tDCS 5.09 (−10.13 to 20.31) — Taichi 10.13 (−4.76 to 25.02) 5.04 (−9.50 to 19.58) — BT −6.45 (−21.00 to 8.11) −11.54 (−25.73 to 2.66) −16.58 (−30.41 to −2.75) a — rTMS 1.93 (−10.91 to 14.77) −3.16 (−15.60 to 9.28) −8.20 (−20.22 to 3.82) 8.38 (−3.23 to 19.98) — AT 3.33 (−8.08 to 14.74) −1.76 (−12.71 to 9.19) −6.80 (−17.28 to 3.67) 9.77 (−0.22 to 19.77) 1.40 (−5.77 to 8.57) — CT 12.00 (1.00 to 23.00) a 6.91 (−3.61 to 17.43) 1.87 (−8.16 to 11.90) 18.45 (8.92 to 27.97) a 10.07 (3.45 to 16.69) a 8.67 (5.65 to 11.70) a — Open in a new tab a The values between the 2 comparisons are statistically significant. Fugl-Meyer Assessment–Upper Extremity Eight therapies were compared directly and indirectly, and four comparisons showed statistically significant differences. Compared with CT, AT (MD = 5.96, 95% CI = 3.64 to 8.28), VR (MD = 5.25, 95% CI = 1.89 to 8.62), and BT (MD = 9.45, 95% CI = 4.04 to 14.85) could significantly increase FMA-UE. Compared with BT, improvement in FMA-UE by rTMS (MD = −7.31, 95% CI = −14.46 to −0.16) was slightly worse, and there was no significant difference in other comparisons (Table 2 ). TABLE 2. Network meta-analysis of FMA-UE and FMA-LE (MD [95% CI]) Network Meta-analysis of FMA-UE Network Meta-analysis of FMA-LE Treatment NMES rTMS BT tDCS MT VR AT CT Treatment NMES rTMS tDCS VR MT AT CT NMES — NMES — rTMS 