 CI = −19.75 to −3.52) improved BBS significantly less than did VR. No significant differences were observed in the other comparisons (Table 3 ). TABLE 3. Network meta-analysis of BBS and ARAT (MD [95% CI]) Network Meta-analysis of BBS Network Meta-analysis of ARAT Treatment NMES MT tDCS VR AT CT Treatment MT VR NMES tDCS CT NMES — MT — MT 2.12 (−3.57 to 7.81) — VR −4.07 (−16.13 to 7.98) — tDCS 10.42 (−8.83 to 29.66) 8.30 (−10.09 to 26.68) — NMES −0.86 (−14.24 to 12.52) 3.21 (−4.24 to 10.67) — VR −1.22 (−19.91 to 17.48) −3.34 (−21.15 to 14.47) −11.63 (−19.75 to −3.52) a — tDCS −3.83 (−16.79 to 9.14) 0.25 (−6.43 to 6.92) −2.97 (−11.82 to 5.89) — AT −1.74 (−20.18 to 16.69) −3.86 (−21.40 to 13.67) −12.16 (−19.66 to −4.66) a −0.53 (−6.47 to 5.42) — CT 1.20 (−10.39 to 12.79) 5.27 (1.96 to 8.58) a 2.06 (−4.62 to 8.74) 5.03 (−0.79 to 10.84) — CT 7.92 (−10.17 to 26.00) 5.80 (−11.37 to 22.96) −2.50 (−9.09 to 4.09) 9.13 (4.40 to 13.87) a 9.66 (6.08 to 13.23) a — Open in a new tab a The values between the 2 comparisons are statistically significant. Action Research Arm Test Five rehabilitation therapies were compared directly and indirectly, and one comparison showed a statistical difference. Compared with CT, VR (MD = 5.27, 95% CI = 1.96 to 8.58) significantly increased ARAT, and there was no statistical difference in other comparisons (Table 3 ). Surface Under the Cumulative Ranking According to the results of SUCRA, BT may be the most effective intervention to increase FMA (BT [93.9%] > MT [69.2%] > rTMS [62%] > AT [52.9%] > tDCS [44.4%] > Taichi [19.8%] > CT [7.8%]), BT may be the most effective intervention to increase FMA-UE (BT [91.9%] > AT [70.1%] > VR [61.4%] > NMES [59.8%] > tDCS [46.1%] > MT [36.5%] > rTMS [28.5%] > CT [5.7%]), VR may be the most effective intervention to increase FMA-LE (VR [84.8.7%] > NMES [80.5%] > AT [60.6%] > MT [58.5%] > rTMS [28.8%] > tDCS [21.9%] > CT [14.9%]), AT may be the most effective intervention to increase BBS (AT [76%] > VR [72.2%] > NMES [66.3%] > MT [50%] > CT [24.2%] > tDCS [11.3%]), and VR may be the most effective intervention to increase ARAT (VR [76.8%] > tDCS [72%] > NMES [43.6%] > MT [39.2%] > CT [18.3%]; Fig. 2 B). Publication Bias In this study, the FMA-UE outcome index of the MDAS involved eight different rehabilitation therapies. The dots of different colors in the funnel chart represented the direct comparison between two different therapies, and the number of dots represented the number of studies. Most of the dots in the funnel map of this study were symmetrically distributed on the vertical line and its two sides, which were symmetrical on both sides; however, there may still be a certain degree of publication bias, and 12 control studies were distributed outside the 95% CI in the funnel diagram, suggesting the existence of a small sample effect. There were few experiments of pairwise direct comparison between other indexes; hence, bias analysis was not conducted (Fig. 2 C). DISCUSSION In recent years, bed rehabilitation therapies after stroke have been gradually standardized, and an increasing number of emerging physical technologies have been applied to clinical rehabilitation. 82 Based on the analysis method of network meta-analysis, this study summarized the comparative evaluation of the intervention effects of eight conventional and emerging rehabilitation therapies according to the direct and indirect comparisons between the motor function indices of the upper and lower limbs, combined with the principle of frequency method. Fugl-Meyer Assessment The FMA is a clinical evaluation method that is mainly aimed at determining the degree of poststroke dysfunction. It is a limb function evaluation scale developed by Fugl Meyer, according to Brunnstrom’s six-stage theory. The FMA is widely used as an outcome index for the recovery of motor function in patients with stroke. The main limitation of this method is the ceiling effect in patients with stroke with mild hemiplegia. According to the results of the SUCRA, BT was the best intervention technique for improving the total FMA score. Biofeedback therapy, as a modern physiological scientific instrument, selectively converts the normal or abnormal physiological activity information in the human body into recognizable visual or auditory signals; thus, after a series of intensive training and treatment, it is a new therapy that can consciously self-regulate and control this physiological or pathological information in the body, regulate physiological function, eliminate pathological state, and restore physical and mental health. 83 It can record and display the muscle potential signal through electronic instruments so that patients can intuitively observe the changes in muscle potential closely related to their diseases, control these physiological changes with their subjective consciousness through intensive training, reduce or increase the corresponding muscle tension, and relax or strengthen muscle contraction. Therefore, it can be used in the clinical treatment of some patients with muscle spasms or paralysis. 84 Although the results of the forest map suggest that except for Taichi therapy, rehabilitation therapies have no significant statistical significance, they still have a certain clinical guiding significance. Studies by Zhao et al. 85 and Li et al. 86 have shown that after BT intervention, motor function of the upper and lower limbs of patients with poststroke hemiplegia significantly improved. However, as a relatively new rehabilitation technology, BT has limited clinical research. There were only two studies involving the total FMA score in the treatment of MDAS with BT in this study. More high-quality and large sample size studies are needed to support these results. Upper Limbs The results of this study suggest that BT is the best rehabilitation therapy for improving the FMA-UE score. The FMA-UE includes 33 items related to proximal and distal movements of the upper limbs. The FMA-UE mainly evaluates reflex activity, motor control, and muscle strength of the upper limb on the hemiplegic side. The meta-analysis results of Wang et al. 87 suggested that BT is helpful for the functional recovery of the upper limb nervous system in poststroke patients and accelerates their rehabilitation process, which is consistent with the results of this study. The ARAT is a standard scale for measuring upper limb and hand function. The ARAT explains and evaluates upper limb movements in daily life based on four basic movements. Compared with the movement requirements of FMA-UE, those of ARAT are more complex, including grasping, kneading, and gross movement under the shoulder and elbow flexion and extension. Therefore, combination evaluation with FMA-UE can compensate for the ceiling effect of FMA in patients with mild hemiplegia. In this study, the secondary index for evaluating upper limb motor function was the ARAT. The results of the SUCRA suggested that VR was the best intervention for improving this index. The VR game system creates a simulation model to make participants feel that the environment is real and allows them to interact with the dynamic environment created by a computer. 88 By creating a stimulating an entertaining environment and ensuring patient interest, these systems support motor recovery by activating new motor projection areas and resting synapses. Sensory stimuli at different levels, such as motor activities and cognitive planning of movement, lead to permanent cellular, anatomical, physiological, and behavioral remodeling of the central nervous system. 89 It has been clinically confirmed that the Brunnstrom stage of patients with stroke after VR technology intervention are significantly higher than those in the CT group. 49 Combined with the results of the FMA-UE, the main index for evaluating upper limb motor function in this study (VR was the third-best intervention in terms of improvement in FMA-UE), VR had great advantages in improving upper limb motor function, especially fine motor function. Lower Limbs The FMA-LE is the most commonly used index for evaluating the motor function of the lower limbs. The scale has 17 items and is scored using a three-level scale. The higher the score, the better the motor function of the lower limb. The SUCRA results in this study suggested that VR was the best intervention method for improving FMA-LE indicators. Some studies have shown that VR is better than traditional rehabilitation methods for providing the exercise intensity required to induce neural plasticity. The intervention measures provided by VR can significantly improve motor function, balance, gait, and depression. 69 , 90 – 92 The BBS was first reported by Berg. 93 The BBS focuses more on the evaluation of the balance function of the lower limbs than the FMA-LE. The lower the score, the more serious the balance dysfunction. Therefore, the BBS score is also one of the most commonly used evaluation indices of lower limb function. In this study, AT was an intervention that improved BBS score. As one of the representative rehabilitation technologies of traditional Chinese medicine, its efficacy has been confirmed in many clinical studies. Its unique advantage is that it can select different acupoints and meridians for syndrome differentiation and treatment according to the limb function characteristics of patients with stroke. Some scholars evaluated the impact of interactive dynamic scalp acupuncture on lower limb motor function and gait in patients with stroke with hemiplegia, and the results showed that the effect of interactive dynamic scalp acupuncture on lower limb motor function and walking ability was better than that of routine therapy. 94 To date, medical institutions in many Asian countries use AT as a routine rehabilitation therapy in the poststroke rehabilitation stage. Limitations This study had some limitations. First, in this study, patients included in the literature were not analyzed by stage or subgroup analysis. There are acute, subacute, and chronic recovery stages after a stroke. The rehabilitation methods adopted in each stage were different according to the limb function; thus, it may also affect the inconsistency of this study, mainly because there are few clinical studies on the application of individual emerging rehabilitation technologies to the staged treatment of poststroke motor rehabilitation. We look forward to more high-quality clinical studies for further verification. Second, most of the literature in this study did not mention the blind method, random method, and so on, which may affect the reliability of the study to some extent. Third, most of the results in this study were positive and a few were negative. The possibility of a publication bias cannot be ruled out. In addition, it is worth noting that MDAS is a movement state that changes with time and recovery, such as the soft and hard paralysis periods, and separation movement period after stroke. The characteristics of MDAS at each stage are different, so the rehabilitation focus and applicable rehabilitation technology at each stage are also different. For example, patients in the soft paralysis period should mainly induce muscle tension, while patients in the hard paralysis period should mainly relieve muscle tension. However, at present, many clinical studies include only patients with MDAS as study participants in a general way and do not explicitly classify patients with MDAS according to their movement disorder characteristics. Future research should better focus on the exercise staging of MDAS to better refine the appropriate rehabilitation technologies at various stages after MDAS. CONCLUSIONS Several rehabilitation treatments included in this network meta-analysis could improve poststroke motor function to varying degrees. Biofeedback therapy had the best comprehensive effect, whereas VR was the best intervention for improving ARAT and FMA-LE scores. Acupuncture therapy had the greatest advantage in improving lower limb balance function, and there was no significant statistical difference between most interventions. However, because of the limitations of this study, the efficacy ranking cannot fully explain the advantages and disadvantages of clinical efficacy, but only for clinical reference. However, given the large difference in the number of clinical studies among the included rehabilitation technologies, which will have a certain impact on the research results, we expect more large-scale multicenter randomized controlled trials to provide a basis for the clinical efficacy of different rehabilitation therapies in the treatment of MDAS. Supplementary Material SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL ajpmr-102-504-s001.doc (111KB, doc) Footnotes RYL and QY contributed equally to the study. RYL and X-RW prepared the manuscript. QY and LX revised the manuscript. K-YC and X-RW participated in the data collection. All the authors have read and approved the final version of the manuscript. All relevant data are within the article and its supporting information files. The study was supported by grants (Sichuan Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine [2016Z001], Department of Science and Technology of Sichuan Province [2021YFS0132], and Department of Science and Technology of Sichuan Province [2020YFS0415]) from the Foundation of Basic Research. Financial disclosure statements have been obtained, and no conflicts of interest have been reported by the authors or by any individuals in control of the content of this article. Supplemental digital content is available for this article. Direct URL citations appear in the printed text and are provided in the HTML and PDF versions of this article on the journal’s Web site ( www.ajpmr.com ). Contributor Information Ruo Yang Li, Email: alis7718@outlook.com. Ke-Yu Chen, Email: cky19940916@126.com. Xiao-Rong Wang, Email: 362937637@qq.com. Qian Yu, Email: yqswc11@163.com. Li Xu, Email: xuli_0125@163.com. REFERENCES 1. Boumrah M, Garbaya S, Radgui A: Real-time visual analytics for in-home medical rehabilitation of stroke patient—systematic review. Med Biol Eng Comput 2022;60:889–906 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Peng QC, Yin L, Cao Y: Effectiveness of virtual reality in the rehabilitation of motor function of patients with subacute stroke: a meta-analysis. Front Neurol 2021;12:639535. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Domínguez-Téllez P Moral-Muñoz JA Salazar A, et al. : Game-based virtual reality interventions to improve upper limb motor function and quality of life after stroke: systematic review and meta-analysis. Games Health J 2020;9:1–10 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Bastani A, Jaberzadeh S: Does anodal transcranial direct current stimulation enhance excitability of the motor cortex and motor function in healthy individuals and subjects with stroke: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Clin Neurophysiol 2012;123:644–57 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Zhang L Xing G Fan Y, et al. : Short- and long-term effects of repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation on upper limb motor function after stroke: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Clin Rehabil 2017;31:1137–53 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Xie YJ Chen Y Tan HX, et al. : Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation for lower extremity motor function in patients with stroke: a systematic review and network meta-analysis. Neural Regen Res 2021;16:1168–76 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Chaimani A Higgins JP Mavridis D, et al. : Graphical tools for network meta-analysis in STATA. PLoS One 2013;8:e76654. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ban YC Lin XX Geng XX, et al. : Effect of acupuncture combined with rehabilitation technology on upper limb motor dysfunction in the early stage of stroke and magnetic resonance diffusion tensor imaging. Mod Med J 2019;47:439–42 [ Goog