dance for maintaining essential health services during an outbreak. In. Geneva, Switzerland; 2020. 28. World Health Organization: WHO and UNICEF warn of a decline in vaccinations during COVID-19. In.; 2020. 29. Global Measles Outbreaks [ https://www.cdc.gov/globalhealth/measles/data/global-measles-outbreaks.html ] 30. Bogaert K, Castrucci BC, Gould E, Sellers K, Leider JP: Changes in the State Governmental Public Health Workforce: Demographics and Perceptions, 2014–2017. Journal of public health management and practice: JPHMP 2019, 25 Suppl 2, Public Health Workforce Interests and Needs Survey 2017(2 Suppl):S58-s66. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 31. Rovner J: Opinion: Always The Bridesmaid, Public Health Rarely Spotlighted Until It’s Too Late. In: NPR Shots . 2020. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials S1 Text. Survey questions. (DOCX) Click here for additional data file. (28.4KB, docx) S1 Dataset. Survey responses. (XLSX) Click here for additional data file. (91KB, xlsx) Data Availability Statement All relevant data are within the manuscript and its Supporting Information files.',1128,'2025-10-20 07:24:29','2025-10-20 07:24:29');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7476,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12506193','Effects of remote rehabilitation weekend training for stroke patients in China: a randomised controlled trial protocol.','Zhang Y, Sun PP, Zhu SY, Wu JY, Chu YT, Wu YQ, Jiang YX, Liu SY, Bai Y, Xiang YW.','2025','metre zarit chictr2500101741 metre zarit technologically personalisation brunnstrom gpower metre 10mwt yueyang pudong totalling completes logging saturday pillows cushions protocolised postdischarge predischarge biweekly metre 10mwt 10mwt metre walkway stopwatch distractions stopwatch metre metre metres deceleration metres ambulators zarit zbi zbi zbi zbi 10mwt zbi absorbing contraindicate unordered postdischarge minimises h24060 prepublication gbds teasell salbach jpc ayl bakas commiskey hausenloy teasell ijs vongsirinavarat hiengkaw ingo hoad maayken vdb enwu mml brusco jphys hsl janita combo kaad028 walkways svn langhorne stott liz michaelsen pzad054 pzad054 bosetti ayata e40416 hajar zeinab ladan zarit s12912 elson bes2022','Abstract Introduction Stroke is one of the leading causes of disability and mortality worldwide, particularly in China, where it imposes significant economic and psychological burdens on patients and their families. Traditional in-hospital rehabilitation is often limited by resource constraints, leading to interruptions in weekend therapy, which can delay recovery. Remote rehabilitation platforms offer a potential solution by providing continuous, therapist-guided rehabilitation training during weekends. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of a remote rehabilitation training platform in improving motor function, self-efficacy and social participation in stroke patients while also assessing its impact on caregiver burden. The findings will provide evidence for the integration of remote rehabilitation into stroke care protocols, offering a scalable solution to enhance recovery and reduce societal costs. Methods and analysis A total of 168 stroke patients will be recruited from three hospitals in Shanghai and randomly assigned to either an intervention group or a control group. A remote rehabilitation platform will be used to perform guided exercises on weekends for the intervention group, whereas the control group will receive standard in-hospital rehabilitation. Primary outcomes will be assessed using the 10 metre walk test, and secondary outcomes will include the Barthel Index, Fugl–Meyer motor function assessment and Zarit Caregiver Burden Scale. Ethics and dissemination This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Institutional Review Board of Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine (Ethics Approval Number: 2024-2-14-01). All participants will provide written informed consent prior to enrolment. Trial results will be disseminated through peer-reviewed publications, presentations at international stroke and rehabilitation conferences, and engagement with patient advocacy groups. Findings will also be shared with healthcare policymakers to promote the integration of remote rehabilitation into clinical practice. Individual participant data will remain confidential and be stored securely in accordance with data protection regulations. Trial registration number This trial was registered with the Chinese Clinical Trial Registry (Registration number: ChiCTR2500101741). The date of registration was 29 April 2025. Keywords: Stroke, Physical Therapy Modalities, Health STRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS OF THIS STUDY. The study employs a multicentre randomised controlled trial design, ensuring robust evidence generation and enhancing the generalisability of findings across diverse healthcare settings. The remote rehabilitation platform integrates personalised care plans, real-time therapist-patient communication and active caregiver involvement, addressing key limitations of conventional approaches. A comprehensive follow-up mechanism ensures long-term assessment of functional recovery and caregiver burden, providing insights into the sustainability of the intervention. The inclusion of standardised outcome measures (eg, 10 metre walk test, Fugl-Meyer assessment, Zarit caregiver burden scale) with high reliability and validity strengthens the methodological rigour of the study. Several limitations should be acknowledged, including geographic restrictions in participant recruitment, potential adherence challenges among technologically inexperienced patients and variability in intervention duration. However, these limitations are mitigated through specific methodological safeguards. Background Stroke is one of the leading causes of disability and mortality worldwide, particularly in China. 1 In 2020, China had approximately 17.8 million stroke patients over the age of 40, with around 3.4 million new cases annually. 2 Two-thirds of stroke patients experience motor, language, cognitive and emotional dysfunctions, contributing to caregiver burden, including risks of depression, social isolation and reduced life satisfaction. Optimal rehabilitation outcomes require patients to engage in at least 3 hour of daily rehabilitation training, 5 days a week. 3 However, owing to limited hospital resources, inpatients typically receive rehabilitation therapy only three times a week on weekdays, leading to a suspension of training on weekends. This interruption delays recovery and exacerbates the burden on patients and their families. 4 5 To address the need for continuous rehabilitation training during hospitalisation and alleviate caregiver burden, remote rehabilitation training and management platforms have been developed. 6 7 Recent clinical trials have shown that remote rehabilitation can safely and effectively enhance motor function recovery, improve self-efficacy, social participation and reduce depression in stroke patients, 8 thereby enhancing their quality of life. 9 10 Additional studies 10 , 13 indicate that sustained remote rehabilitation can activate neuroplasticity, significantly improving rehabilitation outcomes. In China, the adoption of remote rehabilitation platforms is gradually increasing, particularly in urban areas with better technological infrastructure. However, challenges such as limited personalisation, insufficient therapist–patient interaction and lack of long-term follow-up mechanisms 14 remain prevalent. To overcome these limitations, our group has developed a comprehensive remote rehabilitation platform that integrates therapist-guided exercises, real-time communication and caregiver involvement. We aim to provide continuous and personalised rehabilitation support for stroke patients. Our remote rehabilitation platform integrates therapist and patient interfaces, offering a comprehensive suite of functionalities, including patient information management, dynamic rehabilitation prescription updates and remote video guidance. It is equipped with over 600 rehabilitation training videos, ensuring that patients can maintain consistent training and progress towards their recovery goals. Our platform also enables therapists to develop individualised rehabilitation plans based on the specific needs of each patient, ensuring that rehabilitation strategies are optimised based on the patient’s unique condition, progress and goals. It facilitates seamless communication between patients, caregivers and therapists, fostering a collaborative environment where patients can receive timely feedback and address any concerns during their rehabilitation process. Importantly, caregivers are involved in the rehabilitation process, allowing them to guide and assist patients during training sessions, which enhances patient engagement and provides emotional and practical support, 15 critical for long-term rehabilitation success. 16 The study includes a robust follow-up mechanism to further ensure the effectiveness and sustainability of the rehabilitation process. Patients will undergo long-term follow-up assessments to track their progress, monitor functional recovery and identify any potential challenges that may arise over time. This study will investigate the application of a remote rehabilitation training platform in the health management of stroke patients through a multicentre randomised controlled trial (RCT). We aim to provide continuous self-rehabilitation training for stroke patients during weekends while hospitalised, explore the potential for improving motor function, self-efficacy and social participation, and assess the impact on caregiver burden. Methods Study design This study is a multicentre RCT designed to evaluate the efficacy of a remote rehabilitation training platform in the health management of stroke patients. The planned start date is estimated to be 1 April 2025, and the end date is estimated to be 30 April 2026. The flow diagram of the study protocol is shown in figure 1 . Figure 1. Flow diagram of the study. Open in a new tab Inclusion and exclusion criteria Diagnostic criteria Stroke patients will be included according to the criteria of the Chinese Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Acute Ischaemic Stroke 2023 . Inclusion criteria (1) Stroke occurrence within the past 3 months, (2) Age between 40 and 80 years, (3) Ability to communicate in Chinese, (4) Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) score >20, (5) Will sign informed consent and voluntarily participate in the study, (6) Presence of at least one caregiver (defined as a non-social worker and non-professional, non-volunteer caregiver who will protect the interests of stroke patients and undertake the main daily care work) 17 and (7) Access to internet-enabled devices at home. Exclusion criteria (1) Brunnstrom Stage I (complete paralysis), (2) Severe speech or hearing impairments preventing participation in video calls, (3) Severe mental or cognitive disorders (eg, major depression, schizophrenia, which prevents understanding and/or performing rehabilitation tasks), (4) Contraindications to rehabilitation training (eg, severe cardiopulmonary or orthopaedic conditions), (5) Pain or poor motor control interfering with movement, (6) Conditions affecting dietary intake and nutrient absorption (eg, serious digestive diseases, resulting in poor nutritional support) (7) Inability to cooperate with remote rehabilitation owing to lack of equipment or poor internet access and (8) Receiving hospice care. Sample-size calculation Sample-size calculation will be performed using GPower software (V.3.1.9.7). Based on the primary outcome measure (10 metre walk test (10MWT)), the sample size will be calculated to detect an intergroup difference of −0.06 m/s (control: 0.65 m/s, SD=0.43; intervention: 0.71 m/s, SD=0.43) in walking speed between the control and intervention groups. 18 A bilateral independent t-test will be applied, yielding an estimated sample size of 136 participants. Accounting for a 20% dropout rate, the target enrolment will be 168 participants. Intervention Participant recruitment Prior to any study procedures, all potential participants will provide written informed consent after receiving comprehensive explanations regarding study objectives, interventions, potential risks/benefits and their rights as research subjects. Hospital selection The study will be conducted across three major hospitals in Shanghai, China, namely, the Yueyang Hospital of Integrated Chinese and Western Medicine, Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, the Second Rehabilitation Hospital of Shanghai and the Shanghai Pudong New Area People’s Hospital, totalling 168 participants. These hospitals are recognised for specialised rehabilitation departments. They cover urban and suburban areas of Shanghai, ensuring a diverse patient population that reflects the broader demographic and socioeconomic characteristics of the region. This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Institutional Review Board of Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine (Ethics Approval Number: 2024-2-14-01). Randomisation An independent statistician will generate the randomisation sequence by using a computer-based random number generator. Participants will be randomly assigned in a 1:1 ratio to either the intervention group or the control group. The allocation sequence will be concealed using opaque, sequentially numbered, sealed envelopes. Each envelope will contain the group assignment (intervention or control) and will be opened only after the participant completes the baseline assessment and has been deemed eligible for the study. This method ensures that the allocation sequence remains concealed from researchers and participants until the point of assignment. Blinding In view of the nature of the intervention, blinding participants or caregivers to the group assignment will not be feasible. However, to minimise bias, the outcome assessors and data analysts will remain blinded to the group assignments throughout the study. The assessors will not be involved in the recruitment, randomisation or intervention delivery processes. They will receive training on standardised assessment protocols to ensure consistency and reliability in data collection. Participants will be instructed not to disclose their group assignment to the assessors during follow-up visits to maintain blinding. The data provided to the analysts will be coded, with group identifiers removed, to prevent any potential bias in the analysis. The coding key will be securely stored and revealed only after the completion of the primary analysis. Intervention Remote weekend-training intervention group Participants will be required to access the platform by using their personal electronic devices, logging in with personal accounts to perform their individualised exercise programmes. These programmes will be prescribed and delivered by a licensed physiotherapist via the remote rehabilitation platform. Prior to initiating remote rehabilitation training, participants and their caregivers will receive comprehensive instruction from the physiotherapist on proper exercise techniques and platform usage. When participants are able to perform self-rehabilitation tasks and navigate the platform, they will be instructed to complete assigned remote rehabilitation exercises two times per week (once on Saturday and once on Sunday) with caregiver assistance. The exercises will use basic, low-cost rehabilitation equipment readily available in clinical settings (eg., pillows, cushions and objects of varying shapes and sizes). Each participant will receive a personalised exercise prescription, tailored by the physiotherapist based on baseline injury characteristics and ongoing assessments. The prescription specifies exercise type, intensity (eg, repetition count), time and frequency (eg, sessions per week). Participants will be required to follow video-guided instructions provided by the therapist, performing each exercise three times with caregiver assistance. After each Sunday session, participants must upload a feedback video through the platform’s feedback system. The therapist will review these videos and self-reported recovery progress to adjust the intervention dynamically, implementing progressive rehabilitation measures as needed. Throughout the study, participants will engage in progressive exercises targeting gross and fine motor skills of the upper limbs, lower limbs and trunk. Communication between participants, caregivers and therapists is facilitated through the platform, which supports scheduled video consultations and messaging. The intervention is protocolised for a 4-week duration to standardise rehabilitation exposure. 18 For participants discharged prior to completing 4 weeks of inpatient care, the weekend training regimen will be maintained through identical remote delivery postdischarge, whether continuing as outpatients or in home settings. All such participants will receive standardised predischarge training on platform operation and exercise execution, continued two times per week 45 min therapist-guided sessions via the remote platform, and protocol-mandated assessments at both the 4-week and 12-week endpoints. To ensure intervention fidelity, adherence will be systematically monitored through automated platform metrics (session duration/completion rates). Critical non-adherence (completion rates <50% over 2 weeks) will trigger a multidisciplinary review. Centralised dashboards will enable weekly monitoring by study coordinators, with biweekly quality control meetings to optimise implementation. Control group Participants in the control group will undergo evaluation by a licensed physiotherapist within the hospital setting. During their hospitalisation, participants will receive standard rehabilitation therapy, which includes routine care and pharmacological management on weekends. Routine care encompasses a multidisciplinary approach involving medical, physical, psychological and occupational rehabilitation services delivered by hospital staff or affiliated support groups. These services may include physical or cognitive training, psychosocial support programmes, health-education seminars and medical counselling, as referenced in prior studies. 19 Pharmacological interventions will be prescribed and monitored by a qualified physician. Participants, assisted by their caregivers, will conduct weekly self-assessments of outcome measures by using standardised scales. The results will be reported to their attending physicians to facilitate adjustments in medication regimens as necessary. Outcomes Primary outcome measure: 10 metre walk test (10MWT) The 10MWT is a simple, reliable and valid test that directly assesses walking speed, a critical functional outcome for stroke patients. It has demonstrated good test-retest reliability (Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC) =0.83) 20 in stroke populations. The test requires a 10-metre walkway with marked start and finish lines, a stopwatch and the patient’s usual walking aids if needed. The testing environment should be quiet and free of distractions or obstacles that could affect performance. Before beginning the test, say: ‘walk at your normal, comfortable pace until you reach the far mark’ for the comfortable speed trial, instruct: ‘walk as quickly as you can safely walk until you reach the far mark’ for the fast speed trial. Each speed condition should be performed twice to ensure reliability. Start the stopwatch when any part of the patient’s leading foot crosses the 2-metre mark, and stop timing when any part of that foot crosses the 8-metre mark. This focuses on the middle six metres of the walk to eliminate acceleration and deceleration effects. The walking speed is then calculated by dividing six metres by the recorded time in seconds. For stroke patients, speeds below 0.4 m/s indicate household ambulation only, while speeds between 0.4 m/s and 0.8 m/s suggest limited community ambulation. Patients achieving speeds above 0.8 m/s are typically considered community ambulators. Secondary outcome measures Barthel index (BI) The BI is a widely used and validated measure of daily living activities (ADL) in stroke patients. 21 It has high validity 22 and excellent test-retest reliability (ICC=0.94) 23 . The BI will be administered through a structured interview or observation of the patient performing ADL tasks. Each task will be scored based on the level of assistance required, and all scores will be summed to provide a total ADL score. The BI evaluates 10 ADL items, with each scored based on the level of assistance required: (1) 0: dependent (unable to perform the task), (2) 5 or 10: requires assistance (partial dependence) and (3) 10 or 15: independent (full independence). The total score ranges from 0–100, with higher scores indicating greater independence: (1) 100: normal (fully independent), (2) 60–99: good (mostly self-sufficient), (3) 40–59: moderate dysfunction (requires assistance), (4) 20–39: severe dysfunction (significant dependence) and (5) <20: complete dependence. 24 Fugl-Meyer assessment (FMA) The FMA is a comprehensive and validated tool to assess motor function recovery in stroke patients (0.96 ≤ ICC ≤ 0.99). 25 26 It is sensitive to motor-function changes following rehabilitation interventions. The FMA will be administered by a trained therapist who will observe the patient performing specific motor tasks for the upper and lower limbs. Each task will be scored according to the patient’s ability to complete it, and all scores will be summed to provide a total motor function score. The FMA consists of 50 items (33 for upper limb and 17 for lower limb), each scored on a 3-point scale: (1) 0: unable to perform the task, (2) 1: partially performs the task and (3) 2: fully performs the task. The total score ranges from 0–100, with higher scores indicating better motor function: 27 (1) <50: severe motor impairment, (2) 50–84: moderate motor impairment and (3) ≥85: mild to no motor impairment. Zarit caregiver burden scale (ZBI) Given the critical role of caregivers in the rehabilitation process, assessing caregiver burden is indispensable for understanding the broader impact of the intervention on patients and their families. The ZBI is a widely used and validated measure of caregiver burden (ICC=0.88 28 ). The ZBI will be administered through a self-reported questionnaire completed by the caregiver. Each item will be scored based on the caregiver’s perceived burden of caring for the stroke patient, and all scores will be summed to provide a total burden score. This ZBI consists of 22 items, each of which is scored on a 5-point scale. The total score ranges from 0–88, with higher scores indicating heavier caregiver burden: (1) 0–20: little to no burden, (2) 21–40: mild to moderate burden, (3) 41–60: moderate to severe burden and (4) >60: severe burden. Assessment procedures All functional assessments (10MWT, FMA, BI, ZBI) will be conducted in a standardised testing room adjacent to the rehabilitation department, maintained at 15–30°C with sound-absorbing panels to minimise ambient noise. All therapists will undergo rigorous standardised training prior to the study to ensure consistency in evaluation. To minimise inter-rater variability and ensure measurement reliability, each participant will be consistently assessed by the same trained therapist across all evaluation timepoints. The evaluation schedule is standardised as follows: baseline (T0): within 24 hours of enrolment, postintervention (T1): 24–48 hours after completing the 4-week intervention and follow-up (T2): 12 weeks (±3 days) postintervention. The time schedule of enrolment, interventions and assessments is shown in table 1 . Table 1. Schedule of enrolment, interventions and assessments. Study period Enrolment Allocation Post-allocation Follow-up TIMEPOINT T0 * T1 † T2 ‡ ENROLMENT Eligibility screen × Informed consent × Allocation × INTERVENTION Intervention group × × Control group × × ASSESSMENT Sociodemographic data × Outcome variables × × × Open in a new tab “x” means things will be done. * within 24 hours of enrolment † 24–48 hours after completing the 4-week intervention ‡ 12 weeks (±3 days) postintervention Criteria for trial discontinuation For participants meeting any of the following criteria: (1) Occurrence of serious adverse events or complications that contraindicate continued trial participation based on medical evaluation, (2) Voluntary withdrawal requested by participants or their legal representatives, (3) Loss to follow-up or mortality events and (4) Development of concurrent medical conditions that significantly confound outcome assessments, as determined by the attending physician to warrant trial discontinuation. Such cases will be documented as protocol deviations. For participants who discontinue the intervention protocol, the complete set of outcome measures will still be obtained, along with comprehensive adverse event documentation. Statistical analysis Data collection and entry will be managed by designated personnel to ensure accuracy and consistency. SPSS (V.26.0) will be used to perform statistical analyses for all randomised participants. A two-sided significance level of p<0.05 will be applied for all statistical tests. Descriptive statistics will be reported as frequencies (proportions) for categorical variables. For unordered categorical data, the χ 2 test will be used, whereas ordered categorical data will be analysed using the Mann–Whitney U test, as appropriate. Continuous variables will first undergo normality testing using the Shapiro–Wilk test. Normally distributed data will be expressed as mean±SD and analysed using independent t-tests . Data with non-normal distributions will be summarised as median and IQR and analysed using non-parametric tests, such as the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. The incidence of adverse events between groups will be compared according to the expected frequency using χ 2 tests or Fisher’s precision tests. All adverse events observed during the study will be systematically documented, summarised and presented in tabular form, including details on severity, causality and resolution. This approach aligns with high-quality RCT reporting standards and ensures the robust statistical evaluation of the intervention’s efficacy and safety. To ensure comprehensive characterisation of intervention effects, we will conduct pre-planned subgroup analyses examining three clinically relevant dimensions: sex, age and treatment setting completion status (full 4-week inpatient intervention or early discharge transitioning to home-based training). These analyses will employ mixed-effects regression models incorporating interaction terms between subgroup variables and treatment allocation, with maintenance of the primary alpha level (p<0.05) and application of Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons. Effect estimates with 95% CIs will be reported for all subgroup comparisons to facilitate clinical interpretation. This analytical approach adheres to consolidated standards of reporting trials guidelines for subgroup analysis reporting and will provide robust evidence regarding potential effect modification across these clinically meaningful patient strata. To mitigate potential confounding from differential hospitalisation durations, pre-specified subgroup analyses will stratify outcomes by actual intervention setting (full inpatient vs partial inpatient/home completion). Hospitalisation duration will be incorporated as a covariate in mixed-effects modelling. This ensures methodological rigour while addressing clinical realities of variable hospitalisation periods. The standardised postdischarge continuation protocol maintains intervention integrity, and the stratified analysis approach permits examination of setting-specific effects on primary outcomes. Discussion Stroke remains a leading cause of disability and mortality worldwide, with China bearing a significant portion of this burden owing to its large population and ageing demographics. 29 30 Traditional in-hospital rehabilitation is essential, but it is often limited to weekdays owing to resource constraints, leading to interrupted therapy on weekends. This interruption delays recovery and exacerbates the burden on patients and their families. Our study addresses this critical gap by introducing a remote rehabilitation platform that provides continuous, therapist-guided rehabilitation training during weekends, aiming to enhance motor function, self-efficacy and social participation among stroke patients while reducing caregiver burden. Our remote rehabilitation platform integrates personalised care plans, real-time therapist–patient communication and active caregiver involvement. These features collectively address several limitations of conventional approaches. It enables therapists to tailor rehabilitation strategies to each patient’s unique needs, ensuring optimal progression and adaptability. Additionally, the inclusion of a caregiver-led training model and a robust long-term follow-up mechanism enhances patient engagement, emotional support and sustained recovery outcomes. Moreover, this study lays the foundation for future research on the scalability and long-term effectiveness of remote rehabilitation platforms. Given the rapid adoption of digital health technologies in the country, our remote rehabilitation platform can serve as a scalable solution to meet the rehabilitation needs of stroke patients in rural and medically underserved areas, where stroke incidence is high, yet access to inpatient rehabilitation is often limited. 30 , 32 The rapid adoption of digital health technologies in China presents a unique opportunity to scale up remote rehabilitation services and bridge the gap in stroke care between urban and rural populations. 33 Future research should explore the scalability and long-term effectiveness of remote rehabilitation platforms in these settings, as well as their impact on healthcare disparities. Potential adherence limitations may arise among older patients with limited digital literacy. To address this, comprehensive training is included for both patients and caregivers on platform usage. Standardised therapist training combined with blinded assessment procedures minimises inter-rater variability in outcome measurements. While geographic generalisability may be constrained by the single-region sampling frame, the multicentre design within Shanghai enhances population representativeness within the study context, which will serve to inform subsequent nationwide efficacy trials. While the caregiver-assisted model ensures safe and effective platform utilisation for most participants, it inherently excludes two vulnerable subgroups: patients who lack voluntary movement capacity with no consistent caregiver support. Future platform iterations will explore alternative support mechanisms, such as community health worker involvement or automated motion-assist technologies, to address this accessibility gap. This study represents a significant step towards addressing the gaps in traditional stroke rehabilitation by leveraging remote rehabilitation technology. By providing continuous, accessible and personalised rehabilitation training, the platform has the potential to improve patient outcomes, reduce caregiver burden and lower societal costs. The findings of this study are expected to contribute to the growing evidence on the efficacy of remote rehabilitation in stroke recovery. 9 11 The integration of technology into healthcare will be supported, ultimately enhancing the quality of life for stroke patients and their families. Ethics and dissemination This study involves human participants and was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine (Approval Number: 2024-2-14-01). All participants will provide written informed consent prior to enrolment. The dissemination of research findings is an integral component of this trial. We plan to publish the primary results in a peer-reviewed international journal, irrespective of the outcome magnitude or direction. Additionally, key findings will be presented at relevant scientific conferences to reach academic and clinical audiences. To facilitate translation of research into practice, summaries of results will be disseminated to participating hospitals and relevant health policy makers. A lay summary will be shared with study participants and made available to patient advocacy groups to ensure broader public dissemination. Acknowledgements The study has been approved by the IRB of Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine (Approval Number: 2024-2-14-01). All participants will provide written informed consent prior to enrolment. Trial results will be disseminated through peer-reviewed publications, presentations at international stroke and rehabilitation conferences, and engagement with patient advocacy groups. Findings will also be shared with healthcare policymakers to promote the integration of remote rehabilitation into clinical practice. Individual participant data will remain confidential and be stored securely in accordance with data protection regulations. Footnotes Funding: This work was supported by the Application and evaluation of a full-cycle, all-encompassing digital intelligent rehabilitation platform technology for stroke patients from Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine (E4- H24060 ). Prepublication history for this paper is available online. To view these files, please visit the journal online ( https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105002 ). Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Ethics approval: This study involves human participants and was approved by the Ethics Committee of the IRB of Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine. Ethics Approval Number: 2024-2-14-01. Participants gave informed consent to participate in the study before taking part. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Refer to the Methods section for further details. References 1. Lin B, Zhang Z, Mei Y, et al. Cumulative risk of stroke recurrence over the last 10 years: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Neurol Sci. 2021;42:61–71. doi: 10.1007/s10072-020-04797-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Collaborators GBDS Global, regional, and national burden of stroke and its risk factors, 1990-2019: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2019. Lancet Neurol. 2021;20:795–820. doi: 10.1016/S1474-4422(21)00252-0. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Teasell R, Salbach NM, Foley N, et al. Canadian Stroke Best Practice Recommendations: Rehabilitation, Recovery, and Community Participation 